(window.MAG=window.MAG||{}).d=(window.MAG.d||{});window.MAG.d["2-trent"]=[["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p1","Session 1 — 13 December 1545","Most reverend and reverend fathers, does it please you that for the praise and glory of the holy and undivided Trinity, Father and Son and Holy Spirit, for the increase and exaltation of the faith and the Christian religion, for the extirpation of heresies, for the peace and unity of the church, for the reformation of the clergy and the Christian people, for the humiliation and extinction of the enemies of the Christian name, to decree and declare that the sacred Tridentine and general council begins and has begun?","Reverendissimi ac reverendi patres, placet ne vobis ad laudem et gloriam sanctae et individuae trinitatis patris et filii et spiritus sancti, ad incrementum et exaltationem fidei et religionis christianae, ad extirpationem haeresum, ad pacem et unionem ecclesiae, ad reformationem cleri et populi christiani, ad depressionem et extinctionem hostium christiani nominis, decernere et declarare sacrum Tridentinum et generale concilium incipere et inceptum esse?"],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p2","Session 1 — 13 December 1545","And since the celebration of the nativity of our Lord Jesus Christ is near, and the other festivities of the passing and beginning year follow, does it please you that the first future session be held on Thursday after the Epiphany, which will be the seventh day of January of the year of the Lord 1546?","Et cum proxima sit celebritas nativitatis domini nostri Iesu christi et subsequantur aliae festivitates labentis et incipientis anni, placet ne vobis primam futuram sessionem habendam esse die iovis post epiphaniam, quae erit septima mensis ianuarii anno domini 1546?"],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p3","Session 2 — 7 January 1546","The sacred and holy Tridentine synod, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same three legates of the Apostolic See presiding therein, acknowledging with blessed James the apostle that every best gift and every perfect gift is from above, coming down from the Father of lights, who gives to all who ask of Him wisdom abundantly and does not reproach them; and at the same time knowing that the beginning of wisdom is the fear of the Lord: has established and decreed that all and each of the faithful of Christ gathered in the city of Trent are to be exhorted (as it exhorts them) that they be willing to amend themselves from the evils and sins hitherto committed, and henceforth to walk in the fear of the Lord and not to fulfill the desires of the flesh, to attend to prayers, to confess more often, to receive the sacrament of the Eucharist, to frequent churches, finally to fulfill the Lord's precepts as far as each one is able, and also daily to pray privately for the peace of Christian princes and for the unity of the church; but that the bishops and all others constituted in the priestly order, concelebrating the oecumenical council in that city, strive assiduously to be intent upon the praises of God, to offer up sacrifices, praises, and prayers, to perform the sacrifice of the mass at least every Lord's day (on which God created the light and rose from the dead and poured forth the Holy Spirit upon the disciples), doing as the same Holy Spirit commands through the apostle: supplications, prayers, intercessions, thanksgivings for our most holy lord the pope, for the emperor, for kings, and for others who are constituted in high position, and for all men, that we may lead a quiet and tranquil life, enjoy peace, and see the increase of faith.","Sacrosancta Tridentina synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, in ea praesidentibus eisdem tribus apostolicae sedis legatis, agnoscens cum beato Iacobo apostolo quod omne datum optimum et omne donum perfectum desursum est descendens a patre luminum, qui iis qui postulant a se sapientiam dat omnibus affluenter et non improperat eis; et simul sciens quod initium sapientiae est timor domini: statuit et decrevit omnes et singulos christifideles in civitate Tridentina congregatos exhortandos esse (prout exhortatur) ut se a malis et peccatis hactenus commissis emendare ac de cetero in timore domini ambulare et desideria carnis non perficere, orationibus instare, saepius confiteri, eucharistiae sacramentum sumere, ecclesias frequentare, praecepta denique dominica (quantum quisque poterit) adimplere, necnon quotidie pro pace principum christianorum et unitate ecclesiae privatim orare velint; episcopos vero et quoscunque alios in ordine sacerdotali constitutos, oecumenicum concilium in ea civitate concelebrantes, ut assidue in dei laudibus incumbere, hostias, laudes et preces offerre, sacrificium missae quolibet saltem die dominico (in quo deus lucem condidit et a mortuis resurrexit ac spiritum sanctum in discipulos infudit) peragere satagant, facientes sicut idem spiritus sanctus per apostolum praecipit: obsecrationes, orationes, postulationes, gratiarum actiones pro sanctissimo domino nostro papa, pro imperatore, pro regibus et ceteris qui in sublimitate constituti sunt et pro omnibus hominibus, ut quietam et tranquillam vitam agamus, pace fruamur et fidei incrementum videamus."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p4","Session 2 — 7 January 1546","Moreover, it exhorts that they fast at least on each sixth feria in memory of the Lord's passion, and give alms to the poor.","Praeterea hortatur ut ieiunent saltem singulis sextis feriis in memoriam passionis domini et eleemosynas pauperibus erogent."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p5","Session 2 — 7 January 1546","In the cathedral church, moreover, on each fifth feria let a mass of the Holy Spirit be celebrated with litanies and other prayers instituted for this purpose; in the other churches, however, on the same day let at least litanies and prayers be said.","In ecclesia autem cathedrali singulis quintis feriis celebretur missa de spiritu sancto cum laetaniis et aliis orationibus ad hoc institutis; in aliis vero ecclesiis eadem die dicantur ad minus laetaniae et orationes."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p6","Session 2 — 7 January 1546","But at the time when sacred things are being performed, let there be no conversations and confabulations, but let it be attended with mouth and mind to the celebrant.","Tempore autem quo sacra peraguntur, collocutiones et confabulationes non fiant, sed ore et animo celebranti assistatur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p7","Session 2 — 7 January 1546","And since it is fitting that bishops be irreprehensible, sober, chaste, well placed over their own house, it also exhorts that before all things each one preserve sobriety and moderation of foods at table; then, since idle speeches are often accustomed to arise in that place, that at the table of the bishops themselves the reading of the divine scriptures be mixed in.","Et quoniam oportet episcopos esse irreprehensibiles, sobrios, castos, domui suae bene praepositos, hortatur etiam ut ante omnia quilibet in mensa servet sobrietatem moderationem que ciborum; deinde cum in eo loco saepe otiosi sermones oriri soleant, ut in ipsorum episcoporum mensis divinarum scripturarum lectio admisceatur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p8","Session 2 — 7 January 1546","Moreover, let each one instruct and teach his own familiars, that they be not quarrelsome, drunken, shameless, greedy, arrogant, blasphemous, and lovers of pleasures; finally let them flee vices and embrace virtues, and in dress and adornment and all acts let them bear honesty before themselves, as befits ministers of the ministers of God.","Familiares vero suos unusquisque instruat et erudiat, ne sint rixosi, vinosi, impudici, cupidi, elati, blasphemi et voluptatum amatores; vitia demum fugiant et virtutes amplectantur et in vestitu et cultu et omnibus actibus honestatem prae se ferant, sicut decet ministros ministrorum dei."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p9","Session 2 — 7 January 1546","To these things, since the chief care, solicitude, and intention of this sacred council is that, the darkness of heresies (which for so many years have covered the earth) having been driven out, the light of catholic truth (Jesus Christ, who is the true light, granting it) may shine forth in its splendor and purity, and that those things which need reformation may be reformed, the same synod exhorts all catholics here gathered and to be gathered, and especially those who have skill in sacred letters, that by sedulous meditation they diligently consider with themselves by what chief ways and means the intention of the same synod may be directed and may obtain the desired effect, so that more maturely and considerately those things which are to be condemned may be condemned and those which are to be approved may be approved, that through the whole world all with one mouth and the same confession of faith may glorify God and the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ.","Ad haec, cum huius sacrosancti concilii praecipua cura, sollicitudo et intentio sit ut, propulsatis haeresum tenebris (quae per tot annos operuerunt terram), catholicae veritatis lux (Iesu christo qui vera lux est annuente) candor puritas que refulgeat et ea quae reformatione egent reformentur, ipsa synodus hortatur omnes catholicos hic congregatos et congregandos atque eos praesertim qui sacrarum litterarum peritiam habent, ut sedula meditatione diligenter secum ipsi cogitent quibus potissimum viis et modis ipsius synodi intentio dirigi et optatum effectum sortiri possit, quo maturius et consultius damnari damnanda et probanda probari queant, ut per totum orbem omnes uno ore et eadem fidei confessione glorificent deum et patrem domini nostri Iesu christi."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p10","Session 2 — 7 January 1546","In giving sentences, moreover, according to the statute of the Toledan council, with the lord priests sitting in the place of blessing, no one ought to make a din with immodest voices or to disturb with tumults, nor also to contend with any false or vain or obstinate disputations; but whatever is said, let it be so tempered with the most mild utterance of words that neither the hearers be offended nor the keenness of right judgment be turned aside by a disturbed mind.","In sententiis vero dicendis iuxta Toletani concilii statutum, in loco benedictionis considentibus domini sacerdotibus, nullus debeat aut immodestis vocibus perstrepere aut tumultibus perturbare, nullis etiam falsis, vanis ve aut obstinatis disceptationibus contendere; sed quidquid dicatur, sic mitissima verborum prolatione temperetur, ut nec audientes offendantur nec recti iudicii acies perturbato animo inflectatur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p11","Session 2 — 7 January 1546","Moreover, the sacred synod itself has established and decreed that if it should happen that any persons do not sit in their due place and give a sentence even under the word \"placet\" to be present at congregations and perform any other acts while the council lasts, on that account no prejudice be generated to anyone nor any new right be acquired to anyone.","Insuper ipsa sacra synodus statuit ac decrevit quod si forte contigerit aliquos debito in loco non sedere et sententiam etiam sub verbo \"placet\" proferre, congregationibus interesse et alios quoscumque actus facere concilio durante, nulli propterea praeiudicium generetur, nulli que novum ius acquiratur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p12","Session 3 — 4 February 1546","In the name of the holy and undivided Trinity, Father and Son and Holy Spirit.","In nomine sanctae et individuae trinitatis, patris et filii et spiritus sancti."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p13","Session 3 — 4 February 1546","This sacred, oecumenical, and general Tridentine synod, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same three legates of the Apostolic See presiding therein, considering the magnitude of the matters to be treated, especially those which are contained in those two chapters concerning the extirpation of heresies and the reformation of morals, for whose sake it has been chiefly gathered; acknowledging moreover with the apostle that its wrestling is not against flesh and blood, but against the spiritual powers of wickedness in the heavens: with the same apostle it exhorts all and each first of all that they be strengthened in the Lord and in the might of His power, in all things taking the shield of faith, in which they may be able to extinguish all the fiery darts of the most wicked one, and that they may receive the helmet of the hope of salvation with the sword of the Spirit, which is the word of God.","Haec sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis Tridentina synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, in ea praesidentibus eisdem tribus apostolicae sedis legatis, magnitudinem rerum tractandarum considerans, praesertim earum quae duobus illis capitibus de extirpandis haeresibus et moribus reformandis continentur, quorum causa praecipue est congregata; agnoscens autem cum apostolo non esse sibi colluctationem adversum carnem et sanguinem, sed adversus spirituales nequitias in coelestibus, cum eodem omnes et singulos in primis hortatur ut confortentur in domino et in potentia virtutis eius, in omnibus sumentes scutum fidei, in quo possint omnia tela nequissimi ignea extinguere atque galeam spei salutis accipiant cum gladio spiritus, quod est verbum dei."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p14","Session 3 — 4 February 1546","Therefore, that this pious solicitude of its may have its beginning and progress through the grace of God, before all things it has established and decreed that the confession of faith is to be prefixed, following the example of the fathers who, in the more sacred councils, were accustomed to place this shield against all heresies at the beginning of their actions, by which alone sometimes they drew the unbelieving to faith, conquered heretics, and confirmed the faithful.","Itaque, ut haec pia eius sollicitudo principium et progressum suum per dei gratiam habeat, ante omnia statuit et decernit praemittendam esse confessionem fidei, patrum exempla in hoc secuta, qui sacratioribus conciliis hoc scutum contra omnes haereses in principio suarum actionum apponere consuevere, quo solo aliquando et infideles ad fidem traxerunt, haereticos expugnaverunt et fideles confirmarunt."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p15","Session 3 — 4 February 1546","Wherefore it has judged that the symbol of faith which the holy Roman church uses, as that principle in which all who profess the faith of Christ necessarily agree, and as the firm and only foundation against which the gates of hell shall never prevail, is to be expressed in those same words in which it is read in all churches. Which indeed is of this sort:","Quare symbolum fidei, quo sancta Romana ecclesia utitur, tanquam principium illud in quo omnes qui fidem christi profitentur necessario conveniunt ac fundamentum firmum et unicum contra quod portae inferi nunquam praevalebunt, totidem verbis quibus in omnibus ecclesiis legitur, exprimendum esse censuit. Quod quidem eiusmodi est:"],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p16","Session 3 — 4 February 1546","I believe in one God, the Father almighty, maker of heaven and earth, of all things visible and invisible.","Credo in unum deum, patrem omnipotentem, factorem coeli et terrae, visibilium omnium et invisibilium."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p17","Session 3 — 4 February 1546","And in one Lord Jesus Christ, the only-begotten Son of God, born of the Father before all ages, God from God, light from light, true God from true God, begotten, not made, consubstantial with the Father, through whom all things were made.","Et in unum dominum Iesum christum, filium dei unigenitum, et ex patre natum ante omnia saecula, deum de deo, lumen de lumine, deum verum de deo vero, genitum non factum, consubstantialem patri, per quem omnia facta sunt."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p18","Session 3 — 4 February 1546","Who for us men and for our salvation descended from heaven, and was incarnate of the Holy Spirit from the Virgin Mary, and was made man.","Qui propter nos homines et propter nostram salutem descendit de coelis, et incarnatus est de spiritu sancto ex Maria virgine, et homo factus est."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p19","Session 3 — 4 February 1546","He was also crucified for us under Pontius Pilate, suffered, and was buried.","Crucifixus etiam pro nobis sub Pontio Pilato, passus et sepultus est."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p20","Session 3 — 4 February 1546","And He rose again on the third day according to the scriptures, and ascended into heaven, sits at the right hand of the Father.","Et resurrexit tertia die secundum scripturas, et ascendit in coelum, sedet ad dexteram patris."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p21","Session 3 — 4 February 1546","And He is to come again with glory to judge the living and the dead, of whose kingdom there shall be no end.","Et iterum venturus est cum gloria iudicare vivos et mortuos, cuius regni non erit finis."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p22","Session 3 — 4 February 1546","And in the Holy Spirit, the Lord and life-giver, who proceeds from the Father and the Son.","Et in spiritum sanctum, dominum et vivificantem, qui ex patre filioque procedit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p23","Session 3 — 4 February 1546","Who together with the Father and the Son is adored and glorified, who spoke through the prophets.","Qui cum patre et filio simul adoratur et conglorificatur, qui locutus est per prophetas."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p24","Session 3 — 4 February 1546","And one holy, catholic, and apostolic church.","Et unam sanctam catholicam et apostolicam ecclesiam."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p25","Session 3 — 4 February 1546","I confess one baptism for the remission of sins.","Confiteor unum baptisma in remissionem peccatorum."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p26","Session 3 — 4 February 1546","And I await the resurrection of the dead and the life of the world to come. Amen.","Et expecto resurrectionem mortuorum et vitam venturi saeculi. Amen."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p27","Session 4 — 8 April 1546","The sacred, oecumenical, and general Tridentine synod, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same three legates of the Apostolic See presiding therein, keeping this perpetually before its eyes: that, errors having been removed, the purity itself of the gospel may be preserved in the church, which gospel, promised beforehand through the prophets in the holy scriptures, our Lord Jesus Christ, the Son of God, first promulgated with His own mouth, and then commanded to be preached by His apostles to every creature as the fountain of all saving truth and of the discipline of morals; and perceiving that this truth and discipline is contained in the written books and in the unwritten traditions which, received by the apostles from the mouth of Christ Himself, or from the apostles themselves, the Holy Spirit dictating, have come down to us, as it were handed down by hand, the synod, following the example of the orthodox fathers, receives and venerates with a like affection of piety and reverence all the books of the old and new testament, since the author of both is one God, as also the traditions themselves, both those pertaining to faith and those pertaining to morals, as having been dictated either orally by Christ or by the Holy Spirit, and preserved by continuous succession in the catholic church.","Sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis Tridentina synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, praesidentibus in ea eisdem tribus apostolicae sedis legatis, hoc sibi perpetuo ante oculos proponens ut, sublatis erroribus, puritas ipsa evangelii in ecclesia conservetur, quod promissum ante per prophetas in scripturis sanctis dominus noster Iesus christus, dei filius, proprio ore primum promulgavit, deinde per suos apostolos tamquam fontem omnis et salutaris veritatis et morum disciplinae omni creaturae praedicari iussit; perspiciens que hanc veritatem et disciplinam contineri in libris scriptis et sine scripto traditionibus, quae ab ipsius christi ore ab apostolis acceptae aut ab ipsis apostolis spiritu sancto dictante quasi per manus traditae ad nos usque pervenerunt, orthodoxorum patrum exempla secuta, omnes libros tam veteris quam novi testamenti, cum utriusque unus deus sit auctor, nec non traditiones ipsas, tum ad fidem tum ad mores pertinentes, tamquam vel oretenus a christo vel a spiritu sancto dictatas et continua successione in ecclesia catholica conservatas, pari pietatis affectu ac reverentia suscipit et veneratur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p28","Session 4 — 8 April 1546","It has judged that the list of the sacred books is to be appended to this decree, lest any doubt arise as to which are the books that are received by the same synod. They are the following:","Sacrorum vero librorum indicem huic decreto adscribendum censuit, ne cui dubitatio suboriri possit quinam sint qui ab ipsa synodo suscipiuntur. Sunt veri infrascripti."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p29","Session 4 — 8 April 1546","Of the Old Testament: The five books of Moses, that is, Genesis, Exodus, Leviticus, Numbers, Deuteronomy; Joshua, Judges, Ruth, four books of Kings, two of Paralipomenon, the first and second of Esdras (which is called Nehemiah), Tobit, Judith, Esther, Job, the Davidic Psalter of one hundred and fifty psalms, Proverbs, Ecclesiastes, the Song of Songs, Wisdom, Ecclesiasticus, Isaiah, Jeremiah with Baruch, Ezekiel, Daniel, the twelve minor prophets, that is, Hosea, Joel, Amos, Obadiah, Jonah, Micah, Nahum, Habakkuk, Zephaniah, Haggai, Zechariah, Malachi; two books of Maccabees, the first and second.","Testamenti veteris: quinque Moysis, id est Genesis, Exodus, Leviticus, Numeri, Deuteronomium; Iosue, Iudicum, Ruth, quatuor Regum, duo Paralipomenon, Esdrae primus et secundus qui dicitur Nehemias, Tobias, Iudith, Esther, Iob, psalterium Davidicum centum quinquaginta psalmorum, Parabolae, Ecclesiastes, Canticum canticorum, Sapientia, Ecclesiasticus, Isaias, Ieremias cum Baruch, Ezechiel, Daniel, duodecim prophetae minores, id est Osea, Ioel, Amos, Abdias, Ionas, Michaeas, Nahum, Habacuc, Sophonias, Aggaeus, Zacharias, Malachias; duo Machabaeorum, primus et secundus."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p30","Session 4 — 8 April 1546","Of the New Testament: The four gospels according to Matthew, Mark, Luke, John; the Acts of the Apostles, written by Luke the evangelist; fourteen epistles of Paul the apostle: to the Romans, two to the Corinthians, to the Galatians, to the Ephesians, to the Philippians, to the Colossians, two to the Thessalonians, two to Timothy, to Titus, to Philemon, to the Hebrews; two epistles of Peter the apostle, three of John the apostle, one of James the apostle, one of Jude the apostle; and the Apocalypse of John the apostle.","Testamenti novi: quatuor evangelia secundum Matthaeum, Marcum, Lucam, Ioannem; actus apostolorum a Luca evangelista conscripti; quatuordecim epistolae Pauli apostoli: ad Romanos, duae ad Corinthios, ad Galatas, ad Ephesios, ad Philippenses, ad Colossenses, duae ad Thessalonicenses, duae ad Timotheum, ad Titum, ad Philemonem, ad Hebraeos; Petri apostoli duae, Ioannis apostoli tres, Iacobi apostoli una, Iudae apostoli una; et apocalypsis Ioannis apostoli."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p31","Session 4 — 8 April 1546","If anyone, however, does not receive the same books in their entirety, with all their parts, as they have been accustomed to be read in the catholic church and as they are contained in the old Vulgate Latin edition, as sacred and canonical, and knowingly and prudently despises the aforesaid traditions, let him be anathema.","Si quis autem libros ipsos integros cum omnibus suis partibus, prout in ecclesia catholica legi consueverunt et in veteri vulgata latina editione habentur, pro sacris et canonicis non susceperit, et traditiones praedictas sciens et prudens contempserit: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p32","Session 4 — 8 April 1546","Let all therefore understand in what order and way the same synod, after laying the foundation of the confession of faith, is about to proceed, and by what chief testimonies and aids it will be used in confirming dogmas and restoring morals in the church.","Omnes itaque intelligant quo ordine et via ipsa synodus, post iactum fidei confessionis fundamentum, sit progressura et quibus potissimum testimoniis ac praesidiis in confirmandis dogmatibus et instaurandis in ecclesia moribus sit usura."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p33","Decree Concerning the Vulgate Edition and the Manner of Interpreting Sacred Scri","Moreover, the same sacred synod, considering that no small benefit can accrue to the church of God if it be made known which of all the Latin editions of the sacred books that are in circulation is to be held as authentic, establishes and declares that this same old and Vulgate edition, which has been approved by the long use of so many centuries in the church itself, is to be held as authentic in public readings, disputations, preachings, and expositions, and that no one dare or presume to reject it under any pretext.","Insuper eadem sacrosancta synodus, considerans non parum utilitatis accedere posse ecclesiae dei si ex omnibus latinis editionibus quae circumferuntur sacrorum librorum quaenam pro authentica habenda sit innotescat, statuit et declarat, ut haec ipsa vetus et vulgata editio, quae longo tot saeculorum usu in ipsa ecclesia probata est, in publicis lectionibus, disputationibus, praedicationibus et expositionibus pro authentica habeatur, et quod nemo illam reiicere quovis praetextu audeat vel praesumat."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p34","Decree Concerning the Vulgate Edition and the Manner of Interpreting Sacred Scri","Furthermore, to restrain petulant minds, it decrees that no one, relying on his own prudence, in matters of faith and of morals pertaining to the edification of Christian doctrine, wresting sacred scripture to his own senses, dare to interpret the same sacred scripture contrary to that sense which holy mother church has held and holds, to whom it belongs to judge concerning the true sense and interpretation of the holy scriptures, or even contrary to the unanimous consent of the fathers, even if such interpretations should never be published.","Praeterea ad coercenda petulantia ingenia decernit, ut nemo suae prudentiae innixus in rebus fidei et morum ad aedificationem doctrinae christianae pertinentium, sacram scripturam ad suos sensus contorquens, contra eum sensum quem tenuit et tenet sancta mater ecclesia, cuius est iudicare de vero sensu et interpretatione scripturarum sanctarum, aut etiam contra unanimem consensum patrum ipsam scripturam sacram interpretari audeat, etiamsi huiusmodi interpretationes nullo unquam tempore in lucem edendae forent."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p35","Decree Concerning the Vulgate Edition and the Manner of Interpreting Sacred Scri","Those who have acted contrary shall be declared by the ordinaries and punished with the penalties established by law.","Qui contravenerint, per ordinarios declarentur et poenis a iure statutis puniantur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p36","Decree Concerning the Printing and Use of Sacred Books","But also wishing to impose a measure on printers in this regard, as is fitting, who, already without measure (that is, thinking it is lawful for them to print whatever they wish) without the license of ecclesiastical superiors, print the books of sacred scripture themselves, and the annotations and expositions of any authors upon them, indifferently, often with the press silent, often even with it falsely stated, and, what is worse, without the name of the author; and also elsewhere imprudently have such printed books for sale, it decrees and establishes that henceforth sacred scripture, especially this same old Vulgate edition, be printed as correctly as possible, and that it be lawful for no one to print or cause to be printed any books concerning sacred matters without the name of the author, nor in the future to sell such or even to keep them, unless they have first been examined and approved by the ordinary, under the penalty of anathema and of the money penalty set in the canon of the last Lateran council.","Sed et impressoribus modum in hac parte, ut par est, imponere volens, qui iam sine modo (hoc est putantes sibi licere quidquid libet) sine licentia superiorum ecclesiasticorum ipsos sacrae scripturae libros et super illos adnotationes et expositiones quorumlibet indifferenter, saepe tacito, saepe etiam ementito prelo, et (quod gravius est) sine nomine auctoris imprimunt, alibi etiam impressos libros huiusmodi temere venales habent, decernit et statuit ut posthac sacra scriptura, potissimum vero haec ipsa vetus et vulgata editio, quam emendatissime imprimatur, nulli que liceat imprimere vel imprimi facere quosvis libros de rebus sacris sine nomine auctoris, neque illos in futurum vendere aut etiam apud se retinere, nisi primum examinati probati que fuerint ab ordinario, sub poena anathematis et pecuniae in canone concilii novissimi lateranensis apposita."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p37","Decree Concerning the Printing and Use of Sacred Books","And if they are regulars, they are held to obtain license also from their own superiors beyond examination and approval of this kind, the books having been examined by them according to the form of their ordinances.","Et si regulares fuerint, ultra examinationem et probationem huiusmodi licentiam quoque a suis superioribus impetrare teneantur, recognitis per eos libris iuxta formam suarum ordinationum."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p38","Decree Concerning the Printing and Use of Sacred Books","Those who communicate or publish them in writing, unless they have been examined and approved beforehand, shall be subject to the same penalties as printers.","Qui autem scripto eos communicant vel evulgant, nisi antea examinati probati que fuerint, eisdem poenis subiaceant quibus impressores."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p39","Decree Concerning the Printing and Use of Sacred Books","And those who have them or read them, unless they have revealed the author, shall be held as the authors.","Et qui eos habuerint vel legerint, nisi prodiderint auctorem, pro auctoribus habeantur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p40","Decree Concerning the Printing and Use of Sacred Books","The approval itself of such books is to be given in writing, and therefore it is to appear authentically at the front of the book, whether written or printed.","Ipsa vero huiusmodi librorum probatio in scriptis detur, atque ideo in fronte libri vel scripti vel impressi authentice appareat."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p41","Decree Concerning the Printing and Use of Sacred Books","And all this, that is, the approval and examination, is to be done freely, so that those things which are to be approved may be approved and those which are to be disapproved may be disapproved.","Id que totum, hoc est probatio et examen, gratis fiat, ut probanda probentur et reprobentur improbanda."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p42","Decree Concerning the Printing and Use of Sacred Books","After these things, wishing to repress that rashness by which the words and sentences of sacred scripture are turned and twisted to profane things of all kinds, namely to scurrilous, fabulous, vain, flattery, detractions, impious and diabolical incantations, divinations, lots, and also libels, it commands and enjoins, for removing such irreverence and contempt and lest anyone henceforth in any way dare to usurp the words of sacred scripture for these and similar things, that all such persons, as rash ones and violators of the word of God, be coerced by bishops with penalties of law and of judgment.","Post haec, temeritatem illam reprimere volens qua ad profana quaeque convertuntur et torquentur verba et sententiae sacrae scripturae, ad scurrilia scilicet, fabulosa, vana, adulationes, detractiones, superstitiones impias et diabolicas incantationes, divinationes, sortes, libellos etiam famosos, mandat et praecipit ad tollendam huiusmodi irreverentiam et contemptum, et ne de cetero quisquam quomodolibet verba scripturae sacrae ad haec et similia audeat usurpare, ut omnes huius generis homines, ut temeratores et violatores verbi dei, iuris et arbitrii poenis per episcopos coerceantur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p43","Decree Concerning the Printing and Use of Sacred Books","Likewise, this sacred synod establishes and decrees that the next future session is to be held and celebrated on Thursday after the most sacred next feast of Pentecost.","Item haec sacrosancta synodus statuit et decernit proximam futuram sessionem tenendam et celebrandam esse feria quinta post sacratissimum festum proximum pentecostes."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p44","Session 5 — 17 June 1546","That our catholic faith, without which it is impossible to please God, may, errors having been purged, remain in its own sincerity, whole and undefiled, and that the Christian people may not be carried about by every wind of doctrine, since that ancient serpent, the perpetual enemy of the human race, among the many evils with which the church of God is disturbed in these our times, has also stirred up, concerning original sin and its remedy, not only new but also old dissensions: the sacred, oecumenical, and general Council of Trent, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same three legates of the Apostolic See presiding therein, wishing now to come to the recalling of those who err and the confirming of those who waver, following the testimonies of the sacred scriptures, of the holy fathers, and of the most approved councils, as well as the judgment and consent of the church itself, establishes, confesses, and declares these things concerning the same original sin.","Ut fides nostra catholica, sine qua impossibile est placere deo, purgatis erroribus in sua sinceritate integra et illibata permaneat, et ne populus christianus omni vento doctrinae circumferatur, cum serpens ille antiquus, humani generis perpetuus hostis, inter plurima mala quibus ecclesia dei his nostris temporibus perturbatur, etiam de peccato originali eius que remedio non solum nova sed etiam vetera dissidia excitaverit: sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis Tridentina synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, praesidentibus in ea eisdem tribus apostolicae sedis legatis, iam ad revocandos errantes et nutantes confirmandos accedere volens, sacrarum scripturarum et sanctorum patrum ac probatissimorum conciliorum testimonia et ipsius ecclesiae iudicium et consensum secuta, haec de ipso peccato originali statuit, fatetur ac declarat."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p45","Chapter I","If anyone does not confess that the first man, Adam, when he had transgressed the commandment of God in paradise, immediately lost the sanctity and justice in which he had been constituted, and that by the offense of this prevarication he incurred the wrath and indignation of God, and therefore also the death which God had previously threatened to him, and with death captivity under his power who thereafter had the empire of death, that is, the devil, and that the whole Adam through that offense of prevarication was changed in body and soul for the worse: let him be anathema.","Si quis non confitetur primum hominem Adam, cum mandatum dei in paradiso fuisset transgressus, statim sanctitatem et iustitiam in qua constitutus fuerat amisisse, incurrisse que per offensam praevaricationis huiusmodi iram et indignationem dei, atque ideo mortem, quam antea illi comminatus fuerat deus, et cum morte captivitatem sub eius potestate qui mortis deinde habuit imperium, hoc est diaboli, totum que Adam per illam praevaricationis offensam secundum corpus et animam in deterius commutatum fuisse: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p46","Chapter I","If anyone asserts that the prevarication of Adam harmed himself alone and not his progeny, and that the sanctity and justice received from God, which he lost, he lost for himself alone and not also for us; or that he, having been stained by the sin of disobedience, has transfused death and punishments of the body only into the whole human race, but not also sin, which is the death of the soul: let him be anathema, since he contradicts the apostle saying: \"By one man sin entered into the world, and by sin death, and so death passed upon all men, in whom all have sinned.\"","Si quis Adae praevaricationem sibi soli et non eius propagini asserit nocuisse, acceptam a deo sanctitatem et iustitiam, quam perdidit, sibi soli et non nobis etiam eum perdidisse; aut inquinatum illum per inobedientiae peccatum mortem et poenas corporis tantum in omne genus humanum transfudisse, non autem et peccatum, quod mors est animae: anathema sit, cum contradicat apostolo dicenti: \"per unum hominem peccatum intravit in mundum et per peccatum mors, et ita in omnes homines mors pertransiit, in quo omnes peccaverunt.\""],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p47","Chapter I","If anyone asserts that this sin of Adam, which is one in its origin and transfused into all by propagation, not by imitation, is inherent in each as his own, is taken away either by the powers of human nature or by any other remedy than through the merit of the one mediator, our Lord Jesus Christ, who reconciled us to God in His own blood, having been made for us justice, sanctification, and redemption; or denies that the same merit of Jesus Christ is applied both to adults and to infants through the sacrament of baptism rightly conferred in the form of the church: let him be anathema. For there is no other name under heaven given to men, in which we must be saved.","Si quis hoc Adae peccatum, quod origine unum est et propagatione, non imitatione, transfusum omnibus inest unicuique proprium, vel per humanae naturae vires vel per aliud remedium asserit tolli, quam per meritum unius mediatoris, domini nostri Iesu christi, qui nos deo reconciliavit in sanguine suo, factus nobis iustitia, sanctificatio et redemptio; aut negat ipsum christi Iesu meritum per baptismi sacramentum in forma ecclesiae rite collatum tam adultis quam parvulis applicari: anathema sit. Quia non est aliud nomen sub coelo datum hominibus, in quo oporteat nos salvos fieri."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p48","Chapter I","If anyone denies that infants, newly born from their mothers' wombs, are to be baptized, even if they have been born from baptized parents, or says that they are baptized for the remission of sins, but that they derive nothing of original sin from Adam, which must be expiated by the laver of regeneration for them to attain eternal life, whence it follows that in them the form of baptism \"for the remission of sins\" is understood not as true but as false: let him be anathema. For that which the apostle says, \"By one man sin entered into the world, and by sin death, and so death passed upon all men, in whom all have sinned,\" is not to be understood otherwise than as the catholic church everywhere diffused has always understood it. For on account of this rule of faith from the tradition of the apostles, even infants, who have not yet been able to commit any sins in themselves, are truly baptized for the remission of sins, so that what they contracted by generation may be cleansed in them by regeneration. \"For unless a person has been born again of water and the Holy Spirit, he cannot enter the kingdom of God.\"","Si quis parvulos recentes ab uteris matrum baptizandos negat, etiam si fuerint a baptizatis parentibus orti, aut dicit in remissionem quidem peccatorum eos baptizari, sed nihil ex Adam trahere originalis peccati, quod regenerationis lavacro necesse sit expiari ad vitam aeternam consequendam, unde fit consequens ut in eis forma baptismatis \"in remissionem peccatorum\" non vera, sed falsa intelligatur: anathema sit. Quoniam non aliter intelligendum est id quod dicit apostolus: \"per unum hominem peccatum intravit in mundum et per peccatum mors, et ita in omnes homines mors pertransiit, in quo omnes peccaverunt,\" nisi quemadmodum ecclesia catholica ubique diffusa semper intellexit. Propter hanc enim regulam fidei ex traditione apostolorum, etiam parvuli, qui nihil peccatorum in semetipsis adhuc committere potuerunt, ideo in remissionem peccatorum veraciter baptizantur, ut in eis regeneratione mundetur quod generatione contraxerunt. \"Nisi enim quis renatus fuerit ex aqua et spiritu sancto, non potest introire in regnum dei.\""],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p49","Chapter I","If anyone denies that by the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ, which is conferred in baptism, the guilt of original sin is remitted, or even asserts that the whole of that which has the true and proper nature of sin is not taken away, but says that it is only brushed off or not imputed: let him be anathema. For in the regenerate God hates nothing, because there is no condemnation to those who have been truly buried together with Christ by baptism into death, who walk not according to the flesh, but, putting off the old man and putting on the new one who has been created according to God, are made innocent, immaculate, pure, without offense, and beloved of God, heirs indeed of God, co-heirs however of Christ, so that nothing at all delays them from entering heaven. But the holy synod confesses and perceives that concupiscence or the tinder remains in the baptized; which, since it is left for a struggle, is not able to harm those not consenting and manfully resisting by the grace of Jesus Christ. Indeed, he who shall have lawfully struggled will be crowned. This concupiscence, which the apostle sometimes calls sin, the holy synod declares that the catholic church has never understood to be called sin in the sense that it is truly and properly sin in the regenerate, but because it is from sin and inclines to sin.","Si quis per Iesu christi domini nostri gratiam, quae in baptismate confertur, reatum originalis peccati remitti negat, aut etiam asserit non tolli totum id quod veram et propriam peccati rationem habet, sed illud dicit tantum radi aut non imputari: anathema sit. In renatis enim nihil odit deus, quia nihil est damnationis iis qui vere consepulti sunt cum christo per baptisma in mortem, qui non secundum carnem ambulant, sed veterem hominem exuentes et novum, qui secundum deum creatus est, induentes, innocentes, immaculati, puri, innoxii ac deo dilecti filii effecti sunt, heredes quidem dei, coheredes autem christi, ita ut nihil prorsus eos ab ingressu coeli remoretur. Manere autem in baptizatis concupiscentiam vel fomitem, haec sancta synodus fatetur et sentit; quae cum ad agonem relicta sit, nocere non consentientibus et viriliter per christi Iesu gratiam repugnantibus non valet. Quinimmo qui legitime certaverit, coronabitur. Hanc concupiscentiam, quam aliquando apostolus peccatum appellat, sancta synodus declarat ecclesiam catholicam nunquam intellexisse peccatum appellari quod vere et proprie in renatis peccatum sit, sed quia ex peccato est et ad peccatum inclinat."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p50","Chapter I","If anyone, however, thinks the contrary: let him be anathema. This same holy synod nevertheless declares that it is not its intention to include in this decree, where the matter of original sin is treated, the blessed and immaculate Virgin Mary, the mother of God, but that the constitutions of Pope Sixtus IV of happy memory are to be observed, under the penalties contained in those constitutions, which it renews.","Si quis autem contrarium senserit: anathema sit. Declarat tamen haec ipsa sancta synodus non esse suae intentionis comprehendere in hoc decreto, ubi de peccato originali agitur, beatam et immaculatam virginem Mariam, dei genitricem, sed observandas esse constitutiones felicis recordationis Sixti papae IV sub poenis in eis constitutionibus contentis, quas innovat."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p51","Decree Concerning the Reformation","The same sacred synod, the same legates of the Apostolic See presiding, wishing to pursue the matter of reformation, has judged that a beginning is to be made from those who preside over the greater churches. For the integrity of those presiding is the salvation of subjects.","Eadem sacrosancta synodus, eisdem praesidentibus et apostolicae sedis legatis, ad restituendam collapsam admodum ecclesiasticam disciplinam depravatos que in clero et populo christiano mores emendandos se accingere volens, ab iis qui maioribus ecclesiis praesunt initium censuit esse sumendum. Integritas enim praesidentium salus est subditorum."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p52","Decree Concerning the Reformation","Trusting therefore that through the mercy of our Lord and God and the provident diligence of His vicar on earth, it will altogether come to pass that, according to the venerable sanctions of the blessed fathers, those who are most worthy shall be assumed to the governance of churches (a burden indeed to be feared by angelic shoulders), whose prior life and all age from childish beginnings even to more perfect years, having been laudably spent through the disciplines of ecclesiastical training, may provide testimony: it warns all who are placed over patriarchal, primatial, metropolitan, and cathedral churches, under whatsoever name and title, and wishes them to be warned, that, attending to themselves and to the whole flock in which the Holy Spirit has placed them to rule the church of God which He has purchased with His blood, they watch, as the apostle commands, in all things, labor, and fulfill their ministry.","Confidens itaque per domini ac dei nostri misericordiam providam que ipsius dei in terris vicarii sollertiam omnino futurum ut ad ecclesiarum regimen (onus quippe angelicis humeris formidandum) qui maxime digni fuerint, quorum que prior vita ac omnis aetas a puerilibus exordiis usque ad perfectiores annos per disciplinae stipendia ecclesiasticae laudabiliter acta testimonium praebeat, secundum venerabiles beatorum patrum sanctiones assumantur, omnes patriarchalibus, primatialibus, metropolitanis et cathedralibus ecclesiis quibuscunque quovis nomine ac titulo praefectos monet ac monitos esse vult, ut attendentes sibi et universo gregi, in quo spiritus sanctus posuit eos regere ecclesiam dei, quam acquisivit sanguine suo, vigilent, sicut apostolus praecipit, in omnibus laborent et ministerium suum impleant."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p53","Decree Concerning the Reformation","But they know that they cannot fulfill it if, after the manner of hirelings, they desert the flocks entrusted to them and do not attend to the keeping of their sheep, whose blood will be required from their hands by the supreme Judge, since it is most certain that the excuse of the shepherd is not admitted if the wolf eats the sheep and the shepherd knows it not.","Implere autem illud se nequaquam posse sciant, si greges sibi commissos mercenariorum more deserant atque ovium suarum, quarum sanguis de eorum est manibus a supremo iudice requirendus, custodiae minime incumbant, cum certissimum sit non admitti pastoris excusationem, si lupus oves comedit et pastor nescit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p54","Decree Concerning the Reformation","And nevertheless, because some (which is greatly to be deplored) are found at this time who, unmindful even of their own salvation and preferring earthly things to heavenly and human to divine, wander about in various courts or keep themselves occupied in the solicitude of temporal affairs, the sheepfold having been abandoned and the care of the sheep entrusted to them having been neglected, it has pleased the sacred synod to renew the ancient canons (which have almost fallen into disuse by the injury of times and men) promulgated against non-residents, just as by the force of the present decree it renews them, and further for the stronger security of their residence and for reforming morals in the church, to establish and decree in the following manner.","Ac nihilominus, quia nonnulli (quod vehementer dolendum est) hoc tempore reperiuntur qui propriae etiam salutis immemores, terrena que coelestibus ac divinis humana praeferentes, in diversis curiis vagantur aut in negotiorum temporalium sollicitudine (ovili derelicto atque ovium sibi commissarum cura neglecta) se detinent occupatos, placuit sacrosanctae synodo antiquos canones (qui temporum atque hominum iniuria paene in dissuetudinem abierunt) adversus non residentes promulgatos innovare, quemadmodum virtute praesentis decreti innovat, ac ulterius pro firmiori eorundem residentia et reformandis in ecclesia moribus in hunc qui sequitur modum statuere atque sancire."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p55","Decree Concerning the Reformation","If anyone, from a patriarchal, primatial, metropolitan, or cathedral church committed to him under any title, cause, name, or right, with whatever dignity, grade, and preeminence he may shine, lacking legitimate impediment or just and reasonable causes, shall have been absent by remaining outside his diocese for six continuous months, let him by the law itself incur the penalty of a fourth part of the fruits of one year, to be applied to the fabric of the church and to the poor of the place by the ecclesiastical superior.","Si quis a patriarchali, primatiali, metropolitana seu cathedrali ecclesia sibi quocumque titulo, causa, nomine seu iure commissa, quacumque ille dignitate, gradu et praeeminentia praefulgeat, legitimo impedimento seu iustis et rationabilibus causis cessantibus, sex mensibus continuis extra suam dioecesim morando abfuerit, quartae partis fructuum unius anni fabricae ecclesiae et pauperibus loci per superiorem ecclesiasticum applicandorum poenam ipso iure incurrat."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p56","Decree Concerning the Reformation","But if he shall have persisted in such absence for another six months, let him lose another fourth part of the fruits, likewise to be applied.","Quod si per alios sex menses in huiusmodi absentia perseveraverit, aliam quartam partem fructuum similiter applicandam eo ipso amittat."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p57","Decree Concerning the Reformation","Contumacy increasing, that he may be subjected to the severer censure of the sacred canons, the metropolitan shall be bound to denounce to the Roman pontiff absent suffragan bishops, and the senior resident suffragan bishop an absent metropolitan, under the penalty of interdict of entrance into the church to be incurred by that very act, within three months by letter or nuncio; who, by the authority of his supreme see, may animadvert against the absent persons as the greater or lesser contumacy of each shall have demanded, and may provide the churches themselves with more useful pastors, as he shall know to be salutarily expedient in the Lord.","Crescente vero contumacia, ut severiori sacrorum canonum censurae subiiciatur, metropolitanus suffraganeos episcopos absentes, metropolitanum vero absentem suffraganeus episcopus antiquior residens sub poena interdicti ingressus ecclesiae eo ipso incurrenda infra tres menses per litteras seu nuntium Romano pontifici denuntiare teneatur, qui in ipsos absentes, prout cuiusque maior aut minor contumacia exegerit, suae supremae sedis auctoritate animadvertere et ecclesiis ipsis de pastoribus utilioribus providere poterit, sicut in domino noverit salubriter expedire."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p58","Decree Concerning the Reformation","Inferior bishops, obtaining in title or commendam any ecclesiastical benefices requiring personal residence by law or custom, shall be compelled by their ordinaries, by the opportune remedies of law, to reside, just as it will seem expedient to them for the good governance of the churches and the increase of divine worship, the quality of places and persons having been considered; and no privileges or perpetual indults of non-residence or of receiving fruits in absence shall avail them.","Episcopis inferiores, quaevis beneficia ecclesiastica personalem residentiam de iure sive consuetudine exigentia in titulum sive commendam obtinentes, ab eorum ordinariis (quemadmodum eis pro bono ecclesiarum regimine et divini cultus augmento, locorum et personarum qualitate pensata, expediens videbitur) opportunis iuris remediis residere cogantur, nulli que privilegia seu indulta perpetua de non residendo aut de fructibus in absentia percipiendis suffragentur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p59","Decree Concerning the Reformation","Indulgences and dispensations, however, granted for temporal reasons only from true and reasonable causes and to be legitimately proven before the ordinary, shall remain in their force; in which cases, nevertheless, it is the office of bishops (as in this matter delegated by the Apostolic See) to provide, by the deputation of suitable vicars and the assignment of a congruent portion of fruits, that the care of souls be by no means neglected, no privilege or exemption availing anyone in this regard.","Indulgentiis vero et dispensationibus temporalibus ex veris et rationabilibus causis tantum concessis et coram ordinario legitime probandis in suo robore permansuris; quibus casibus nihilominus officium sit episcoporum (tamquam in hac parte a sede apostolica delegatorum) providere, ut per deputationem idoneorum vicariorum et congruae portionis fructuum assignationem, cura animarum nullatenus negligatur, nemini quoad hoc privilegio seu exemptione quacumque suffragante."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p60","Decree Concerning the Reformation","The prelates of churches shall prudently and diligently attend to correcting the excesses of subjects, and no secular cleric of any personal or regular order, living outside a monastery, even by pretext of a privilege of his order, shall be deemed safe so that, if he has offended, he cannot be visited, punished, and corrected by the ordinary of the place (as in this matter delegated by the Apostolic See) according to the canonical sanctions.","Ecclesiarum praelati ad corrigendum subditorum excessus prudenter et diligenter intendant, et nemo saecularis clericus cuiusvis personalis vel regularis extra monasterium degens, etiam sui ordinis privilegii praetextu, tutus censeatur quominus, si deliquerit, ab ordinario loci (tamquam super hoc a sede apostolica delegato) secundum canonicas sanctiones visitari, puniri et corrigi valeat."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p61","Decree Concerning the Reformation","The chapters of cathedral and other greater churches, and their persons, shall be able to protect themselves by no exemptions, customs, sentences, oaths, agreements (which bind only their authors, not also their successors), so that they cannot be visited, corrected, and amended, even by apostolic authority, by their bishops and other greater prelates, by themselves alone or with those whom they shall see fit to join, according to the canonical sanctions, as often as there shall be need.","Capitula cathedralium et aliarum maiorum ecclesiarum, illorum que personae, nullis exemptionibus, consuetudinibus, sententiis, iuramentis, concordiis (quae tantum suos obligent auctores, non etiam successores) tueri se possint, quominus a suis episcopis et aliis maioribus praelatis, per se ipsos solos vel illis quibus sibi videbitur adiunctis, iuxta canonicas sanctiones, toties quoties opus fuerit, visitari, corrigi et emendari, etiam auctoritate apostolica, possint et valeant."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p62","Decree Concerning the Reformation","It shall not be lawful for any bishop, under pretext of any privilege, to exercise pontificals in another's diocese without the express license of the ordinary of the place and only upon persons subject to the same ordinary; if it shall have been done otherwise, let the bishop be suspended from the exercise of pontificals, and those thus ordained from the execution of orders, by the law itself.","Nulli episcopo liceat, cuiusvis privilegii praetextu, pontificalia in alterius diocesi exercere, nisi de ordinarii loci expressa licentia et in personas eidem ordinario subiectas tantum; si secus factum fuerit, episcopus ab exercitio pontificalium et sic ordinati ab executione ordinum sint ipso iure suspensi."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p63","Session 6 — 13 January 1547","Most reverend and reverend fathers, does it please you that the next future session be celebrated on Thursday after the first Sunday of the following Lent, which will be the third day of the month of March?","Reverendissimi ac reverendi patres, placet ne vobis proximam futuram sessionem celebrari die iovis feria quinta post primam dominicam subsequentis quadragesimae, quae erit dies tertia mensis martii?"],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p64","Session 6 — 13 January 1547","Since at this time, not without the loss of many souls and grave detriment to ecclesiastical unity, a certain erroneous doctrine concerning justification has been disseminated, for the praise and glory of almighty God, the tranquility of the church, and the salvation of souls, the sacred, oecumenical, and general Council of Trent, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same three legates of the Apostolic See presiding therein in the name of our most holy father in Christ and lord, Paul III, by divine providence pope, intends to set forth to all the faithful of Christ the true and sound doctrine of justification itself, which the sun of justice, Christ Jesus, the author and finisher of our faith, taught, the apostles transmitted, and the catholic church, the Holy Spirit suggesting, has perpetually retained; strictly inhibiting that henceforth anyone dare to believe, preach, or teach otherwise than is established and declared in this present decree.","Cum hoc tempore, non sine multarum animarum iactura et gravi ecclesiasticae unitatis detrimento, erronea quaedam disseminata sit de iustificatione doctrina, ad laudem et gloriam omnipotentis dei, ecclesiae tranquillitatem et animarum salutem, sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis Tridentina synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, praesidentibus in ea nomine sanctissimi in christo patris et domini nostri Pauli, divina providentia papae tertii, reverendissimis dominis Ioanne Maria episcopo Praenestino de Monte et Marcello tituli sanctae crucis in Hierusalem presbytero sacrae Romanae ecclesiae cardinalibus et apostolicis de latere legatis, exponere intendit omnibus christifidelibus veram sanam que doctrinam ipsius iustificationis, quam sol iustitiae christus Iesus, fidei nostrae auctor et consummator, docuit, apostoli tradiderunt, et catholica ecclesia, spiritu sancto suggerente, perpetuo retinuit; districtius inhibendo, ne deinceps audeat quisquam aliter credere, praedicare aut docere, quam praesenti decreto statuitur ac declaratur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p65","Chapter I","First, the holy synod declares that for the doctrine of justification to be properly and sincerely understood, it is necessary that each one acknowledge and confess that, when all men had lost innocence in the prevarication of Adam, having become unclean and, as the apostle says, children of wrath by nature, as the synod has set forth in the decree concerning original sin, they were so far servants of sin and under the power of the devil and death, that not only the gentiles through the force of nature, but not even the Jews through the very letter of the law of Moses, could be freed or rise from that state, although free will in them was by no means extinct, albeit its powers were weakened and inclined.","Primum declarat sancta synodus, ad iustificationis doctrinam probe et sincere intelligendam, oportere ut unusquisque agnoscat et fateatur, quod cum omnes homines in praevaricatione Adae innocentiam perdidissent, facti immundi et (ut apostolus inquit) natura filii irae, quemadmodum in decreto de peccato originali exposuit, usque adeo servi erant peccati et sub potestate diaboli ac mortis, ut non modo gentes per vim naturae, sed ne Iudaei quidem per ipsam etiam litteram legis Moysi inde liberari aut surgere possent, tametsi in eis liberum arbitrium minime extinctum esset, viribus licet attenuatum et inclinatum."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p66","Chapter II","Whence it came to pass that the heavenly Father, the Father of mercies and the God of all consolation, when that blessed fullness of time had come, sent to men Christ Jesus, His own Son, both declared and promised to many holy fathers before the law and during the time of the law, that He might both redeem the Jews who were under the law, and that the gentiles who did not follow after justice might apprehend justice, and that all might receive the adoption of sons. Him God proposed as a propitiator through faith in His blood for our sins, and not only for our sins but also for those of the whole world.","Quo factum est, ut coelestis pater, pater misericordiarum et deus totius consolationis, cum veniret beata illa plenitudo temporis, christum Iesum filium suum, et ante legem et legis tempore multis sanctis patribus declaratum ac promissum, ad homines miserit, ut et Iudaeos qui sub lege erant redimeret, et gentes quae non sectabantur iustitiam iustitiam apprehenderent, atque omnes adoptionem filiorum reciperent. Hunc proposuit deus propitiatorem per fidem in sanguine ipsius pro peccatis nostris, non solum autem pro nostris, sed etiam pro totius mundi."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p67","Chapter III","But although He died for all, not all, however, receive the benefit of His death, but only those to whom the merit of His passion is communicated. For just as truly men, if they were not born propagated from the seed of Adam, would not be born unjust, since by that propagation they contract their own unrighteousness when they are conceived, so if they were not born again in Christ they would never be justified, since by that regeneration, through the merit of His passion, the grace by which they are made just is bestowed upon them.","Verum, etsi ille pro omnibus mortuus est, non omnes tamen mortis eius beneficium recipiunt, sed ii dumtaxat, quibus meritum passionis eius communicatur. Nam sicut revera homines, nisi ex semine Adae propagati nascerentur, non nascerentur iniusti, cum ea propagatione per ipsum dum concipiuntur propriam iniustitiam contrahant: ita nisi in christo renascerentur, numquam iustificarentur, cum ea renascentia per meritum passionis eius gratia, qua iusti fiunt, illis tribuatur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p68","Chapter IV","For this benefit the apostle exhorts us always to give thanks to the Father, who made us worthy to be partakers of the lot of the saints in light, and who delivered us from the power of darkness and translated us into the kingdom of the Son of His love, in whom we have redemption and remission of sins. By which words the description of the justification of the impious is indicated, as a translation from that state in which man is born a child of the first Adam into the state of grace and the adoption of the sons of God through the second Adam, Jesus Christ our Savior. Which translation, after the gospel has been promulgated, cannot be effected without the laver of regeneration or its desire, as it is written: \"Unless a man is born again of water and the Holy Spirit, he cannot enter into the kingdom of God.\"","Pro hoc beneficio apostolus gratias nos semper agere hortatur patri, qui dignos nos fecit in partem sortis sanctorum in lumine, et eripuit de potestate tenebrarum transtulit que in regnum filii dilectionis suae, in quo habemus redemptionem et remissionem peccatorum. Quibus verbis iustificationis impii descriptio insinuatur, ut sit translatio ab eo statu, in quo homo nascitur filius primi Adae, in statum gratiae et adoptionis filiorum dei per secundum Adam Iesum christum salvatorem nostrum; quae quidem translatio post evangelium promulgatum sine lavacro regenerationis aut eius voto fieri non potest, sicut scriptum est: \"nisi quis renatus fuerit ex aqua et spiritu sancto, non potest introire in regnum dei.\""],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p69","Chapter V","Moreover, the synod declares that the beginning of justification itself in adults is to be taken from the prevenient grace of God through Christ Jesus, that is, from His vocation, by which, without any merits on their part, they are called, so that they who were turned away from God by sins may, by His exciting and assisting grace, be disposed to convert themselves to their own justification by freely assenting to and cooperating with that same grace; in such a way that, while God touches the heart of man through the illumination of the Holy Spirit, man himself does nothing at all in receiving that inspiration, since he is able also to reject it, nor yet without the grace of God can he move himself by his own free will to justice before Him.","Declarat praeterea ipsius iustificationis exordium in adultis a dei per christum Iesum praeveniente gratia sumendum esse, hoc est ab eius vocatione, qua nullis eorum existentibus meritis vocantur, ut qui per peccata a deo aversi erant, per eius excitantem atque adiuvantem gratiam ad convertendum se ad suam ipsorum iustificationem eidem gratiae libere assentiendo et cooperando disponantur; ita ut, tangente deo cor hominis per spiritus sancti illuminationem, neque homo ipse nihil omnino agat inspirationem illam recipiens, quippe qui illam et abiicere potest, neque tamen sine gratia dei movere se ad iustitiam coram illo libera sua voluntate possit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p70","Chapter V","Whence in the sacred writings, when it is said: \"Turn to me, and I will turn to you,\" we are admonished of our liberty; when we reply: \"Turn us, O Lord, to you, and we shall be turned,\" we confess that we are prevented by the grace of God.","Unde in sacris litteris, cum dicitur: \"convertimini ad me, et ego convertar ad vos,\" libertatis nostrae admonemur; cum respondemus: \"converte nos, domine, ad te, et convertemur,\" dei nos gratia praeveniri confitemur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p71","Chapter VI","But they are disposed to justice itself when, aroused by divine grace and helped, conceiving faith from hearing, they are freely moved toward God, believing those things to be true which have been divinely revealed and promised, and especially that the impious man is justified by God through His grace by the redemption which is in Christ Jesus; and when, understanding themselves to be sinners, they turn themselves from the fear of divine justice, by which they are profitably shaken, to consider the mercy of God, being raised up into hope, trusting that God will be propitious to them for Christ's sake; and they begin to love Him as the fountain of all justice; and therefore they are moved against sins by a certain hatred and detestation, that is, by that penance which must be performed before baptism; finally, when they propose to receive baptism, to begin a new life, and to keep the divine commandments.","Disponuntur autem ad ipsam iustitiam dum excitati divina gratia et adiuti, fidem ex auditu concipientes, libere moventur in deum, credentes vera esse quae divinitus revelata et promissa sunt, atque illud in primis a deo iustificari impium per gratiam eius per redemptionem quae est in christo Iesu; et dum peccatores se esse intelligentes, a divinae iustitiae timore, quo utiliter concutiuntur, ad considerandam dei misericordiam se convertendo, in spem eriguntur, fidentes deum sibi propter christum propitium fore, illum que tamquam omnis iustitiae fontem diligere incipiunt; ac propterea moventur adversus peccata per odium aliquod et detestationem, hoc est per eam poenitentiam, quam ante baptismum agi oportet; denique dum proponunt suscipere baptismum, inchoare novam vitam et servare divina mandata."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p72","Chapter VI","Of this disposition it is written: \"He who comes to God must believe that He is, and that He is a rewarder of those who seek Him\"; and: \"Be of good cheer, son, your sins are forgiven you\"; and: \"The fear of the Lord drives away sin\"; and: \"Do penance, and be baptized every one of you in the name of Jesus Christ for the remission of your sins, and you shall receive the gift of the Holy Spirit\"; and: \"Going therefore, teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, teaching them to observe all things whatsoever I have commanded you\"; finally: \"Prepare your hearts unto the Lord.\"","De hac dispositione scriptum est: \"accedentem ad deum oportet credere quia est, et quod inquirentibus se remunerator sit\"; et: \"confide fili, remittuntur tibi peccata tua\"; et: \"timor domini expellit peccatum\"; et: \"poenitentiam agite, et baptizetur unusquisque vestrum in nomine Iesu christi in remissionem peccatorum vestrorum, et accipietis donum spiritus sancti\"; et: \"euntes ergo docete omnes gentes, baptizantes eos in nomine patris et filii et spiritus sancti, docentes eos servare quaecumque mandavi vobis\"; denique: \"praeparate corda vestra domino.\""],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p73","Chapter VII","This disposition or preparation is followed by justification itself, which is not only the remission of sins but also the sanctification and renewal of the inner man through the voluntary reception of grace and gifts, whereby a man from unjust becomes just, and from an enemy becomes a friend, that he may be an heir according to the hope of eternal life.","Hanc dispositionem seu praeparationem iustificatio ipsa consequitur, quae non est sola peccatorum remissio, sed et sanctificatio et renovatio interioris hominis per voluntariam susceptionem gratiae et donorum, unde homo ex iniusto fit iustus, et ex inimico amicus, ut sit haeres secundum spem vitae aeternae."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p74","Chapter VII","The causes of this justification are: the final cause, indeed, the glory of God and of Christ and eternal life; the efficient cause, however, is the merciful God who gratuitously washes and sanctifies, signing and anointing with the Holy Spirit of promise, who is the pledge of our inheritance; the meritorious cause, however, is His most beloved only-begotten, our Lord Jesus Christ, who, when we were enemies, by the exceeding love with which He loved us, merited justification for us by His most holy passion on the wood of the cross and made satisfaction for us to God the Father; the instrumental cause, likewise, is the sacrament of baptism, which is the sacrament of faith, without which no one was ever justified.","Huius iustificationis causae sunt: finalis quidem gloria dei et christi ac vita aeterna; efficiens vero misericors deus, qui gratuito abluit et sanctificat, signans et ungens spiritu promissionis sancto, qui est pignus haereditatis nostrae; meritoria autem dilectissimus unigenitus suus, dominus noster Iesus christus, qui cum essemus inimici, propter nimiam charitatem qua dilexit nos, sua sanctissima passione in ligno crucis nobis iustificationem meruit et pro nobis deo patri satisfecit; instrumentalis item sacramentum baptismi, quod est sacramentum fidei, sine qua nulli umquam contigit iustificatio."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p75","Chapter VII","Finally, the sole formal cause is the justice of God, not by which He Himself is just, but by which He makes us just, that is, by which we, gifted by Him, are renewed in the spirit of our mind, and not only are we reputed, but we are truly called and are just, each one receiving his own justice within himself according to the measure which the Holy Spirit distributes to each as He wills, and according to each one's own disposition and cooperation.","Demum unica formalis causa est iustitia dei, non qua ipse iustus est, sed qua nos iustos facit, qua videlicet ab eo donati renovamur spiritu mentis nostrae, et non modo reputamur, sed vere iusti nominamur et sumus, iustitiam in nobis recipientes unusquisque suam secundum mensuram, quam spiritus sanctus partitur singulis prout vult, et secundum propriam cuiusque dispositionem et cooperationem."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p76","Chapter VII","Although, indeed, no one can be just unless the merits of the passion of our Lord Jesus Christ are communicated to him, this, however, is done in the justification of the impious when, by the merit of the same most holy passion, the charity of God is poured forth by the Holy Spirit into the hearts of those who are justified and inheres in them. Whence, in justification itself, together with the remission of sins, man receives all these things infused at once through Jesus Christ, into whom he is ingrafted: faith, hope, and charity. For faith, unless hope and charity be added to it, neither unites perfectly with Christ nor makes a living member of His body.","Quamquam enim nemo possit esse iustus, nisi cui merita passionis domini nostri Iesu christi communicantur, id tamen in hac impii iustificatione fit, dum eiusdem sanctissimae passionis merito per spiritum sanctum charitas dei diffunditur in cordibus eorum qui iustificantur atque ipsis inhaeret. Unde in ipsa iustificatione cum remissione peccatorum haec omnia simul infusa accipit homo per Iesum christum, cui inseritur: fidem, spem et charitatem. Nam fides, nisi ad eam spes accedat et charitas, neque unit perfecte cum christo neque corporis eius vivum membrum efficit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p77","Chapter VII","For which reason it is most truly said that \"faith without works is dead and idle,\" and \"in Christ Jesus neither circumcision avails anything nor uncircumcision, but faith which works through charity.\"","Qua ratione verissime dicitur fidem sine operibus mortuam et otiosam esse, et \"in christo Iesu neque circumcisionem aliquid valere neque praeputium, sed fidem quae per charitatem operatur.\""],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p78","Chapter VII","This faith, before the sacrament of baptism, the catechumens ask of the church from the tradition of the apostles, when they ask for faith which bestows eternal life, which faith without hope and charity faith cannot bestow. Whence also they immediately hear the word of Christ: \"If you will enter into life, keep the commandments.\"","Hanc fidem ante baptismi sacramentum, ex apostolorum traditione, catechumeni ab ecclesia petunt, cum petunt fidem vitam aeternam praestantem, quam sine spe et charitate fides praestare non potest. Unde et statim verbum christi audiunt: \"si vis ad vitam ingredi, serva mandata.\""],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p79","Chapter VII","Thus, receiving true and Christian justice, they are commanded immediately after being born again to preserve it as the first robe given to them through Christ Jesus in place of that which Adam by his disobedience lost for himself and for us, white and immaculate, so that they may bear it before the tribunal of our Lord Jesus Christ and have eternal life.","Itaque veram et christianam iustitiam accipientes, eam ceu primam stolam pro illa quam Adam sua inobedientia sibi et nobis perdidit, per christum Iesum illis donatam, candidam et immaculatam, iubentur statim renati conservare, ut eam perferant ante tribunal domini nostri Iesu christi et habeant vitam aeternam."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p80","Chapter VIII","But when the apostle says that man is justified by faith and freely, those words are to be understood in that sense which the perpetual consent of the catholic church has held and expressed, namely, that we are said to be justified by faith because faith is the beginning of human salvation, the foundation and root of all justification, without which it is impossible to please God and to come to the fellowship of His sons; and we are said to be justified freely because none of those things which precede justification, whether faith or works, merit the grace of justification. For if it is grace, it is no longer by works; otherwise (as the same apostle says) grace is no longer grace.","Cum vero apostolus dicit iustificari hominem per fidem et gratis, ea verba in eo sensu intelligenda sunt, quem perpetuus ecclesiae catholicae consensus tenuit et expressit, ut scilicet per fidem ideo iustificari dicamur, quia fides est humanae salutis initium, fundamentum et radix omnis iustificationis, sine qua impossibile est placere deo et ad filiorum eius consortium pervenire; gratis autem iustificari ideo dicamur, quia nihil eorum quae iustificationem praecedunt, sive fides sive opera, ipsam iustificationis gratiam promeretur; si enim gratia est, iam non ex operibus, alioquin (ut idem apostolus inquit) gratia iam non est gratia."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p81","Chapter IX","But although it is necessary to believe that sins are neither remitted nor have ever been remitted except freely by divine mercy for Christ's sake, it is nevertheless not to be said to anyone who boasts of the confidence and certainty of the remission of his sins and rests in it alone, that sins are forgiven or have been forgiven, since this vain and altogether remote from piety confidence can exist among heretics and schismatics, indeed in our time it is preached with great contention against the catholic church.","Quamvis autem necessarium sit credere, neque remitti neque remissa umquam fuisse peccata nisi gratis divina misericordia propter christum, nemini tamen fiduciam et certitudinem remissionis peccatorum suorum iactanti et in ea sola quiescenti peccata dimitti vel dimissa esse dicendum est, cum apud haereticos et schismaticos possit, immo nostra tempestate sit, et magna contra ecclesiam catholicam contentione praedicetur, vana haec et ab omni pietate remota fiducia."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p82","Chapter IX","Neither is that to be asserted, that those who are truly justified ought to determine with themselves without any doubt at all that they are justified, and that no one is absolved from sins and justified except him who certainly believes that he is absolved and justified, and that absolution and justification are perfected by this faith alone, as if he who does not believe this doubts the promises of God and the efficacy of the death and resurrection of Christ.","Sed neque illud asserendum est, oportere eos qui vere iustificati sunt absque ulla omnino dubitatione apud semetipsos statuere se esse iustificatos, neminem que a peccatis absolvi ac iustificari nisi eum qui certo credat se absolutum et iustificatum esse, atque hac sola fide absolutionem et iustificationem perfici, quasi qui hoc non credit, de dei promissis de que mortis et resurrectionis christi efficacia dubitet."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p83","Chapter IX","For just as no pious person ought to doubt the mercy of God, the merit of Christ, and the virtue and efficacy of the sacraments, so each one, when he considers himself and his own weakness and indisposition, can fear and be anxious concerning his grace, since no one can know with the certainty of faith, which cannot be subject to falsehood, that he has obtained the grace of God.","Nam sicut nemo pius de dei misericordia, de christi merito, de que sacramentorum virtute et efficacia dubitare debet, sic quilibet, dum se ipsum suam que propriam infirmitatem et indispositionem respicit, de sua gratia formidare et timere potest, cum nullus scire valeat certitudine fidei, cui non potest subesse falsum, se gratiam dei esse consecutum."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p84","Chapter X","Thus, therefore, justified and made friends of God and domestics, going from virtue to virtue, they are renewed, as the apostle says, day by day, that is, by mortifying the members of their flesh and presenting them as instruments of justice unto sanctification, through the observance of the commandments of God and of the church, in that very justice received through the grace of Christ, cooperating with faith through good works, they grow and are more and more justified, as it is written: \"He who is just, let him be justified still\"; and: \"Be not afraid to be justified even unto death\"; and again: \"You see that by works a man is justified, and not by faith only.\" This increase of justice the holy church asks for when it prays: \"Give us, Lord, increase of faith, hope, and charity.\"","Sic ergo iustificati et amici dei ac domestici facti, euntes de virtute in virtutem, renovantur, ut apostolus inquit, de die in diem, hoc est mortificando membra carnis suae et exhibendo ea arma iustitiae in sanctificationem, per observationem mandatorum dei et ecclesiae, in ipsa iustitia per christi gratiam accepta, cooperante fide bonis operibus, crescunt atque magis iustificantur, sicut scriptum est: \"qui iustus est, iustificetur adhuc\"; et: \"ne verearis usque ad mortem iustificari\"; et rursus: \"videtis quoniam ex operibus iustificatur homo, et non ex fide tantum.\" Hoc vero iustitiae incrementum petit sancta ecclesia, cum orat: \"da nobis, domine, fidei, spei et charitatis augmentum.\""],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p85","Chapter XI","But no one, however much justified, ought to think himself free from the observance of the commandments; no one ought to use that rash saying, forbidden by the fathers under anathema, that the precepts of God are impossible for a justified man to observe. For God does not command impossibilities, but by commanding He warns both to do what you can and to ask for what you cannot, and He helps so that you may be able. His commandments are not heavy; His yoke is sweet and His burden light.","Nemo autem, quantumvis iustificatus, liberum se esse ab observatione mandatorum putare debet; nemo temeraria illa et a patribus sub anathemate prohibita voce uti, dei praecepta homini iustificato ad observandum esse impossibilia. Nam deus impossibilia non iubet, sed iubendo monet et facere quod possis et petere quod non possis, et adiuvat ut possis. Cuius mandata gravia non sunt, cuius iugum suave est et onus leve."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p86","Chapter XI","For those who are the sons of God love Christ; but those who love Him, as He Himself testifies, keep His words, which indeed with divine help they are able to do. For although in this mortal life, however holy and just, they sometimes fall into at least light and daily sins, which are also called venial, they do not therefore cease to be just. For the saying of the just is both humble and true: \"Forgive us our debts.\"","Qui enim sunt filii dei, christum diligunt; qui autem diligunt eum, ut ipsemet testatur, servant sermones eius, quod utique cum divino auxilio praestare possunt. Licet enim in hac mortali vita, quantumvis sancti et iusti, in levia saltem et quotidiana, quae etiam venialia dicuntur, peccata quandoque cadant, non propterea desinunt esse iusti. Nam iustorum illa vox est et humilis et verax: \"dimitte nobis debita nostra.\""],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p87","Chapter XI","Whence it follows that the just themselves ought to feel themselves the more obliged to walk in the way of justice, since, now freed from sin and made servants of God, living soberly, justly, and piously, they can make progress through Christ Jesus, through whom they have had access into this grace.","Quo fit, ut iusti ipsi eo magis se obligatos ad ambulandum in via iustitiae sentire debeant, quo liberati iam a peccato, servi autem facti deo, sobrie et iuste et pie viventes, proficere possunt per christum Iesum, per quem accessum habuerunt in gratiam istam."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p88","Chapter XI","For God does not desert those who have been once justified by His grace, unless He is first deserted by them.","Deus namque sua gratia semel iustificatos non deserit, nisi ab eis prius deseratur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p89","Chapter XI","Thus no one ought to flatter himself in faith alone, thinking that by faith alone he has been made an heir and will obtain the inheritance, even if he does not suffer with Christ, that he may be also glorified with Him. For Christ Himself, as the apostle says, \"though He was a Son, yet learned obedience by the things which He suffered; and being made perfect, He became the author of eternal salvation to all who obey Him.\" For which reason the apostle himself admonishes the justified, saying: \"Do you not know that those who run in a race indeed all run, but one receives the prize? So run that you may obtain. I therefore so run, not as uncertainly; I so fight, not as one beating the air; but I chastise my body and bring it into subjection, lest perhaps when I have preached to others, I myself should be rejected.\" Likewise the prince of the apostles, Peter: \"Labor to make your calling and election sure by good works; for if you do these things, you shall not sin at any time.\"","Itaque nemo sibi in sola fide blandiri debet, putans fide sola se haeredem esse constitutum haereditatem que consecuturum, etiamsi christo non compatiatur, ut et conglorificetur. Nam et christus ipse (ut inquit apostolus) \"cum esset filius dei, didicit ex his quae passus est oboedientiam, et consummatus factus est omnibus obtemperantibus sibi causa salutis aeternae.\" Propterea apostolus ipse monet iustificatos dicens: \"nescitis quod ii qui in stadio currunt, omnes quidem currunt, sed unus accipit bravium? Sic currite ut comprehendatis. Ego igitur sic curro, non quasi in incertum; sic pugno, non quasi aerem verberans; sed castigo corpus meum et in servitutem redigo, ne forte cum aliis praedicaverim, ipse reprobus efficiar.\" Item princeps apostolorum Petrus: \"satagite ut per bona opera certam vestram vocationem et electionem faciatis; haec enim facientes, non peccabitis aliquando.\""],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p90","Chapter XI","Whence it is evident that those oppose the doctrine of orthodox religion who say that the just man sins venially at least in every good work, or (what is more intolerable) that he merits eternal punishments, and also those who say that the just sin in all their works if, in exciting their own sluggishness and exhorting themselves to run the race, they also look to the eternal reward, since it is written: \"I have inclined my heart to perform your justifications for the sake of the reward,\" and the apostle says of Moses that \"he looked to the reward.\"","Unde constat eos orthodoxae religionis doctrinae adversari, qui dicunt iustum in omni bono opere saltem venialiter peccare, aut (quod intolerabilius est) poenas aeternas mereri, atque etiam eos qui statuunt in omnibus operibus iustos peccare, si in illis suam ipsorum socordiam excitando et sese ad currendum in stadio cohortando, cum hoc ut in primis glorificetur deus, mercedem quoque intuentur aeternam, cum scriptum sit: \"inclinavi cor meum ad faciendas iustificationes tuas propter retributionem\"; et de Moyse dicat apostolus quod \"aspiciebat in remunerationem.\""],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p91","Chapter XII","No one also, as long as he lives in this mortality, ought to presume so much concerning the secret mystery of divine predestination as to determine certainly that he is altogether in the number of the predestined, as if it were true that the justified either cannot sin any more, or that if he does sin, he ought to promise himself certain repentance. For except by special revelation, it cannot be known whom God has chosen to Himself.","Nemo quoque, quamdiu in hac mortalitate vivitur, de arcano divinae praedestinationis mysterio usque adeo praesumere debet, ut certo statuat se omnino esse in numero praedestinatorum, quasi verum esset quod iustificatus aut amplius peccare non possit, aut si peccaverit, certam sibi resipiscentiam promittere debeat. Nam nisi ex speciali revelatione, sciri non potest quos deus sibi elegerit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p92","Chapter XII","Likewise, concerning the gift of perseverance, of which it is written: \"He who perseveres to the end, he shall be saved,\" which indeed cannot be obtained from elsewhere except from Him who is able to make him stand who stands that he may stand perseveringly, and to restore him who falls, no one ought to promise himself anything certain with absolute certainty, although all ought to place and repose most firm hope in the divine help.","Similiter de perseverantiae munere, de quo scriptum est: \"qui perseveraverit usque in finem, hic salvus erit\" (quod quidem aliunde haberi non potest nisi ab eo, qui potens est eum qui stat statuere ut perseveranter stet, et eum qui cadit restituere), nemo sibi certi aliquid absoluta certitudine polliceatur, tametsi in dei auxilio firmissimam spem collocare et reponere omnes debent."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p93","Chapter XII","For God, unless they themselves lack His grace, as He has begun a good work, so He will perfect it, working to will and to accomplish. Nevertheless, those who think they stand, let them take heed lest they fall; and let them work out their salvation with fear and trembling, in labors, in vigils, in alms, in prayers, in offerings, in fastings, in chastity. For they ought to fear, knowing that they are born again to the hope of glory, and not yet to glory, and that there remains a struggle with the flesh, with the world, with the devil, in which they cannot be victorious unless, with the grace of God, they obey the apostle saying: \"We are debtors, not to the flesh, to live according to the flesh; for if you live according to the flesh, you shall die; but if by the spirit you mortify the deeds of the flesh, you shall live.\"","Deus enim, nisi ipsi illius gratiae defuerint, sicut coepit opus bonum, ita perficiet, operans velle et perficere. Verumtamen qui se existimant stare, videant ne cadant, et cum timore ac tremore salutem suam operentur, in laboribus, in vigiliis, in eleemosynis, in orationibus, in oblationibus, in ieiuniis, in castitate. Formidare enim debent, scientes quod in spem gloriae, et nondum in gloriam renati sunt, de pugna quae superest cum carne, cum mundo, cum diabolo, in qua victores esse non possunt, nisi cum dei gratia apostolo obtemperent dicenti: \"debitores sumus non carni ut secundum carnem vivamus; si enim secundum carnem vixeritis, moriemini; si autem spiritu facta carnis mortificaveritis, vivetis.\""],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p94","Chapter XIII","But those who by sin have fallen from the received grace of justification shall be able to be justified again, when, God exciting them, through the sacrament of penance they shall have procured to recover the lost grace by the merit of Christ. For this manner of justification is the repair of the fallen, which the holy fathers have fittingly called the second plank after the shipwreck of lost grace.","Qui vero ab accepta iustificationis gratia per peccatum exciderunt, rursus iustificari poterunt, cum excitante deo per poenitentiae sacramentum meritum christi amissam gratiam recuperare procuraverint. Hic enim iustificationis modus est lapsi reparatio, quam secundam post naufragium deperditae gratiae tabulam sancti patres apte nuncuparunt."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p95","Chapter XIII","For for those who after baptism fall into sins, Christ Jesus instituted the sacrament of penance, when He said: \"Receive the Holy Spirit: whose sins you shall forgive, they are forgiven them; and whose sins you shall retain, they are retained.\" Whence it is to be taught that the penance of a Christian man after his fall is very different from that of baptism, and that it contains not only a cessation from sins and a detestation of them, or a contrite and humble heart, but also a sacramental confession of those same sins, at least in desire and to be made in its own time, and priestly absolution, likewise satisfaction by fasting, alms, prayers, and other pious exercises of the spiritual life, not indeed for eternal punishment, which is remitted together with the guilt either by the sacrament or by the desire of the sacrament, but for temporal punishment, which (as the sacred writings teach) is not always entirely remitted, as in baptism, to those who, ungrateful for the grace they have received, have grieved the Holy Spirit and have not feared to violate the temple of God.","Etenim pro iis qui post baptismum in peccata labuntur, christus Iesus sacramentum instituit poenitentiae, cum dixit: \"accipite spiritum sanctum: quorum remiseritis peccata, remittuntur eis, et quorum retinueritis, retenta sunt.\" Unde docendum est christianae hominis poenitentiam post lapsum multo aliam esse a baptismali, eaque contineri non modo cessationem a peccatis et eorum detestationem, aut cor contritum et humiliatum, verum etiam eorundem sacramentalem confessionem, saltem in voto et suo tempore faciendam, et sacerdotalem absolutionem, item que satisfactionem per ieiunium, eleemosynas, orationes et alia pia spiritualis vitae exercitia, non quidem pro poena aeterna, quae vel sacramento vel sacramenti voto una cum culpa remittitur, sed pro poena temporali, quae (ut sacrae litterae docent) non tota semper, ut in baptismo fit, dimittitur illis qui, gratiae dei quam acceperunt ingrati, spiritum sanctum contristaverunt et templum dei violare non sunt veriti."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p96","Chapter XIII","Concerning which penance it is written: \"Remember whence you have fallen, do penance, and do the first works\"; and again: \"The sorrow that is according to God works penance steadfast unto salvation\"; and again: \"Do penance\" and \"Bring forth fruits worthy of penance.\"","De qua poenitentia scriptum est: \"memor esto unde excideris, age poenitentiam, et prima opera fac\"; et iterum: \"quae secundum deum tristitia est, poenitentiam in salutem stabilem operatur\"; et rursus: \"poenitentiam agite\" et \"facite fructus dignos poenitentiae.\""],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p97","Chapter XIV","Moreover, against the crafty wits of certain men who by sweet words and benedictions seduce the hearts of the innocent, it is to be asserted that not only by infidelity, by which faith itself is lost, but also by any other mortal sin, even if faith is not lost, the received grace of justification is lost, defending the doctrine of the divine law which excludes from the kingdom of God not only unbelievers, but also the faithful who are fornicators, adulterers, effeminate, abusers of themselves with mankind, thieves, covetous, drunkards, revilers, extortioners, and all others who commit deadly sins, from which with the help of divine grace they are able to abstain, and for which they are separated from the grace of Christ.","Adversus etiam hominum quorumdam callida ingenia, qui per dulces sermones et benedictiones seducunt corda innocentium, asserendum est non modo infidelitate, per quam et ipsa fides amittitur, sed etiam quocumque alio mortali peccato, quamvis non amittatur fides, acceptam iustificationis gratiam amitti; divinae legis doctrinam defendendo, quae a regno dei non solum infideles excludit, sed et fideles quoque fornicarios, adulteros, molles, masculorum concubitores, fures, avaros, ebriosos, maledicos, rapaces, ceteros que omnes qui letalia committunt peccata, a quibus cum divinae gratiae adiumento abstinere possunt, et pro quibus a christi gratia separantur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p98","Chapter XV","Against those also who say that the just man sins at least venially in every good work, or what is more intolerable, that he merits eternal punishment, and also those who say that the just sin in all works if, in exciting their own sluggishness and exhorting themselves to run the race, they also look to the eternal reward, since it is written: \"I have inclined my heart to perform your justifications for the sake of the reward,\" and the apostle says of Moses that \"he looked to the reward.\"","Adversus eos etiam qui dicunt iustum in omni bono opere saltem venialiter peccare, aut quod intolerabilius est, poenas aeternas mereri, atque etiam eos qui statuunt in omnibus operibus iustos peccare, si in illis suam ipsorum socordiam excitando et sese ad currendum in stadio cohortando, cum hoc ut in primis glorificetur deus, mercedem quoque intuentur aeternam, cum scriptum sit: \"inclinavi cor meum ad faciendas iustificationes tuas propter retributionem\"; et de Moyse dicat apostolus quod \"aspiciebat in remunerationem.\""],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p99","Chapter XVI","Therefore, to justified men, whether they have preserved the received grace perpetually or have recovered it when lost, the words of the apostle are to be set forth: \"Abound in every good work, knowing that your labor is not in vain in the Lord.\" For God is not unjust, that He should forget your work and the love which you have shown in His name. And \"do not lose your confidence, which has great reward.\" And thus, to those who work well to the end and hope in God, eternal life is to be set forth, both as a grace mercifully promised to the sons of God through Christ Jesus, and as a reward faithfully to be rendered to their good works and merits by the promise of God Himself.","Iustificatis igitur hominibus, sive acceptam gratiam perpetuo conservaverint sive amissam recuperaverint, proponenda sunt apostoli verba: \"abundate in omni opere bono, scientes quod labor vester non est inanis in domino.\" \"Non enim iniustus est deus, ut obliviscatur operis vestri et dilectionis, quam ostendistis in nomine ipsius.\" Et \"nolite amittere confidentiam vestram, quae magnam habet remunerationem.\" Atque ideo bene operantibus usque in finem et in deo sperantibus proponenda est vita aeterna, et tamquam gratia filiis dei per christum Iesum misericorditer promissa, et tamquam merces ex ipsius dei promissione bonis ipsorum operibus et meritis fideliter reddenda."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p100","Chapter XVI","For this is that crown of justice which the apostle said was laid up for him after his combat and course, to be rendered to him by the just judge, not only to him, however, but to all who love His coming. For since Christ Jesus Himself, as the head into the members and as the vine into the branches, continually pours His virtue into the justified, which virtue always precedes, accompanies, and follows their good works, and without which they could by no means be pleasing to God and meritorious, nothing more is to be believed to be lacking to the justified, so that they are considered to have fully satisfied the divine law by those works which are done in God, according to the state of this life, and to have truly merited eternal life, to be obtained in its own time, provided they have died in grace.","Haec est enim illa corona iustitiae, quam post suum certamen et cursum repositam sibi esse aiebat apostolus a iusto iudice sibi reddendam, non solum autem sibi, sed et omnibus qui diligunt adventum eius. Cum enim ille ipse christus Iesus, tamquam caput in membra et tamquam vitis in palmites, in ipsos iustificatos iugiter virtutem influat, quae virtus bona eorum opera semper antecedit, comitatur et subsequitur, et sine qua nullo pacto deo grata et meritoria esse possent, nihil ipsis iustificatis amplius deesse credendum est, quominus plene illis quidem operibus, quae in deo sunt facta, divinae legi pro huius vitae statu satisfecisse et vitam aeternam suo etiam tempore (si tamen in gratia decesserint) consequendam vere promeruisse censeantur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p101","Chapter XVI","For Christ our Savior says: \"If anyone drinks of the water that I shall give him, he shall not thirst forever; but it shall become in him a fountain of water springing up into everlasting life.\" Thus neither is our own justice established as from ourselves, nor is the justice of God ignored or rejected. For that which is called our justice, because by it inhering in us we are justified, is the same also the justice of God, because it is infused into us by God through the merit of Christ.","Cum christus salvator noster dicat: \"si quis biberit ex aqua quam ego dabo ei, non sitiet in aeternum, sed fiet in eo fons aquae salientis in vitam aeternam.\" Ita neque propria nostra iustitia, tamquam ex nobis propria, statuitur, neque ignoratur aut repudiatur iustitia dei; quae enim iustitia nostra dicitur, quia per eam nobis inhaerentem iustificamur, illa eadem dei est, quia a deo nobis infunditur per christi meritum."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p102","Chapter XVI","Nor is that to be omitted, that although good works are so much attributed in the sacred writings that Christ promises that he who has given a cup of cold water to one of his least shall not lose his reward, and the apostle testifies that that which is at present momentary and light of our tribulation works for us above measure an eternal weight of glory, nevertheless, far be it that a Christian man should trust or glory in himself and not in the Lord, whose goodness toward all men is so great that He wills that those things which are His own gifts be their merits.","Neque vero illud omittendum est, quod licet bonis operibus in sacris litteris usque adeo tribuatur, ut etiam qui uni ex minimis suis potum aquae frigidae dederit, promittat christus eum non esse sua mercede cariturum, et apostolus testetur id quod in praesenti est momentaneum et leve tribulationis nostrae supra modum in sublimitate aeternum gloriae pondus operari in nobis, absit tamen ut christianus homo in se ipso vel confidat vel glorietur et non in domino, cuius tanta est erga omnes homines bonitas, ut eorum velit esse merita, quae sunt ipsius dona."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p103","Chapter XVI","And because we all offend in many things, each one ought to have before his eyes both mercy and goodness and severity and judgment, and neither should anyone judge himself, even if he is conscious of nothing to himself, since the whole life of men is to be examined and judged not by human judgment but by God, who will bring to light the hidden things of darkness and will make manifest the counsels of the hearts, and then will every man have praise from God, who, as it is written, will render to every man according to his works.","Et quia in multis offendimus omnes, unusquisque sicut misericordiam et bonitatem, ita severitatem et iudicium ante oculos habere debet, neque se ipsum aliquis, etiam si nihil sibi conscius fuerit, iudicare, quoniam omnis hominum vita non humano iudicio examinanda et iudicanda est, sed dei, qui illuminabit abscondita tenebrarum et manifestabit consilia cordium, et tunc laus erit unicuique a deo, qui, ut scriptum est, reddet unicuique secundum opera sua."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p104","Canons Concerning Justification","If anyone says that man can be justified before God by his own works, which are done either by the powers of human nature or by the teaching of the law, without the divine grace through Christ Jesus: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit hominem suis operibus, quae vel per humanae naturae vires vel per legis doctrinam fiunt, absque divina per christum Iesum gratia posse iustificari coram deo: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p105","Canon II","If anyone says that divine grace through Christ Jesus is given for this only, that man may be able more easily to live justly and to merit eternal life, as if by free will without grace he can do both, though with difficulty and hardly: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit ad hoc solum divinam gratiam per christum Iesum dari, ut facilius homo iuste vivere ac vitam aeternam promereri possit, quasi per liberum arbitrium sine gratia utrumque, sed aegre tamen et difficulter possit: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p106","Canon III","If anyone says that without the prevenient inspiration of the Holy Spirit and His help, man can believe, hope, love, or repent as he ought, so that the grace of justification may be conferred upon him: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit sine praeveniente spiritus sancti inspiratione atque eius adiutorio hominem credere, sperare et diligere aut poenitere posse sicut oportet, ut ei iustificationis gratia conferatur: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p107","Canon IV","If anyone says that man's free will moved and excited by God does not cooperate at all by assenting to God exciting and calling, so that it may dispose and prepare itself to obtain the grace of justification, and that it cannot dissent if it wishes, but that, like something inanimate, it does nothing at all and remains merely passive: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit liberum hominis arbitrium a deo motum et excitatum nihil cooperari assentiendo deo excitanti atque vocanti, quo ad obtinendam iustificationis gratiam se disponat ac praeparet, neque posse dissentire, si velit, sed velut inanime quoddam nihil omnino agere mere que passive se habere: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p108","Canon V","If anyone says that free will was lost and extinguished after the sin of Adam, or that it is a thing only in name, indeed a name without a thing, a fiction finally brought into the church by Satan: let him be anathema.","Si quis liberum hominis arbitrium post Adae peccatum amissum et extinctum esse dixerit, aut rem esse de solo titulo, immo titulum sine re, figmentum denique a satana invectum in ecclesiam: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p109","Canon VI","If anyone says that it is not in man's power to make his ways evil, but that God works evil works just as He works good, not only permissively but also properly and by Himself, so that the betrayal of Judas is as much His own work as the calling of Paul: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit non esse in potestate hominis vias suas malas facere, sed mala opera ita ut bona deum operari, non permissive solum, sed etiam proprie et per se, adeo ut sit proprium eius opus non minus proditio Iudae quam vocatio Pauli: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p110","Canon VII","If anyone says that all works which are done before justification, in whatever way they are done, are truly sins, or merit the hatred of God, or that the more vehemently one strives to dispose himself for grace, the more grievously he sins: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit opera omnia quae ante iustificationem fiunt, quacumque ratione facta sint, vere esse peccata, vel odium dei mereri, aut quanto vehementius quis nititur se disponere ad gratiam, tanto eum gravius peccare: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p111","Canon VIII","If anyone says that the fear of hell, by which we flee to the mercy of God sorrowing for sins or abstain from sinning, is a sin, or that it makes sinners worse: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit gehennae metum, per quem ad misericordiam dei de peccatis dolendo confugimus vel a peccando abstinemus, peccatum esse, aut peccatores peiores facere: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p112","Canon IX","If anyone says that the impious is justified by faith alone, so as to understand that nothing else is required to cooperate to obtain the grace of justification, and that it is not necessary in any part that he be prepared and disposed by the movement of his own will: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit sola fide impium iustificari, ita ut intelligat nihil aliud requiri, quo ad iustificationis gratiam consequendam cooperetur, et nulla ex parte necesse esse eum suae voluntatis motu praeparari atque disponi: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p113","Canon X","If anyone says that men are justified without the justice of Christ, by which He merited justification for us, or that by that justice itself they are formally just: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit homines sine christi iustitia, per quam nobis meruit iustificari, aut per eam ipsam formaliter iustos esse: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p114","Canon XI","If anyone says that men are justified either by the sole imputation of the justice of Christ or by the sole remission of sins, excluding grace and charity which is poured forth into their hearts by the Holy Spirit and inheres in them, or that the grace by which we are justified is only the favor of God: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit homines iustificari vel sola imputatione iustitiae christi vel sola peccatorum remissione, exclusa gratia et charitate quae in cordibus eorum per spiritum sanctum diffundatur atque illis inhaereat, aut etiam gratiam qua iustificamur esse tantum favorem dei: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p115","Canon XII","If anyone says that justifying faith is nothing else than trust in divine mercy remitting sins for Christ's sake, or that it is this trust alone by which we are justified: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit fidem iustificantem nihil aliud esse quam fiduciam divinae misericordiae peccata remittentis propter christum, vel eam fiduciam solam esse qua iustificamur: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p116","Canon XIII","If anyone says that it is necessary for every man for the attainment of the remission of sins that he believe certainly and without any hesitation concerning his own weakness and indisposition that his sins are remitted to him: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit omni homini ad remissionem peccatorum assequendam necessarium esse, ut credat certo et absque ulla haesitatione propriae infirmitatis et indispositionis peccata sibi esse remissa: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p117","Canon XIV","If anyone says that man is absolved from sins and justified because he certainly believes that he is absolved and justified, or that no one is truly justified except he who believes that he is justified, and that absolution and justification are perfected by this faith alone: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit hominem a peccatis absolvi ac iustificari ex eo quod se absolvi ac iustificari certo credat, aut neminem vere esse iustificatum nisi qui credit se esse iustificatum, et hac sola fide absolutionem et iustificationem perfici: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p118","Canon XV","If anyone says that a man who is born again and justified is bound from faith to believe that he is certainly in the number of the predestined: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit hominem renatum et iustificatum teneri ex fide ad credendum se certo esse in numero praedestinatorum: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p119","Canon XVI","If anyone says that he will certainly have that great gift of perseverance to the end with an absolute and infallible certainty, unless he has learned this from special revelation: let him be anathema.","Si quis magnum illud usque in finem perseverantiae donum se certo habiturum absoluta et infallibili certitudine dixerit, nisi hoc ex speciali revelatione didicerit: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p120","Canon XVII","If anyone says that the grace of justification falls only to those who are predestined to life, but that all others who are called are called indeed but do not receive grace, as being by divine power predestined to evil: let him be anathema.","Si quis iustificationis gratiam non nisi praedestinatis ad vitam contingere dixerit, reliquos vero omnes qui vocantur vocari quidem sed gratiam non accipere, utpote divina potestate praedestinatos ad malum: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p121","Canon XVIII","If anyone says that the commandments of God are impossible for a man who is justified and constituted under grace to observe: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit dei praecepta homini etiam iustificato et sub gratia constituto esse ad observandum impossibilia: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p122","Canon XIX","If anyone says that nothing is commanded in the gospel beyond faith, that the rest are indifferent, neither commanded nor prohibited but free, or that the ten commandments pertain nothing to Christians: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit nihil praeceptum esse in evangelio praeter fidem, cetera esse indifferentia, neque praecepta neque prohibita, sed libera, aut decem praecepta nihil pertinere ad christianos: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p123","Canon XX","If anyone says that a man who is justified and however perfect is not bound to observe the commandments of God and of the church, but only to believe, as if the gospel were a bare and absolute promise of eternal life without the condition of observing the commandments: let him be anathema.","Si quis hominem iustificatum et quantumlibet perfectum dixerit non teneri ad observantiam mandatorum dei et ecclesiae, sed tantum ad credendum, quasi vero evangelium sit nuda et absoluta promissio vitae aeternae sine conditione observationis mandatorum: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p124","Canon XXI","If anyone says that Christ Jesus was given by God to men as a redeemer in whom they are to trust, not also as a legislator whom they are to obey: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit christum Iesum a deo hominibus datum fuisse ut redemptorem cui fidant, non etiam ut legislatorem cui obediant: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p125","Canon XXII","If anyone says that the justified man can persevere in the received justice without the special help of God, or that with that help he cannot: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit iustificatum vel sine speciali auxilio dei in accepta iustitia perseverare posse, vel cum eo non posse: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p126","Canon XXIII","If anyone says that a man once justified can sin no more nor lose grace, and therefore that he who falls and sins was never truly justified; or, on the contrary, that he can in his whole life avoid all sins, even venial ones, except by a special privilege of God, as the church holds concerning the blessed Virgin: let him be anathema.","Si quis hominem semel iustificatum dixerit amplius peccare non posse neque gratiam amittere, atque ideo eum qui labitur et peccat numquam vere fuisse iustificatum; aut contra posse in tota vita peccata omnia, etiam venialia, vitare, nisi ex speciali dei privilegio, quemadmodum de beata virgine tenet ecclesia: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p127","Canon XXIV","If anyone says that the received justice is not preserved and also not increased before God through good works, but that those works are only the fruits and signs of justification obtained, not the cause of its increase: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit iustitiam acceptam non conservari atque etiam non augeri coram deo per bona opera, sed opera ipsa fructus solummodo et signa esse iustificationis adeptae, non etiam ipsius augendae causam: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p128","Canon XXV","If anyone says that in every good work the just man sins at least venially, or (what is more intolerable) mortally, and therefore merits eternal punishments, and that he is not damned for this reason only because God does not impute those works to damnation: let him be anathema.","Si quis in quolibet bono opere iustum saltem venialiter peccare dixerit, aut (quod intolerabilius est) mortaliter, atque ideo poenas aeternas mereri, tantum que ob id non damnari, quia deus ea opera non imputet ad damnationem: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p129","Canon XXVI","If anyone says that the just ought not to expect and hope from God through His mercy and the merit of Jesus Christ an eternal retribution for their good works which have been done in God, if by doing well and keeping the divine commandments they have persevered to the end: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit iustos non debere pro bonis operibus quae in deo fuerint facta exspectare et sperare aeternam retributionem a deo per eius misericordiam et Iesu christi meritum, si bene agendo et divina mandata custodiendo usque in finem perseveraverint: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p130","Canon XXVII","If anyone says that there is no mortal sin except that of unbelief, or that by no other sin, however grave and enormous, except that of unbelief, is the once received grace lost: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit nullum esse mortale peccatum nisi infidelitatis, aut nullo alio quantumvis gravi et enormi, praeterquam infidelitatis peccato, semel acceptam gratiam amitti: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p131","Canon XXVIII","If anyone says that when grace is lost by sin, faith is also always lost, or that the faith which remains is not true faith, although it is not living, or that he who has faith without charity is not a Christian: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit amissa per peccatum gratia simul et fidem semper amitti, aut fidem quae remanet non esse veram fidem, licet non sit viva, aut eum qui fidem sine charitate habet non esse christianum: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p132","Canon XXIX","If anyone says that he who has fallen after baptism cannot rise again by the grace of God, or that he can indeed rise but recover lost justice by faith alone without the sacrament of penance, as the holy Roman and universal church, taught by Christ the Lord and His apostles, has hitherto professed, observed, and taught: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit eum qui post baptismum lapsus est non posse per dei gratiam resurgere, aut posse quidem sed sola fide amissam iustitiam recuperare sine sacramento poenitentiae, prout sancta Romana et universalis ecclesia a christo domino et eius apostolis edocta hucusque professa est, servavit et docuit: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p133","Canon XXX","If anyone says that after the grace of justification has been received, to any penitent sinner guilt is so remitted and the guilt of eternal punishment so blotted out that no guilt of temporal punishment remains to be paid, either in this world or in the future in purgatory, before the entrance to the kingdom of heaven can be opened: let him be anathema.","Si quis post acceptam iustificationis gratiam cuilibet peccatori poenitenti ita culpam remitti et reatum aeternae poenae deleri dixerit, ut nullus remaneat reatus poenae temporalis exsolvendae vel in hoc saeculo vel in futuro in purgatorio, antequam ad regna coelorum aditus patere possit: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p134","Canon XXXI","If anyone says that the justified man sins when he does good works with a view to eternal reward: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit iustificatum peccare, dum intuitu aeternae mercedis bene operatur: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p135","Canon XXXII","If anyone says that the good works of a justified man are so the gifts of God that they are not also the good merits of the justified man himself, or that the justified man by the good works which are done by him through the grace of God and the merit of Jesus Christ, of whom he is a living member, does not truly merit an increase of grace, eternal life, and the attainment of eternal life itself (provided he has died in grace), and also an increase of glory: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit hominis iustificati bona opera ita esse dona dei, ut non sint etiam bona ipsius iustificati merita, aut ipsum iustificatum bonis operibus, quae ab eo per dei gratiam et Iesu christi meritum (cuius vivum membrum est) fiunt, non vere mereri augmentum gratiae, vitam aeternam et ipsius vitae aeternae (si tamen in gratia decesserit) consecutionem, atque etiam gloriae augmentum: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p136","Canon XXXIII","If anyone says that by this catholic doctrine concerning justification, expressed by this holy synod in this present decree, any part of the glory of God or of the merits of our Lord Jesus Christ is derogated from, and not rather that the truth of our faith, and the glory of God and of Christ Jesus, is illustrated: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit per hanc doctrinam catholicam de iustificatione, a sancta synodo hoc praesenti decreto expressam, aliqua ex parte gloriae dei vel meritis Iesu christi domini nostri derogari, et non potius veritatem fidei nostrae, dei denique ac christi Iesu gloriam illustrari: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p137","Decree Concerning the Reformation","The same sacred synod, the same legates of the Apostolic See presiding, wishing to apply itself to restoring the ecclesiastical discipline which has very much collapsed, and to amending the depraved morals in the clergy and the Christian people, has judged that a beginning is to be made from those who preside over the greater churches. For the integrity of those presiding is the salvation of subjects.","Eadem sacrosancta synodus, eisdem praesidentibus et apostolicae sedis legatis, ad restituendam collapsam admodum ecclesiasticam disciplinam depravatos que in clero et populo christiano mores emendandos se accingere volens, ab iis qui maioribus ecclesiis praesunt initium censuit esse sumendum. Integritas enim praesidentium salus est subditorum."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p138","Decree Concerning the Reformation","Trusting therefore that through the mercy of our Lord and God and the provident diligence of His vicar on earth, it will altogether come to pass that, according to the venerable sanctions of the blessed fathers, those who are most worthy shall be assumed to the governance of churches (a burden indeed to be feared by angelic shoulders), whose prior life and all age from childish beginnings even to more perfect years, having been laudably spent through the disciplines of ecclesiastical training, may provide testimony: it warns all who are placed over patriarchal, primatial, metropolitan, and cathedral churches, under whatsoever name and title, and wishes them to be warned, that, attending to themselves and to the whole flock in which the Holy Spirit has placed them to rule the church of God which He has purchased with His blood, they watch, as the apostle commands, in all things, labor, and fulfill their ministry.","Confidens itaque per domini ac dei nostri misericordiam providam que ipsius dei in terris vicarii sollertiam omnino futurum ut ad ecclesiarum regimen (onus quippe angelicis humeris formidandum) qui maxime digni fuerint, quorum que prior vita ac omnis aetas a puerilibus exordiis usque ad perfectiores annos per disciplinae stipendia ecclesiasticae laudabiliter acta testimonium praebeat, secundum venerabiles beatorum patrum sanctiones assumantur, omnes patriarchalibus, primatialibus, metropolitanis et cathedralibus ecclesiis quibuscunque quovis nomine ac titulo praefectos monet ac monitos esse vult, ut attendentes sibi et universo gregi, in quo spiritus sanctus posuit eos regere ecclesiam dei, quam acquisivit sanguine suo, vigilent, sicut apostolus praecipit, in omnibus laborent et ministerium suum impleant."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p139","Decree Concerning the Reformation","But they know that they cannot fulfill it if, after the manner of hirelings, they desert the flocks entrusted to them and do not attend to the keeping of their sheep, whose blood will be required from their hands by the supreme Judge, since it is most certain that the excuse of the shepherd is not admitted if the wolf eats the sheep and the shepherd knows it not. And nevertheless, because some (which is greatly to be deplored) are found at this time who, unmindful even of their own salvation and preferring earthly things to heavenly and human to divine, wander about in various courts or keep themselves occupied in the solicitude of temporal affairs, the sheepfold having been abandoned and the care of the sheep entrusted to them having been neglected, it has pleased the sacred synod to renew the ancient canons (which have almost fallen into disuse by the injury of times and men) promulgated against non-residents, just as by the force of the present decree it renews them, and further for the stronger security of their residence and for reforming morals in the church, to establish and decree in the following manner.","Implere autem illud se nequaquam posse sciant, si greges sibi commissos mercenariorum more deserant atque ovium suarum, quarum sanguis de eorum est manibus a supremo iudice requirendus, custodiae minime incumbant, cum certissimum sit non admitti pastoris excusationem, si lupus oves comedit et pastor nescit. Ac nihilominus, quia nonnulli (quod vehementer dolendum est) hoc tempore reperiuntur qui propriae etiam salutis immemores, terrena que coelestibus ac divinis humana praeferentes, in diversis curiis vagantur aut in negotiorum temporalium sollicitudine (ovili derelicto atque ovium sibi commissarum cura neglecta) se detinent occupatos, placuit sacrosanctae synodo antiquos canones (qui temporum atque hominum iniuria paene in dissuetudinem abierunt) adversus non residentes promulgatos innovare, quemadmodum virtute praesentis decreti innovat, ac ulterius pro firmiori eorundem residentia et reformandis in ecclesia moribus in hunc qui sequitur modum statuere atque sancire."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p140","Decree Concerning the Reformation","If anyone, from a patriarchal, primatial, metropolitan, or cathedral church committed to him under any title, cause, name, or right, with whatever dignity, grade, and preeminence he may shine, lacking legitimate impediment or just and reasonable causes, shall have been absent by remaining outside his diocese for six continuous months, let him by the law itself incur the penalty of a fourth part of the fruits of one year, to be applied to the fabric of the church and to the poor of the place by the ecclesiastical superior.","Si quis a patriarchali, primatiali, metropolitana seu cathedrali ecclesia sibi quocumque titulo, causa, nomine seu iure commissa, quacumque ille dignitate, gradu et praeeminentia praefulgeat, legitimo impedimento seu iustis et rationabilibus causis cessantibus, sex mensibus continuis extra suam dioecesim morando abfuerit, quartae partis fructuum unius anni fabricae ecclesiae et pauperibus loci per superiorem ecclesiasticum applicandorum poenam ipso iure incurrat."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p141","Decree Concerning the Reformation","But if he shall have persisted in such absence for another six months, let him lose another fourth part of the fruits, likewise to be applied.","Quod si per alios sex menses in huiusmodi absentia perseveraverit, aliam quartam partem fructuum similiter applicandam eo ipso amittat."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p142","Decree Concerning the Reformation","Contumacy increasing, that he may be subjected to the severer censure of the sacred canons, the metropolitan shall be bound to denounce to the Roman pontiff absent suffragan bishops, and the senior resident suffragan bishop an absent metropolitan, under the penalty of interdict of entrance into the church to be incurred by that very act, within three months by letter or nuncio; who, by the authority of his supreme see, may animadvert against the absent persons as the greater or lesser contumacy of each shall have demanded, and may provide the churches themselves with more useful pastors, as he shall know to be salutarily expedient in the Lord.","Crescente vero contumacia, ut severiori sacrorum canonum censurae subiiciatur, metropolitanus suffraganeos episcopos absentes, metropolitanum vero absentem suffraganeus episcopus antiquior residens sub poena interdicti ingressus ecclesiae eo ipso incurrenda infra tres menses per litteras seu nuntium Romano pontifici denuntiare teneatur, qui in ipsos absentes, prout cuiusque maior aut minor contumacia exegerit, suae supremae sedis auctoritate animadvertere et ecclesiis ipsis de pastoribus utilioribus providere poterit, sicut in domino noverit salubriter expedire."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p143","Session 7 — 3 March 1547","For the completion of the salutary doctrine concerning justification, which was promulgated in the preceding session with the unanimous consent of all the fathers, it has seemed fitting to treat of the most holy sacraments of the church, through which all true justice either begins, or, having been begun, increases, or, having been lost, is repaired.","Ad consummationem salutaris de iustificatione doctrinae, quae in praecedenti proxima sessione uno omnium patrum consensu promulgata fuit, consentaneum visum est de sanctissimis ecclesiae sacramentis agere, per quae omnis vera iustitia vel incipit, vel coepta augetur, vel amissa reparatur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p144","Session 7 — 3 March 1547","Therefore the sacred, oecumenical, and general Council of Trent, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same legates of the Apostolic See presiding therein, for the purpose of eliminating errors and extirpating heresies which concerning these most holy sacraments, both those raised up in this our age from heresies formerly condemned by our fathers and those newly invented, greatly oppose the purity of the catholic church and the salvation of souls, following the doctrine of the holy scriptures, the apostolic traditions, and the consent of other councils and fathers, has judged that these present canons are to be established and decreed, about to publish the remaining ones which are left for the perfection of the work begun, with the help of the divine Spirit.","Propterea sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis Tridentina synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, praesidentibus in ea eisdem apostolicae sedis legatis, ad errores eliminandos et exstirpandas haereses quae circa ipsa sanctissima sacramenta hac nostra tempestate, tum de damnatis olim a patribus nostris haeresibus suscitatae, tum etiam de novo adinventae sunt, quae catholicae ecclesiae puritati et animarum saluti magnopere officiunt, sanctarum scripturarum doctrinae, apostolicis traditionibus atque aliorum conciliorum et patrum consensui inhaerendo, hos praesentes canones statuendos et decernendos censuit, reliquos qui supersunt ad coepti operis perfectionem deinceps (divino spiritu adiuvante) editura."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p145","Canons on the Sacraments in General","If anyone says that the sacraments of the new law were not all instituted by our Lord Jesus Christ, or that there are more or fewer than seven, namely, baptism, confirmation, eucharist, penance, extreme unction, order, and matrimony, or that any one of these seven is not truly and properly a sacrament: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit sacramenta novae legis non fuisse omnia a Iesu christo domino nostro instituta, aut esse plura vel pauciora quam septem, videlicet baptismum, confirmationem, eucharistiam, poenitentiam, extremam unctionem, ordinem et matrimonium, aut etiam aliquod horum septem non esse vere et proprie sacramentum: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p146","Canon II","If anyone says that these same sacraments of the new law differ from the sacraments of the old law only because the ceremonies are different and the external rites are different: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit ea ipsa novae legis sacramenta a sacramentis antiquae legis non differre, nisi quia caeremoniae sunt aliae et alii ritus externi: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p147","Canon III","If anyone says that these seven sacraments are so equal among themselves that one is not in any way more worthy than another: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit haec septem sacramenta ita esse inter se paria, ut nulla ratione aliud sit alio dignius: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p148","Canon IV","If anyone says that the sacraments of the new law are not necessary for salvation, but are superfluous, and that without them or without the desire of them men obtain from God the grace of justification through faith alone, although all are not necessary for each individual: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit sacramenta novae legis non esse ad salutem necessaria, sed superflua, et sine eis aut eorum voto per solam fidem homines a deo gratiam iustificationis adipisci, licet omnia singulis necessaria non sint: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p149","Canon V","If anyone says that these sacraments were instituted for the sake of nourishing faith alone: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit haec sacramenta propter solam fidem nutriendam instituta fuisse: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p150","Canon VI","If anyone says that the sacraments of the new law do not contain the grace which they signify, or that they do not confer that grace itself on those who do not place an obstacle, as if they are only external signs of grace or justice received through faith, and certain marks of Christian profession by which the faithful are distinguished from unbelievers among men: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit sacramenta novae legis non continere gratiam quam significant, aut gratiam ipsam non ponentibus obicem non conferre, quasi signa tantum externa sint acceptae per fidem gratiae vel iustitiae, et notae quaedam christianae professionis, quibus apud homines discernuntur fideles ab infidelibus: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p151","Canon VII","If anyone says that grace is not given through such sacraments always and to all, as far as it is from the side of God, even if they receive them rightly, but sometimes and to some: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit non dari gratiam per huiusmodi sacramenta semper et omnibus, quantum est ex parte dei, etiam si rite ea suscipiant, sed aliquando et aliquibus: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p152","Canon VIII","If anyone says that through the sacraments of the new law grace is not conferred from the work performed, but that faith alone in the divine promise suffices to obtain grace: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit per ipsa novae legis sacramenta ex opere operato non conferri gratiam, sed solam fidem divinae promissionis ad gratiam consequendam sufficere: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p153","Canon IX","If anyone says that in the three sacraments, namely, baptism, confirmation, and order, a character is not imprinted on the soul, that is, a certain spiritual and indelible mark, on account of which they cannot be repeated: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit in tribus sacramentis, baptismo scilicet, confirmatione et ordine, non imprimi characterem in anima, hoc est signum quoddam spirituale et indelebile, unde ea iterari non possunt: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p154","Canon X","If anyone says that all Christians have power to administer the word and all the sacraments: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit christianos omnes in verbo et omnibus sacramentis administrandis habere potestatem: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p155","Canon XI","If anyone says that in ministers, when they confect and confer the sacraments, the intention of at least doing what the church does is not required: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit in ministris, dum sacramenta conficiunt et conferunt, non requiri intentionem saltem faciendi quod facit ecclesia: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p156","Canon XII","If anyone says that a minister existing in mortal sin, provided he has observed all the essential things which pertain to the confecting or conferring of the sacrament, does not confect or confer the sacrament: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit ministrum in peccato mortali exsistentem, modo omnia essentialia quae ad sacramentum conficiendum aut conferendum pertinent servaverit, non conficere aut conferre sacramentum: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p157","Canon XIII","If anyone says that the received and approved rites of the catholic church, accustomed to be used in the solemn administration of the sacraments, may be despised or omitted without sin by the ministers at their pleasure, or may be changed into new ones by any pastor of the church: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit receptos et approbatos ecclesiae catholicae ritus in solemni sacramentorum administratione adhiberi consuetos aut contemni aut sine peccato a ministris pro libito omitti, aut in novos alios per quemcumque ecclesiarum pastorem mutari posse: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p158","Canon XIV — on Baptism","If anyone says that the baptism of John had the same power as the baptism of Christ: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit baptismum Ioannis habuisse eandem vim cum baptismo christi: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p159","Canon XV","If anyone says that true and natural water is not necessary for baptism, and therefore twists the words of our Lord Jesus Christ: \"Unless a man be born again of water and the Holy Spirit,\" to some metaphor: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit aquam veram et naturalem non esse de necessitate baptismi, atque ideo verba illa domini nostri Iesu christi: \"nisi quis renatus fuerit ex aqua et spiritu sancto,\" ad metaphoram aliquam detorserit: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p160","Canon XVI","If anyone says that in the Roman church, which is the mother and teacher of all churches, there is not true doctrine concerning the sacrament of baptism: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit in ecclesia Romana (quae omnium ecclesiarum mater est et magistra) non esse veram de baptismi sacramento doctrinam: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p161","Canon XVII","If anyone says that baptism which is given even by heretics in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, with the intention of doing what the church does, is not true baptism: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit baptismum, qui etiam datur ab haereticis in nomine patris et filii et spiritus sancti, eum intentione faciendi quod facit ecclesia, non esse verum baptismum: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p162","Canon XVIII","If anyone says that baptism is free, that is, not necessary for salvation: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit baptismum liberum esse, hoc est non necessarium ad salutem: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p163","Canon XIX","If anyone says that a baptized person cannot, even if he wishes, lose grace, however much he sins, unless he is unwilling to believe: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit baptizatum non posse, etiam si velit, gratiam amittere, quantumcumque peccet, nisi nolit credere: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p164","Canon XX","If anyone says that the baptized are made debtors by baptism itself only of faith, but not of the whole law of Christ to be kept: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit baptizatos per baptismum ipsum solius tantum fidei debitores fieri, non autem universae legis christi servandae: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p165","Canon XXI","If anyone says that the baptized are free from all the precepts of the holy church, whether written or handed down, so that they are not bound to observe them, unless they have willed to subject themselves to them of their own accord: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit baptizatos liberos esse ab omnibus sanctae ecclesiae praeceptis, quae vel scripta vel tradita sunt, ita ut ea observare non teneantur, nisi se sua sponte illis submittere voluerint: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p166","Canon XXII","If anyone says that men are so to be recalled to the memory of baptism received that they understand that all vows which are made after baptism are void by virtue of the promise already made in baptism itself, as if by them both the faith which they have professed is derogated from and the baptism itself: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit ita revocandos esse homines ad baptismi suscepti memoriam, ut vota omnia quae post baptismum fiunt vi promissionis in baptismo ipso iam factae irrita esse intelligant, quasi per ea et fidei quam professi sunt detrahatur et ipsi baptismo: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p167","Canon XXIII","If anyone says that all sins which are committed after baptism are either remitted or made venial by the sole remembrance and faith of the baptism received: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit peccata omnia quae post baptismum fiunt sola recordatione et fide suscepti baptismi vel dimitti vel venialia fieri: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p168","Canon XXIV","If anyone says that true and rightly conferred baptism is to be repeated for him who has denied the faith of Christ among unbelievers, when he is converted to penance: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit verum et rite collatum baptismum iterandum esse illi qui apud infideles fidem christi negaverit, cum ad poenitentiam convertitur: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p169","Canon XXV","If anyone says that no one is to be baptized except at that age at which Christ was baptized, or in the very article of death: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit neminem esse baptizandum nisi ea aetate qua christus baptizatus est, vel in ipso mortis articulo: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p170","Canon XXVI","If anyone says that infants, because they have not the act of believing, are not to be counted among the faithful after having received baptism, and therefore are to be rebaptized when they have reached the years of discretion, or that it is better to omit their baptism than that they, not believing with their own act, should be baptized in the faith alone of the church: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit parvulos, eo quod actum credendi non habent, suscepto baptismo inter fideles computandos non esse, ac propterea cum ad annos discretionis pervenerint esse rebaptizandos, aut praestare omitti eorum baptisma quam eos non actu proprio credentes baptizari in sola fide ecclesiae: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p171","Canon XXVII","If anyone says that infants who have been baptized, when they have grown up, are to be asked whether they wish to ratify what their sponsors promised in their name when they were baptized, and that when they have answered that they are unwilling, they are to be left to their own choice, and in the meantime not to be compelled to a Christian life by any other penalty, except that they be barred from the reception of the Eucharist and of the other sacraments until they come to their senses: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit huiusmodi parvulos baptizatos, cum adoleverint, interrogandos esse an ratum habere velint quod patrini eorum nomine dum baptizarentur polliciti sunt, et ubi se nolle responderint, suo esse arbitrio relinquendos, nec alia interim poena ad christianam vitam cogendos, nisi ut ab eucharistiae aliorum que sacramentorum perceptione arceantur, donec resipiscant: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p172","Canons on Confirmation","If anyone says that confirmation of the baptized is an idle ceremony, and not rather a true and proper sacrament, or that it was formerly nothing more than a kind of catechesis by which those approaching adolescence gave an account of their faith before the church: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit confirmationem baptizatorum otiosam caeremoniam esse, et non potius verum et proprium sacramentum, aut olim nihil aliud fuisse quam catechesim quandam, qua adolescentiae proximi fidei suae rationem coram ecclesia exponebant: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p173","Canon II","If anyone says that those who attribute some virtue to the sacred chrism of confirmation are injurious to the Holy Spirit: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit iniurios esse spiritui sancto eos qui sacro confirmationis chrismati virtutem aliquam tribuunt: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p174","Canon III","If anyone says that the ordinary minister of holy confirmation is not the bishop alone, but any simple priest: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit sanctae confirmationis ordinarium ministrum non esse solum episcopum, sed quemvis simplicem sacerdotem: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p175","Decree on the Reformation","The same sacred synod, the same legates of the Apostolic See presiding, intending to pursue the matter of reformation which has been begun for the praise of God and the increase of the Christian religion, has judged that it is to be established as follows, the authority of the Apostolic See always being preserved in all things.","Eadem sacrosancta synodus, eisdem praesidentibus et legatis, inceptum residentiae et reformationis negotium ad dei laudem et christianae religionis incrementum prosequi intendens, ut sequitur statuendum censuit, salva semper in omnibus sedis apostolicae auctoritate."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p176","Decree on the Reformation","For the governance of cathedral churches, let no one be assumed except one born of legitimate marriage, of mature age, gravity of morals, and knowledge of letters, according to the constitution of Alexander III which begins Cum in cunctis, promulgated in the Lateran council.","Ad cathedralium ecclesiarum regimen nullus nisi ex legitimo matrimonio natus et aetate matura, gravitate morum, litterarum que scientia iuxta constitutionem Alexandri III quae incipit \"Cum in cunctis\" in concilio Lateranensi promulgatam praeditus assumatur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p177","Decree on the Reformation","Let no one, of whatever dignity, grade, or preeminence he may shine, presume to receive and simultaneously retain several metropolitan or cathedral churches in title or commendam or under any other name, against the institutes of the sacred canons, since he is to be considered very happy to whom it falls to govern one church well and fruitfully and with the salvation of the souls entrusted to him.","Nemo quacumque etiam dignitate, gradu aut praeeminentia praefulgens, plures metropolitanas seu cathedrales ecclesias in titulum sive commendam aut alio quovis nomine contra sacrorum canonum instituta recipere et simul retinere praesumat, cum valde felix sit ille censendus cui unam ecclesiam bene ac fructuose et cum animarum sibi commissarum salute regere contigerit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p178","Decree on the Reformation","Those who now hold several churches against the tenor of this present decree, having retained one which they prefer, shall be bound to relinquish the others within six months if they pertain to the free disposition of the Apostolic See, otherwise within a year; otherwise, the churches themselves (the last obtained only excepted) shall by that very fact be considered vacant.","Qui autem plures ecclesias contra praesentis decreti tenorem nunc detinent, una quam maluerint retenta, reliquas infra sex menses si ad liberam sedis apostolicae dispositionem pertineant, alias infra annum, dimittere teneantur; alioquin ecclesiae ipsae (ultimo obtenta dumtaxat excepta) eo ipso vacare censeantur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p179","Decree on the Reformation","Inferior ecclesiastical benefices, especially those having the cure of souls, shall be conferred upon worthy and suitable persons who are able to reside in the place and to exercise the cure themselves, according to the constitution of Alexander III in the Lateran council which begins Quia nonnulli, and another of Gregory X in the general council of Lyons which begins Licet canon.","Inferiora beneficia ecclesiastica, praesertim curam animarum habentia, personis dignis et habilibus, et quae in loco residere ac per se ipsos curam ipsam exercere valeant, iuxta constitutionem Alexandri III in Lateranensi quae incipit \"Quia nonnulli\" et aliam Gregorii X in generali Lugdunensi concilio quae incipit \"Licet canon\" editam conferantur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p180","Decree on the Reformation","Otherwise, a collation or provision made shall be altogether null, and the ordinary collator shall know that he incurs the penalties of the constitution of the general council which begins Grave nimis.","Aliter autem facta collatio sive provisio omnino irritetur, et ordinarius collator poenas constitutionis concilii generalis quae incipit \"Grave nimis\" se noverit incursurum."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p181","Decree on the Reformation","Whoever henceforth shall have presumed to receive and simultaneously retain several benefices, whether with cure or otherwise incompatible, by way of union for life or perpetual commendam or under any other name and title, against the form of the sacred canons and especially of the constitution of Innocent III which begins De multa, shall by that very fact be deprived of those benefices, also by the force of this present canon.","Quicumque de cetero plura curata aut alias incompatibilia beneficia ecclesiastica sive per viam unionis ad vitam seu commendae perpetuae aut alio quocumque nomine et titulo contra formam sacrorum canonum et praesertim constitutionis Innocentii III quae incipit \"De multa\" recipere ac simul retinere praesumpserit, beneficiis ipsis iuxta ipsius constitutionis dispositionem ipso iure, etiam praesentis canonis vigore, privatus exsistat."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p182","Decree on the Reformation","The ordinaries of places shall strictly compel those obtaining several benefices, whether with cure or otherwise incompatible, to exhibit their dispensations, and otherwise proceed according to the constitution of Gregory X in the general council of Lyons, which begins Ordinarii, which the same holy synod judges to be renewed and renews, adding moreover that the ordinaries themselves, by the deputation of suitable vicars and the assignment of a congruent portion of fruits, shall altogether provide that the care of souls be by no means neglected and that the benefices themselves be not defrauded of the due services, appeals, privileges, exemptions whatsoever, even with the deputation of special judges and their inhibitions, availing no one in the aforesaid matters.","Ordinarii locorum quoscumque plura curata aut alias incompatibilia beneficia ecclesiastica obtinentes dispensationes suas exhibere districte compellant, et alias procedant iuxta constitutionem Gregorii X in generali Lugdunensi concilio editam quae incipit \"Ordinarii,\" quam eadem sancta synodus innovandam censet et innovat, addens insuper quod ipsi ordinarii etiam per idoneorum vicariorum deputationem et congruae portionis fructuum assignationem omnino provideant, ut animarum cura nullatenus negligatur et beneficia ipsa debitis obsequiis minime defraudentur; appellationibus, privilegiis, exemptionibus quibuscumque, etiam cum iudicum specialium deputatione et illorum inhibitionibus, in praemissis nemini suffragantibus."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p183","Decree on the Reformation","Perpetual unions made within the last forty years may be examined by ordinaries as delegates of the Apostolic See, and those which have been obtained by subreption or obreption shall be declared void; but those which have been granted within the said time and have not yet obtained effect in whole or in part, and those which shall be made henceforth at the instance of anyone, unless it shall be established that they were made from legitimate or otherwise reasonable causes, the parties concerned having been summoned and the facts verified before the ordinary of the place, shall be presumed to have been obtained by subreption, and therefore (unless otherwise declared by the Apostolic See) shall altogether lack force.","Uniones perpetuae a quadraginta annis citra factae examinari ab ordinariis tamquam a sede apostolica delegatis possint, et quae per subreptionem vel obreptionem obtentae fuerint irritae declarentur; illae vero quae a dicto tempore citra concessae nondum in toto vel in parte sortitae sunt effectum, et quae deinceps ad cuiusvis instantiam fient, nisi eas ex legitimis aut alias rationabilibus causis, vocatis quorum interest, coram loci ordinario verificandis factas fuisse constiterit, per subreptionem obtentae praesumantur, ac propterea (nisi aliter a sede apostolica declaratum fuerit) viribus omnino careant."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p184","Decree on the Reformation","Benefices with cure which are found to be perpetually united and annexed to cathedral, collegiate, or other churches or to monasteries, benefices, colleges, or pious places whatsoever, shall be visited each year by the ordinaries of the places, who shall take care to provide that the cure of souls be laudably exercised through suitable vicars (even perpetual, unless it shall otherwise seem expedient to the ordinaries themselves for the good governance of the churches) to be appointed there by them, with a third part of the fruits, or a greater or lesser portion at the discretion of the ordinaries, appeals, privileges, exemptions, even with the deputation of judges and their inhibitions, in no way availing in the aforesaid matters.","Beneficia ecclesiastica curata quae cathedralibus, collegiatis seu aliis ecclesiis vel monasteriis, beneficiis seu collegiis aut piis locis quibuscumque perpetuo unita et annexa reperiuntur, ab ordinariis locorum annis singulis visitentur, qui sollicite providere procurent, ut per idoneos vicarios (etiam perpetuos, nisi ipsis ordinariis pro bono ecclesiarum regimine aliter expedire videbitur) ab eis cum tertiae partis fructuum, aut maiori vel minori arbitrio ipsorum ordinariorum portione, etiam super re certa assignanda, ibidem deputandos, animarum cura laudabiliter exerceatur; appellationibus, privilegiis, exemptionibus, etiam cum iudicum deputatione et illorum inhibitionibus quibuscumque, in praemissis minime suffragantibus."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p185","Decree on the Reformation","The ordinaries of places shall be bound to visit any churches whatsoever, however exempt, by apostolic authority each year, and by opportune remedies of law to provide that those things which need repair be repaired, and that the cure of souls, if any is incumbent upon them, and other due services be not defrauded, appeals, privileges, customs even prescribed from immemorial time, deputations of judges, and their inhibitions, being altogether excluded.","Locorum ordinarii ecclesias quascumque quomodolibet exemptas auctoritate apostolica singulis annis visitare teneantur et opportunis iuris remediis providere, ut quae reparatione indigent reparentur, et cura animarum, si qua illis immineat, aliis que debitis obsequiis minime defraudentur; appellationibus, privilegiis, consuetudinibus etiam ab immemorabili tempore praescriptis, iudicum deputationibus et illorum inhibitionibus penitus exclusis."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p186","Decree on the Reformation","Those promoted to greater churches shall receive the gift of consecration within the time established by law, and prorogations granted beyond six months shall avail no one.","Ad maiores ecclesias promoti munus consecrationis infra tempus a iure statutum suscipiant, et prorogationes ultra sex menses concessae nulli suffragentur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p187","Decree on the Reformation","It shall not be lawful for chapters of churches, the see being vacant, within a year from the day of vacation, to grant the license or dimissory letters to be ordained (or as some call them, reverendas) to anyone who has not been constrained by reason of an ecclesiastical benefice received or to be received, by virtue both of the common law and of any privilege or custom whatsoever; if it shall have been done otherwise, the chapter acting contrary shall be subject to ecclesiastical interdict, and those thus ordained, if they have been constituted in minor orders, shall enjoy no clerical privilege, especially in criminal matters; in major orders, however, they shall by the law itself be suspended from the execution of orders at the pleasure of the future prelate.","Non liceat capitulis ecclesiarum sede vacante infra annum a die vacationis ordinandi licentiam aut litteras dimissorias seu reverendas (ut aliqui vocant) tam ex iuris communis dispositione quam etiam cuiusvis privilegii aut consuetudinis vigore alicui qui beneficii ecclesiastici recepti sive recipiendi occasione arctatus non fuerit concedere; si secus fiat, capitulum contraveniens ecclesiastico subiaceat interdicto, et sic ordinati si in minoribus ordinibus constituti fuerint, nullo privilegio clericali praesertim in criminalibus gaudeant; in maioribus vero, ab executione ordinum ad beneplacitum futuri praelati sint ipso iure suspensi."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p188","Decree on the Reformation","Faculties of ordaining, granted to anyone, shall not avail unless those having legitimate cause, on account of which they cannot be ordained by their own bishops, are expressed in the letters; and then let them be ordained only by a bishop residing in his diocese or exercising pontificals for him, and after a diligent prior examination.","Facultates de promovendo a quocumque non suffragentur, nisi habentibus legitimam causam, ob quam a propriis episcopis ordinari non possint, in litteris exprimendam, et tunc non ordinentur nisi ab episcopo in sua dioecesi residente aut pro eo pontificalia exercente, et diligenti praevio examine."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p189","Decree on the Reformation","Faculties of not ordaining, granted except in cases expressed by law, shall avail only for one year.","Facultates de non promovendo, praeterquam in casibus a iure expressis concessae, ad annum tantum suffragentur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p190","Decree on the Reformation","Those presented or elected or nominated by any ecclesiastical persons, even by nuncios of the Apostolic See, to any ecclesiastical benefices, shall not be instituted nor confirmed nor admitted, even under pretext of any privilege or custom even prescribed from immemorial time, unless they have first been examined by the ordinaries of the places and found suitable; and let no one be able to protect himself by the remedy of appeal so as not to be bound to undergo examination.","Praesentati seu electi vel nominati a quibusvis ecclesiasticis personis etiam sedis apostolicae nuntiis ad quaevis ecclesiastica beneficia non instituantur nec confirmentur neque admittantur etiam praetextu cuiusvis privilegii seu consuetudinis etiam ab immemorabili tempore praescriptae, nisi fuerint prius a locorum ordinariis examinati et idonei reperti; et nullus appellationis remedio se tueri possit quominus examen subire teneatur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p191","Decree on the Reformation","Those presented, elected, or nominated by universities or colleges of general studies excepted.","Praesentatis tamen electis seu nominatis ab universitatibus seu collegiis generalium studiorum exceptis."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p192","Decree on the Reformation","In the causes of exempt persons, the constitution of Innocent IV which begins Volentes, issued in the general council of Lyons, shall be observed, which the same sacred synod judged to be renewed and renews, adding moreover that in civil causes of salaries and of miserable persons, secular or regular clerics living outside a monastery, however exempt, even if they have a certain judge deputed by the Apostolic See in those parts, but if they do not have that judge, may be sued before the ordinaries of the places, as in this matter delegated by that same see, and may be compelled and forced to pay the debt by ordinary law; privileges, exemptions, deputations of conservators, and their inhibitions not availing against the aforesaid.","In exemptorum causis constitutio Innocentii IV quae incipit \"Volentes\" in generali Lugdunensi concilio edita servetur, quam eadem sacrosancta synodus innovandam censuit et innovat; addendo insuper quod in civilibus causis mercedum et miserabilium personarum, clerici saeculares aut regulares extra monasterium degentes, quomodolibet exempti, etiam si certum iudicem a sede apostolica deputatum in partibus habeant, in aliis vero si ipsum iudicem non habuerint, coram locorum ordinariis, tamquam in hoc ab ipsa sede delegatis, conveniri et iure medio ad solvendum debitum cogi et compelli possint; privilegiis, exemptionibus, conservatorum deputationibus et eorum inhibitionibus adversus praemissa nequaquam valituris."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p193","Decree on the Reformation","Let ordinaries take care that any hospitals whatsoever, by whatever name they are called, even those subject to anyone however exempt, be faithfully and diligently governed by their administrators according to the form of the constitution of the council of Vienne which begins Quia contingit, which constitution the same holy synod judged to be renewed and renews, with the derogations contained in it.","Curent ordinarii ut hospitalia quaecumque, a suis administratoribus quocumque illi nomine censeantur, etiam quomodolibet exemptis, fideliter et diligenter gubernentur, constitutionis concilii viennensis quae incipit \"Quia contingit\" forma servata; quam quidem constitutionem eadem sancta synodus innovandam duxit et innovat, cum derogationibus in ea contentis."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p194","Session 8 — 11 March 1547","Likewise, this sacred synod has established and decreed that the next future session is to be held and celebrated on Thursday after the following Sunday in albis, which will be 21 April of the present year 1547.","Item haec sacrosancta synodus statuit et decrevit proximam futuram sessionem habendam et celebrandam esse die iovis feria quinta post sequentem dominicam in albis, quae erit 21 mensis aprilis praesentis anni 1547."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p195","Session 8 — 11 March 1547","Most reverend and reverend fathers, does it please you to decree and declare that concerning such a disease from the aforesaid and other allegations it is so manifestly and notoriously evident that the prelates cannot remain in this city without danger of life, and therefore cannot and ought not to be retained unwillingly in it?","Illustrissimi domini, reverendissimi que patres, placet ne vobis decernere et declarare de huiusmodi morbo ex praemissis et aliis allegatis ita manifeste et notorie constare, ut praelati in hac civitate sine vitae discrimine commorari et in ea idcirco inviti minime retineri possint et debeant?"],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p196","Session 8 — 11 March 1547","Likewise, having regard to the departure of many prelates after the last session, and the protests of many others made in general congregations, hence altogether from the fear of the disease itself, wishing to depart, who cannot be justly detained, and from whose departure the council would either be dissolved or its good progress would be hindered by the small number of prelates, and having regard also to the imminent danger of life and other causes alleged by some of the fathers in those congregations, as manifestly true and legitimate: does it please you likewise to decree and declare, for the preservation and prosecution of the council itself and the safety of the life of the prelates, that the council itself is to be transferred to the city of Bologna as a more ready, healthful, and suitable place for the time, and to be transferred from now on, and that the session already appointed for the fixed day of 21 April is to be held and celebrated there, and successively to proceed to further matters until it shall seem expedient to our most holy lord and the sacred council, after consultation also with the most invincible emperor and the most Christian king and with other kings and Christian princes, that the council itself can and ought to be led back to this or another place?","Item que, attento recessu multorum praelatorum post proxime praeteritam sessionem, et attentis protestationibus aliorum quamplurium praelatorum in congregationibus generalibus factis, hinc omnino timore ipsius morbi abire volentium, qui iuste detineri non possunt, et ex quorum discessu concilium vel dissolveretur vel ex paucitate praelatorum bonus eius progressus impediretur, et attento etiam imminenti periculo vitae et aliis causis per aliquos ex patribus in ipsis congregationibus allegatis, utpote notorie veris et legitimis: placet ne vobis similiter decernere et declarare, pro conservatione et prosecutione ipsius concilii securitate vitae ipsorum praelatorum, concilium ipsum ad civitatem Bononiae, veluti ad locum magis paratum, salubrem et idoneum pro tempore, transferendum esse, et ex nunc transferri, et ibidem sessionem iam indictam statuta die vigesima prima aprilis celebrandam esse et celebrari, et successive ad ulteriora procedendum, donec sanctissimo domino nostro et sacro concilio expedire videbitur, ut ad hunc seu alium locum, communicato etiam consilio cum invictissimo Caesare et christianissimo rege et aliis regibus et principibus christianis, ipsum concilium reduci possit ac debeat?"],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p197","SESSION 9 — 21 APRIL 1547 (Bologna)","This sacred, oecumenical, and general synod, which was formerly gathered in the city of Trent, now legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit at Bologna, the same most reverend lords, John Maria bishop of Praeneste of Monte, and Marcellus priest of the title of the Holy Cross in Jerusalem, cardinals of the holy Roman church and apostolic legates de latere, presiding in it in the name of the most holy father in Christ and our lord, Paul III, by divine providence pope, considering that on the eleventh day of the month of March of this present year, in a general public session celebrated in the same city of Trent and in the accustomed place, all things having been duly performed, for causes then pressing, urgent, and legitimate, with the authority of the holy apostolic see also intervening, specially granted to the same most reverend presidents, it decreed and ordained that the council was to be transferred from that place to this city, as it did transfer it; likewise that the session appointed for this present day of 21 April there (so that canons concerning the sacraments and reformation might be established and promulgated, which it had proposed to treat) ought to be celebrated in this same city of Bologna.","Haec sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis synodus, quae dudum in civitate Tridenti congregata erat, nunc Bononiae in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, praesidentibus in ea nomine sanctissimi in christo patris et domini nostri domini Pauli, divina providentia papae III, eisdem reverendissimis dominis Ioanne Maria episcopo Praenestino de Monte et Marcello tituli sanctae crucis in Hierusalem presbytero sanctae Romanae ecclesiae cardinalibus et apostolicis de latere legatis, considerans quod die undecima mensis martii praesentis anni, in generali publica sessione in eadem civitate Tridenti et in loco consueto celebrata, omnibus que agendis de more peractis, ex causis tunc instantibus, urgentibus et legitimis, interveniente etiam auctoritate sanctae sedis apostolicae, eisdem reverendissimis praesidentibus etiam specialiter concessa, decrevit et ordinavit concilium ex eo loco in hanc civitatem transferendum esse, sicuti transtulit; item que sessionem pro praesenti die vigesima prima aprilis illic indictam (ut de sacramentorum et reformationis materiis, de quibus tractandum proposuerat, canones sancirentur et promulgarentur) in hac ipsa civitate Bononiae celebrari debere."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p198","SESSION 9 — 21 APRIL 1547 (Bologna)","And considering that some of the fathers who were accustomed to be present at this council, having been occupied in these previous days of Holy Week and the paschal solemnity in their own churches, and some also detained by other impediments, have not yet arrived here, whom nevertheless it is to be hoped will soon be present, and that on this account it has come about that the matters themselves of the sacraments and of reformation could not be examined and discussed with that frequency of prelates which the holy synod desired, so that all things may be done with mature counsel and with the due dignity and gravity, it has judged it good, opportune, and expedient that the aforesaid session, which was to be celebrated on this very day (as above), be deferred and prorogated as to the expediting of those matters to Thursday within the next octave of Pentecost, as it defers and prorogates. Which day it has judged and judges to be most opportune for the business to be done and very convenient especially for the absent fathers.","Considerans que nonnullos ex patribus qui in hoc concilio interesse consueverunt, his superioribus maioris ebdomadae et solemnitatis paschalis diebus in propriis ecclesiis occupatos, aliquos etiam aliis impedimentis detentos, huc nondum accessisse, quos tamen brevi affuturos sperandum est, ac propterea factum esse, ut non ea, quam sancta synodus desiderabat, praelatorum frequentia potuerint materiae ipsae sacramentorum et reformationis examinari ac discuti; ut omnia maturo consilio et cum dignitate et gravitate debita fiant, bonum, opportunum et expediens censuit censet que sessionem praedictam, quae hoc ipso die (ut praefertur) celebranda erat, ad diem iovis infra octavam penthecostes proxime futuram, quoad ipsas materias expediendas, differendam et prorogandam esse, quemadmodum differt et prorogat. Quam diem et rei gerendae maxime opportunam et patribus praesertim absentibus percommodam iudicavit et iudicat."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p199","SESSION 9 — 21 APRIL 1547 (Bologna)","This however being added, that the same holy synod, according to its judgment and will, as it shall think expedient for the affairs of the council, may be able and has power to restrict and diminish the term itself, even in a private congregation.","Hoc tamen adiecto, quod terminum ipsum, ipsa sancta synodus pro eius arbitrio et voluntate, sicuti rebus concilii putaverit expedire, etiam in privata congregatione restringere et imminuere possit et valeat."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p200","SESSION 10 — 2 JUNE 1547 (Bologna)","Although this sacred, oecumenical, and general synod, in the session which was to have been celebrated on 21 April last, concerning the matters of the sacraments and of reformation in this renowned city of Bologna, by a decree promulgated in the public session in the city of Trent on 11 March, on account of some causes and especially on account of the absence of some fathers whom it hoped would be present shortly, had judged that the session was to be deferred and prorogated to this present day, yet wishing to deal kindly even with those who have not come, the same sacred synod, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same cardinals of the holy Roman church and legates of the Apostolic See presiding, has established and decreed that the session itself, which it had decreed to celebrate on this second day of June of the present year 1547, is to be deferred and prorogated as to the expediting of the aforesaid and other matters to the Thursday after the feast of the nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary, which will be the 15th of September next following, as it defers and prorogates, yet in such a way that the prosecution of the discussion and examination both of those things which pertain to dogmas and of those which pertain to reformation be not omitted in the meantime, and that the same holy synod, according to its pleasure and will, even in a private congregation, may be able and have power freely to shorten and prorogate the term itself.","Quamvis haec sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis synodus, sessionem quae die vigesima prima mensis aprilis proxime praeteriti super sacramentorum et reformationis materiis in hac inclyta civitate Bononiae ex decreto in urbe Tridentina in publica sessione die undecima mensis martii promulgato celebranda erat, propter aliquas causas ac praesertim propter absentiam nonnullorum patrum, quos brevi affuturos sperabat, ad hunc praesentem diem differendam et prorogandam esse censuerit, volens tamen cum iis qui non venerunt etiam adhuc benigne agere, eadem sacrosancta synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, praesidentibus in eadem eisdem sanctae Romanae ecclesiae cardinalibus et apostolicae sedis legatis, statuit et decrevit sessionem ipsam, quam hac die secunda mensis iunii praesentis anni millesimi quingentesimi quadragesimi septimi celebrare decreverat, ad diem iovis post festum nativitatis beatae Mariae virginis, quae erit decima quinta mensis septembris proxime futuri, quoad praedictas et alias materias expediendas, differendam et prorogandam esse, quemadmodum differt et prorogat, ita tamen quod prosecutio discussionis et examinationis tam eorum quae ad dogmata quam ad reformationem pertinent interim non omittatur, et terminum ipsum, ipsa sancta synodus pro eius libito et voluntate, etiam in privata congregatione, abbreviare et prorogare libere possit et valeat."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p201","SESSION 11 — 1 MAY 1551 (Trent)","Most reverend and illustrious lords, reverend fathers, does it please you that for the praise and glory of the holy and undivided Trinity, Father and Son and Holy Spirit, for the increase and exaltation of the faith and the Christian religion, the sacred, oecumenical, and general Council of Trent, according to the form and tenor of the letters of our most holy lord, is to be resumed and proceeded to further matters?","Reverendissimi et illustrissimi domini, reverendi que patres, placet ne vobis ad laudem et gloriam sanctae et individuae trinitatis, patris et filii et spiritus sancti, ad incrementum et exaltationem fidei et religionis christianae, sacrum oecumenicum et generale concilium Tridentinum, iuxta formam et tenorem litterarum sanctissimi domini nostri, resumi debere et procedendum esse ad ulteriora?"],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p202","SESSION 11 — 1 MAY 1551 (Trent)","Most reverend and illustrious lords, reverend fathers, does it please you that the next future session be held and celebrated on the next first day of September?","Reverendissimi et illustrissimi domini, reverendi que patres, placet ne vobis proximam futuram sessionem habendam et celebrandam esse in futuris calendis septembris?"],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p203","Decree on the Indiction of the Next Session","The sacred, oecumenical, and general Tridentine synod, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same apostolic legate and nuncios presiding in it, which in the last session had decreed that this next session was to be held today and proceeded to further matters, has until now deferred to proceed on account of the absence of the illustrious German nation (for whose sake the matter is chiefly treated) and the lack of great frequency of the other fathers, now rejoicing in the Lord at the arrival on this very day of our venerable brothers and sons the archbishops of Mainz and Trier, prince-electors of the holy Roman empire, and of very many bishops of that and of other provinces, and giving due thanks to omnipotent God, and conceiving firm hope that very many other prelates both of the same German and of other nations, moved both by the duty of their office and by this example, will come soon, appoints the next session for the fortieth day, which will be the 11th of October next following, and while prosecuting the council itself in the state in which it is found, since in the past sessions it was defined concerning the seven sacraments of the new law in general, and specifically concerning baptism and confirmation, establishes and decrees that concerning the most holy sacrament of the Eucharist, and as regards reformation concerning the remaining matters which pertain to a more easy and convenient residence of prelates, is to be treated and discussed; and it warns and exhorts all fathers that in the meantime, after the example of our Lord Jesus Christ (as far as human fragility will allow), they attend to fastings and prayers, that at length God (who is blessed forever), having been placated, may deign to bring back hearts to the knowledge of His true faith and to the unity of holy mother church and to the norm of right living.","Sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis Tridentina synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, praesidentibus in ea eisdem apostolicis legato et nuntiis, quae in proxima praeterita sessione sequentem hanc hodie habendam et ad ulteriora procedendum esse decreverat, cum ob inclitae Germanicae nationis (cuius praecipue causa agitur) absentiam ac non magnam caeterorum patrum frequentiam procedere hactenus distulerit, de venerabilium in christo fratrum et filiorum suorum Maguntini et Treverensis archiepiscoporum ac sacri Romani imperii principum electorum et complurium ipsius aliarum que provinciarum episcoporum sub hanc ipsam diem adventu in domino exsultans et dignas ipsi omnipotenti deo agens gratias, firmam que spem concipiens quamplurimos alios tam ipsius Germanicae quam aliarum nationum praelatos et sui officii debito et hoc exemplo commotos propediem esse venturos, futuram sessionem ad quadragesimam diem, quae erit undecima mensis octobris proxime sequentis, indicit et concilium ipsum in statu in quo reperitur prosequendo, cum in praeteritis sessionibus de septem sacramentis novae legis in genere et in specie de baptismate et confirmatione definitum fuerit, statuit et decernit de sanctissimae eucharistiae sacramento, necnon quod ad reformationem attinet, de reliquis quae ad faciliorem et commodiorem praelatorum residentiam pertinent agi et tractari debere, ac monet et hortatur omnes patres, ut interim domini nostri Iesu christi exemplo (quantum tamen humana fragilitas patietur) ieiuniis et orationibus vacent, ut tandem, placatus (qui in saecula sit benedictus) deus, corda ad verae suae fidei agnitionem et sanctae matris ecclesiae unitatem ac recte vivendi normam reducere dignetur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p204","Session 12 — 29 September 1551","The sacred, oecumenical, and general Tridentine synod, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same legate and nuncios of the holy apostolic see presiding, although it has come together for that end not without the particular guidance and governance of the Holy Spirit, to set forth the true and ancient doctrine concerning faith and the sacraments, and to bring a remedy for all heresies and other very grievous disadvantages with which the church of God is now miserably agitated and rent into many and various parts, especially this, even from the beginning, it had in mind to pluck up by the roots the tares of execrable errors and schisms which the enemy man has oversown in these calamitous times in the doctrine of faith, in the use and cult of the most holy Eucharist, which otherwise our Savior left in His church as a symbol of that unity and charity by which He wished all Christians to be joined and bound together among themselves.","Sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis Tridentina synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, praesidentibus in ea eisdem sanctae sedis apostolicae legato et nuntiis, etsi in eum finem non absque peculiari spiritus sancti ductu et gubernatione convenerit, ut veram et antiquam de fide et sacramentis doctrinam exponeret et ut haeresibus omnibus et aliis gravissimis incommodis quibus dei ecclesia misere nunc exagitatur et in multas ac varias partes scinditur remedium afferret, hoc praesertim iam inde a principio in votis habuit, ut stirpitus convelleret zizania execrabilium errorum et schismatum quae inimicus homo his nostris calamitosis temporibus in doctrina fidei, usu et cultu sacrosanctae eucharistiae superseminavit, quam alioqui salvator noster in ecclesia sua tanquam symbolum reliquit eius unitatis et charitatis, qua christianos omnes inter se coniunctos et copulatos esse voluit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p205","Session 12 — 29 September 1551","Therefore, delivering that sound and sincere doctrine concerning this venerable and divine sacrament of the Eucharist, which the catholic church, taught by our Lord Jesus Christ Himself and His apostles, and reminded by the Holy Spirit suggesting all truth to it day by day, has always retained and will preserve until the end of the age, forbids all the faithful of Christ that henceforth they dare to believe, teach, or preach concerning the most holy Eucharist otherwise than as is explained and defined in this present decree.","Itaque eadem sacrosancta synodus sanam et synceram illam de venerabili hoc et divino eucharistiae sacramento doctrinam tradens, quam semper catholica ecclesia ab ipso Iesu christo domino nostro et eius apostolis erudita atque a spiritu sancto illi omnem veritatem in dies suggerente edocta retinuit et ad finem usque saeculi conservabit, omnibus christifidelibus interdicit, ne posthac de sanctissima eucharistia aliter credere, docere aut praedicare audeant quam ut est hoc praesenti decreto explicatum atque definitum."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p206","Chapter I","First, the holy synod teaches and openly and simply professes that in the venerable sacrament of the holy Eucharist, after the consecration of the bread and wine, our Lord Jesus Christ, true God and true man, is truly, really, and substantially contained under the species of those sensible things.","Principio docet sancta synodus et aperte ac simpliciter profitetur, in almo sanctae eucharistiae sacramento post panis et vini consecrationem dominum nostrum Iesum christum, verum deum atque hominem, vere, realiter ac substantialiter sub specie illarum rerum sensibilium contineri."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p207","Chapter I","For neither do these things conflict with one another: that our Savior Himself always sits at the right hand of the Father in heaven according to the natural mode of existing, and that nevertheless in many other places He is sacramentally present to us in His own substance by that mode of existing which, although we can scarcely express in words, we can nevertheless, with understanding illuminated by faith, grasp as possible to God and ought most constantly to believe.","Neque enim haec inter se pugnant, ut ipse salvator noster semper ad dexteram patris in coelis assideat iuxta modum existendi naturalem, et ut multis nihilominus aliis in locis sacramentaliter praesens sua substantia nobis adsit ea existendi ratione, quam etsi verbis exprimere vix possumus, possibilem tamen esse deo cogitatione per fidem illustrata assequi possumus et constantissime credere debemus."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p208","Chapter I","For thus all our forefathers, as many as have been in the true church of Christ, who have spoken concerning this most holy sacrament, have most openly professed that our Redeemer instituted this so admirable sacrament at the last supper when, after the blessing of the bread and wine, He testified in clear and explicit words that He gave them His own body and His own blood. Which words, having been recorded by the holy evangelists and afterwards repeated by Saint Paul, since they bear with them that proper and most explicit signification according to which they have been understood by the fathers, it is a most unworthy crime that they be twisted by certain contentious and wicked men to fictitious and imaginary tropes by which the truth of the flesh and blood of Christ is denied, against the universal sense of the church, which, as the pillar and ground of truth, has detested these inventions devised by impious men as satanic, ever acknowledging this most excellent benefit of Christ with a grateful and mindful heart.","Ita enim maiores omnes nostri, quotquot in vera christi ecclesia fuerunt, qui de sanctissimo hoc sacramento disseruerunt, apertissime professi sunt hoc tam admirabile sacramentum in ultima coena redemptorem nostrum instituisse, cum post panis vini que benedictionem se suum ipsius corpus illis praebere ac suum sanguinem disertis et perspicuis verbis testatus est. Quae verba a sanctis evangelistis commemorata et a divo Paulo postea repetita, cum propriam illam et apertissimam significationem prae se ferant secundum quam a patribus intellecta sunt, indignissimum sane flagitium est ea a quibusdam contentiosis et pravis hominibus ad fictitios et imaginarios tropos, quibus veritas carnis et sanguinis christi negatur, contra universum ecclesiae sensum detorqueri, quae, tanquam columna et firmamentum veritatis, haec ab impiis hominibus excogitata commenta velut sathanica detestata est, grato semper et memore animo praestantissimum hoc christi beneficium agnoscens."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p209","Chapter II","Therefore our Savior, about to depart from this world to the Father, instituted this sacrament in which He poured forth, as it were, the riches of His divine love toward men, making a remembrance of His wonderful works, and He commanded that in its reception we venerate His memory and announce His death until He comes to judge the world.","Ergo salvator noster, discessurus ex hoc mundo ad patrem, sacramentum hoc instituit, in quo divitias divini sui erga homines amoris velut effudit, memoriam faciens mirabilium suorum, et in illius sumptione colere nos sui memoriam praecepit, suam que annunciare mortem, donec ipse ad iudicandum mundum veniat."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p210","Chapter II","He also willed that this sacrament be taken as the spiritual food of souls, by which they may be nourished and strengthened, living by that life who said: \"He who eats me, the same also shall live by me,\" and as an antidote by which we may be freed from daily faults and preserved from mortal sins. Moreover, He willed it to be a pledge of our future glory and perpetual happiness, and thus a symbol of that one body of which He Himself is the head, and to which He willed us to be bound as members by the very close connection of faith, hope, and charity, that we might all speak the same thing, and that there might be no schisms among us.","Sumi autem voluit sacramentum hoc tanquam spiritalem animarum cibum, quo alantur et confortentur viventes vita illius qui dixit: \"qui manducat me, et ipse vivet propter me,\" et tanquam antidotum, quo liberemur a culpis quotidianis et a peccatis mortalibus praeservemur. Pignus praeterea id esse voluit futurae nostrae gloriae et perpetuae felicitatis, adeo que symbolum unius illius corporis, cuius ipse caput existit, cui que nos tanquam membra arctissima fidei, spei et charitatis connexione astrictos esse voluit, ut idipsum omnes diceremus nec essent in nobis schismata."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p211","Chapter III","This, indeed, is common to the most holy Eucharist with the other sacraments: that it is a symbol of a sacred thing and a visible form of invisible grace. But this excellent and singular thing is found in it, that the other sacraments then first have the power of sanctifying when someone uses them, but in the Eucharist the author of sanctity Himself is present before use. For the apostles had not yet received the Eucharist from the hand of the Lord when He Himself truly affirmed that His body was that which He gave.","Commune hoc quidem est sanctissimae eucharistiae cum caeteris sacramentis: symbolum esse rei sacrae et invisibilis gratiae formam visibilem; verum illud in ea excellens et singulare reperitur, quod reliqua sacramenta tunc primum sanctificandi vim habent, cum quis illis utitur, at in eucharistia ipse sanctitatis auctor ante usum est. Nondum enim eucharistiam de manu domini apostoli susceperant, cum vere tamen ipse affirmaret corpus suum esse quod praebebat."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p212","Chapter III","And this faith has always been in the church of God: that immediately after consecration, the true body of our Lord and His true blood, together with His soul and divinity, exist under the species of bread and wine; but the body indeed under the species of bread, and the blood under the species of wine, by virtue of the words; the body moreover under the species of wine, and the blood under the species of bread, and the soul under both, by virtue of that natural connection and concomitance by which the parts of Christ the Lord, who has now risen from the dead to die no more, are joined together; the divinity moreover on account of that admirable hypostatic union of it with the body and soul.","Et semper haec fides in ecclesia dei fuit: statim post consecrationem verum domini nostri corpus verum que eius sanguinem sub panis et vini specie, una cum ipsius anima et divinitate, existere; sed corpus quidem sub specie panis et sanguinem sub specie vini ex vi verborum; ipsum autem corpus sub specie vini et sanguinem sub specie panis, animamque sub utraque, vi naturalis illius connexionis et concomitantiae, qua partes christi domini, qui iam ex mortuis resurrexit non amplius moriturus, inter se copulantur, divinitatem porro propter admirabilem illam eius cum corpore et anima hypostaticam unionem."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p213","Chapter III","Wherefore it is most true that as much is contained under either species as under both. For the whole and entire Christ exists under the species of bread and under any part of that same species, and the whole likewise under the species of wine and under its parts.","Quapropter verissimum est tantundem sub alterutra specie atque sub utraque contineri. Totus enim et integer christus sub panis specie et sub quavis ipsius speciei parte, totus item sub vini specie et sub eius partibus existit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p214","Chapter IV","But since Christ our Redeemer said that that which He offered under the species of bread was truly His body, therefore it has always been persuaded in the church of God, and this holy synod now again declares, that by the consecration of the bread and wine a conversion is made of the whole substance of the bread into the substance of the body of Christ our Lord, and of the whole substance of the wine into the substance of His blood. Which conversion is fittingly and properly called transubstantiation by the holy catholic church.","Quoniam autem christus redemptor noster corpus suum id quod sub specie panis offerebat vere esse dixit, ideo persuasum semper in ecclesia dei fuit, id que nunc denuo sancta haec synodus declarat, per consecrationem panis et vini conversionem fieri totius substantiae panis in substantiam corporis christi domini nostri et totius substantiae vini in substantiam sanguinis eius. Quae conversio convenienter et proprie a sancta catholica ecclesia transsubstantiatio est appellata."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p215","Chapter V","Therefore no place for doubt is left that all the faithful of Christ, according to the custom always received in the catholic church, show to this most holy sacrament in veneration the cult of latria, which is owed to the true God. For it is not for this reason less to be adored because it was instituted by Christ the Lord to be taken. For we believe that same God to be present in it, whom the eternal Father, introducing into the world, says: \"And let all the angels of God adore Him\"; whom the Magi, falling down, adored; whom finally the scripture testifies was adored by the apostles in Galilee.","Nullus itaque dubitandi locus relinquitur, quin omnes christifideles, pro more in catholica ecclesia semper recepto, latriae cultum, qui vero deo debetur, huic sanctissimo sacramento in veneratione exhibeant. Neque enim ideo minus est adorandum, quod fuerit a christo domino ut sumatur institutum. Nam illum eundem deum praesentem in eo adesse credimus, quem pater aeternus introducens in orbem terrarum dicit: \"et adorent eum omnes angeli dei\"; quem magi procidentes adoraverunt; quem denique in Galilea ab apostolis adoratum fuisse scriptura testatur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p216","Chapter V","Moreover, the holy synod declares that the custom, that this sublime and venerable sacrament be celebrated with singular veneration and solemnity on a particular and special feast day each year, and that it be reverently and honorably carried about in processions through the streets and public places, was piously and religiously introduced into the church of God. For it is most just that certain sacred days be established when all Christians show themselves grateful and mindful toward their common Lord and Redeemer with a singular and remarkable testimony, on account of that ineffable and truly divine benefit by which the victory and triumph of His death is represented.","Declarat praeterea sancta synodus pie et religiose admodum in dei ecclesiam inductum fuisse hunc morem, ut singulis annis peculiari quodam et festo die praecelsum hoc et venerabile sacramentum singulari veneratione ac solemnitate celebraretur, ut que in processionibus reverenter et honorifice illud per vias et loca publica circumferretur. Aequissimum est enim sacros aliquos statutos esse dies, cum christiani omnes singulari ac rara quadam significatione gratos et memores testentur animos erga communem dominum et redemptorem pro tam ineffabili et plane divino beneficio, quo mortis eius victoria et triumphus repraesentatur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p217","Chapter V","And thus it was fitting that victorious truth should triumph over falsehood and heresy, so that, placed in the sight of so great splendor and in so great joy of the universal church, its adversaries, weakened and broken, may waste away, or, affected and confounded with shame, may at some time come to their senses.","Ac sic quidem oportuit victricem veritatem de mendacio et haeresi triumphum agere, ut eius adversarii in conspectu tanti splendoris et in tanta universae ecclesiae laetitia positi vel debilitati et fracti tabescant, vel pudore affecti et confusi aliquando resipiscant."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p218","Chapter VI","The custom of preserving the holy Eucharist in a shrine is so ancient that it was recognized even by the age of the Nicene council. Moreover, the carrying of the same sacred Eucharist to the sick and the careful preserving of it in churches for this use, besides that it is joined with the greatest equity and reason, is also found commanded in many councils and is observed by the most ancient custom of the catholic church. Therefore this holy synod establishes that this salutary and necessary custom is altogether to be retained.","Consuetudo asservandi in sacrario sanctam eucharistiam adeo antiqua est, ut eam saeculum etiam Niceni concilii agnoverit. Porro deferri ipsam sacram eucharistiam ad infirmos et in hunc usum diligenter in ecclesiis conservari, praeterquam quod cum summa aequitate et ratione coniunctum est, tum multis in conciliis praeceptum invenitur et vetustissimo catholicae ecclesiae more est observatum. Quare sancta haec synodus retinendum omnino salutarem hunc et necessarium morem statuit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p219","Chapter VII","If it is not fitting that anyone approach to any sacred functions unless he approaches holy, certainly the more a Christian man knows the sanctity and divinity of this heavenly sacrament, the more diligently he ought to take care lest he approach to receive it without great reverence and holiness, especially since we read those words full of fear in the apostle: \"He who eats and drinks unworthily, eats and drinks judgment to himself, not discerning the body of the Lord.\" Therefore it is to be recalled to the memory of one wishing to communicate that precept: \"But let a man prove himself.\"","Si non decet ad sacras ullas functiones quempiam accedere nisi sancte, certe quo magis sanctitas et divinitas coelestis huius sacramenti viro christiano comperta est, eo diligentius cavere ille debet, ne absque magna reverentia et sanctitate ad id percipiendum accedat, praesertim cum illa plena formidinis verba apud apostolum legamus: \"qui manducat et bibit indigne, iudicium sibi manducat et bibit, non diiudicans corpus domini.\" Quare communicare volenti revocandum est in memoriam eius praeceptum: \"probet autem se ipsum homo.\""],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p220","Chapter VII","Moreover, ecclesiastical custom declares that proof is necessary, so that no one conscious of mortal sin, however contrite he may seem to himself, ought to approach the sacred Eucharist without prior sacramental confession. Which this holy synod has decreed is to be perpetually observed by all Christians, even by those priests upon whom it lies by office to celebrate, provided a confessor is not lacking to them. But if, necessity pressing, a priest celebrate without prior confession, let him confess as soon as possible.","Ecclesiastica autem consuetudo declarat eam probationem necessariam esse, ut nullus sibi conscius peccati mortalis, quantumvis sibi contritus videatur, absque praemissa sacramentali confessione ad sacram eucharistiam accedere debeat. Quod a christianis omnibus, etiam ab iis sacerdotibus quibus ex officio incubuerit celebrare, haec sancta synodus perpetuo servandum esse decrevit, modo non desit illis copia confessoris. Quod si necessitate urgente sacerdos absque praevia confessione celebraverit, quam primum confiteatur."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p221","Chapter VIII","Concerning the use, however, our fathers rightly and wisely distinguished three ways of receiving this holy sacrament. For they taught that some receive it only sacramentally, as sinners; others only spiritually, those namely who, eating that heavenly bread in desire, with a living faith which works through love, perceive its fruit and benefit; the third, moreover, receive it both sacramentally and spiritually: these are they who so prove and prepare themselves beforehand that, clothed in the wedding garment, they approach this divine table.","Quoad usum autem, recte et sapienter patres nostri tres rationes hoc sanctum sacramentum accipiendi distinxerunt. Quosdam enim docuerunt sacramentaliter duntaxat id sumere, ut peccatores; alios tantum spiritualiter, illos nimirum qui voto propositum illum coelestem panem edentes, fide viva quae per dilectionem operatur, fructum eius et utilitatem sentiunt; tertios porro sacramentaliter simul et spiritaliter, ii autem sunt qui ita se prius probant et instruunt, ut vestem nuptialem induti ad divinam hanc mensam accedant."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p222","Chapter VIII","In sacramental reception, moreover, the custom has always been in the church of God that laypersons receive communion from priests, but that celebrating priests communicate themselves. Which custom, as descending from apostolic tradition, ought justly and deservedly to be retained.","In sacramentali autem sumptione semper in ecclesia dei mos fuit, ut laici a sacerdotibus communionem acciperent, sacerdotes autem celebrantes se ipsos communicarent; qui mos, tanquam ex traditione apostolica descendens, iure ac merito retineri debet."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p223","Chapter VIII","Finally, with paternal affection the holy synod admonishes, exhorts, beseeches, and conjures, through the bowels of the mercy of our God, that all and each who are called by the Christian name at length at some time concur and agree in this sign of unity, in this bond of charity, in this symbol of concord; and, mindful of so great majesty and so excellent love of our Lord Jesus Christ, who gave His beloved soul as the price of our salvation and His flesh to eat, believe and venerate these sacred mysteries of His body and blood with such constancy and firmness of faith, with such devotion of soul, with such piety and cult that they may be able to receive that supersubstantial bread frequently, and may it truly be to them the life of the soul and the perpetual health of the mind; that, strengthened by its vigor, they may be able to arrive from the journey of this miserable pilgrimage to the heavenly fatherland, about to eat without any veil that same bread of angels which now they eat under sacred veils.","Demum vero paterno affectu admonet sancta synodus, hortatur, rogat et obsecrat per viscera misericordiae dei nostri, ut omnes et singuli qui christiano nomine censentur in hoc unitatis signo, in hoc vinculo charitatis, in hoc concordiae symbolo iam tandem aliquando conveniant et concordent, memores que tantae maiestatis et tam eximii amoris Iesu christi domini nostri, qui dilectam animam suam in nostrae salutis precium et carnem suam nobis dedit ad manducandum, haec sacra mysteria corporis et sanguinis eius ea fidei constantia et firmitate, ea animi devotione, ea pietate et cultu credant et venerentur, ut panem illum supersubstantialem frequenter suscipere possint, et is vere eis sit animae vita et perpetua sanitas mentis, cuius vigore confortati ex huius miserae peregrinationis itinere ad coelestem patriam pervenire valeant, eundem panem angelorum, quem modo sub sacris velaminibus edunt, absque ullo velamine manducaturi."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p224","Canons on the Most Holy Sacrament of the Eucharist","If anyone denies that in the most holy sacrament of the Eucharist there are contained truly, really, and substantially the body and blood together with the soul and divinity of our Lord Jesus Christ, and therefore the whole Christ, but says that it is in it only as in a sign or figure or by virtue: let him be anathema.","Si quis negaverit in sanctissimo eucharistiae sacramento contineri vere, realiter et substantialiter corpus et sanguinem una cum anima et divinitate domini nostri Iesu christi ac proinde totum christum, sed dixerit tantummodo esse in eo ut in signo vel figura aut virtute: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p225","Canon II","If anyone says that in the sacred and holy sacrament of the Eucharist the substance of the bread and wine remains together with the body and blood of our Lord Jesus Christ, and denies that wonderful and singular conversion of the whole substance of the bread into the body and the whole substance of the wine into the blood, the species only of the bread and wine remaining, which conversion the catholic church most fittingly calls transubstantiation: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit in sacrosancto eucharistiae sacramento remanere substantiam panis et vini una cum corpore et sanguine domini nostri Iesu christi, negaverit que mirabilem illam et singularem conversionem totius substantiae panis in corpus et totius substantiae vini in sanguinem, manentibus dumtaxat speciebus panis et vini, quam quidem conversionem catholica ecclesia aptissime transsubstantiationem appellat: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p226","Canon III","If anyone denies that in the venerable sacrament of the Eucharist the whole Christ is contained under each species and under each part of each species, separation having been made: let him be anathema.","Si quis negaverit in venerabili sacramento eucharistiae sub unaquaque specie et sub singulis cuiusque speciei partibus separatione facta totum christum contineri: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p227","Canon IV","If anyone says that, consecration having been performed, the body and blood of our Lord Jesus Christ are not in the admirable sacrament of the Eucharist, but only in use while it is being taken, not however before or after, and that the true body of the Lord does not remain in the consecrated hosts or particles which are reserved or remain after communion: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit, peracta consecratione, in admirabili eucharistiae sacramento non esse corpus et sanguinem domini nostri Iesu christi, sed tantum in usu dum sumitur, non autem ante vel post, et in hostiis seu particulis consecratis quae post communionem reservantur vel supersunt non remanere verum corpus domini: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p228","Canon V","If anyone says that the principal fruit of the most holy Eucharist is the remission of sins, or that no other effects result from it: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit vel praecipuum fructum sanctissimae eucharistiae esse remissionem peccatorum vel ex ea non alios effectus provenire: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p229","Canon VI","If anyone says that in the holy sacrament of the Eucharist Christ, the only-begotten Son of God, is not to be adored with the cult of latria, even external, and that therefore it is not to be venerated with a special festive celebration, nor to be solemnly carried about in processions according to the laudable and universal rite and custom of the holy church, nor to be publicly set before the people for adoration, and that its adorers are idolaters: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit in sancto eucharistiae sacramento christum unigenitum dei filium non esse cultu latriae etiam externo adorandum atque ideo nec festiva peculiari celebritate venerandum, neque in processionibus secundum laudabilem et universalem ecclesiae sanctae ritum et consuetudinem solemniter circumgestandum, vel non publice ut adoretur populo proponendum, et eius adoratores esse idolatras: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p230","Canon VII","If anyone says that it is not lawful that the sacred Eucharist be reserved in a shrine, but that it must necessarily be distributed immediately after consecration to those present, or that it is not lawful that it be honorably carried to the infirm: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit non licere sacram eucharistiam in sacrario reservari, sed statim post consecrationem astantibus necessario distribuendam, aut non licere ut illa ad infirmos honorifice deferatur: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p231","Canon VIII","If anyone says that Christ in the Eucharist is eaten only spiritually, and not also sacramentally and really: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit christum in eucharistia exhibitum spiritualiter tantum manducari et non etiam sacramentaliter ac realiter: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p232","Canon IX","If anyone denies that all and each of the faithful of Christ of both sexes, when they have reached the years of discretion, are bound each year at least at Easter to communicate, according to the precept of holy mother church: let him be anathema.","Si quis negaverit omnes et singulos christifideles utriusque sexus, cum ad annos discretionis pervenerint, teneri singulis annis saltem in paschate ad communicandum iuxta praeceptum sanctae matris ecclesiae: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p233","Canon X","If anyone says that it is not lawful for a celebrating priest to communicate himself: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit non licere sacerdoti celebranti se ipsum communicare: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p234","Canon XI","If anyone says that faith alone is sufficient preparation for receiving the sacrament of the most holy Eucharist: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit solam fidem esse sufficientem praeparationem ad sumendum sanctissimae eucharistiae sacramentum: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p235","Canon XI","And lest so great a sacrament be taken unworthily and therefore unto death and condemnation, the same holy synod establishes and declares that those whom the conscience of mortal sin burdens, however contrite they may think themselves, having the opportunity of a confessor, sacramental confession is necessarily to be preceded. But if anyone shall have presumed to teach, preach, or pertinaciously assert, or also to defend in public disputation contrary to this, by that very fact let him be excommunicated.","Et ne tantum sacramentum indigne atque ideo in mortem et condemnationem sumatur, statuit et declarat ipsa sancta synodus illis quos conscientia peccati mortalis gravat, quantumcunque etiam se contritos existiment, habita copia confessoris, necessario praemittendam esse confessionem sacramentalem. Si quis autem contrarium docere, praedicare vel pertinaciter asserere seu etiam publice disputando defendere praesumpserit, eo ipso excommunicatus existat."],["vol2/trent/part-1/index.html","p236","Decree on the Resumption of the Council and the Indiction of the Next Session","The sacred, oecumenical, and general Tridentine synod, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same legate and nuncios of the holy apostolic see presiding, has established and decreed that the next future session is to be held and celebrated on the Thursday after the second Sunday of Lent, which will be the 26th day of February.","Sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis Tridentina synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, praesidentibus in ea eisdem apostolicae sedis legatis, statuit et decrevit proximam futuram sessionem habendam et celebrandam esse feria quinta post secundam dominicam quadragesimae, quae erit dies 26 mensis februarii."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p237","Session 13 — 11 October 1551","The sacred, oecumenical, and general Tridentine synod, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same legate and nuncios of the Apostolic See presiding, for the completion of the salutary doctrine concerning the sacrament of the Eucharist, which was begun in the preceding session, has decreed that the following matters should be defined concerning the communion under both species and concerning the communion of infants.","Sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis Tridentina synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, praesidentibus in ea eisdem sanctae sedis apostolicae legato et nuntiis, ad consummationem salutaris de eucharistiae sacramento doctrinae, quae in praecedenti sessione inchoata fuit, ea quae sequuntur de communione sub utraque specie et de parvulorum communione definienda esse decrevit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p238","Chapter I","The same holy synod, taught by the Holy Spirit, who is the Spirit of wisdom and understanding, the Spirit of counsel and piety, and following the judgment and custom of the church itself, declares and teaches that laypersons and clerics not celebrating are not bound by any divine precept to receive the sacrament of the Eucharist under both species, nor that it can be doubted in any way (faith being preserved) that communion under the species of bread alone suffices for them for salvation.","Eadem sancta ipsa synodus, a spiritu sancto qui spiritus est sapientiae et intellectus, spiritus consilii et pietatis edocta atque ipsius ecclesiae iudicium et consuetudinem secuta, declarat ac docet nullo divino praecepto laicos et clericos non conficientes obligari ad eucharistiae sacramentum sub utraque specie sumendum, neque ullo pacto (salva fide) dubitari posse quin illis alterius speciei communio ad salutem sufficiat."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p239","Chapter I","For although Christ the Lord instituted this venerable sacrament in the species of bread and wine at the last supper and delivered it to the apostles, nevertheless that institution and delivery do not tend to this, that all the faithful of Christ are bound by the Lord's decree to receive both species. Nor is it rightly gathered from that discourse in John, chapter six, that communion under both species was commanded by the Lord, however it is understood according to the various interpretations of the holy fathers and doctors.","Nam etsi christus dominus in ultima coena venerabile hoc sacramentum in panis et vini speciebus instituit et apostolis tradidit, non tamen illa institutio et traditio eo tendunt, ut omnes christifideles statuto domini ad utramque speciem accipiendam adstringantur. Sed neque ex sermone illo apud Ioannem sexto recte colligitur utriusque speciei communionem a domino praeceptam esse, utcumque iuxta varias sanctorum patrum et doctorum interpretationes intelligatur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p240","Chapter I","For He who said: \"Unless you eat the flesh of the Son of man and drink His blood, you shall not have life in you,\" also said: \"If anyone eats of this bread, he shall live forever.\" And He who said: \"He who eats my flesh and drinks my blood has eternal life,\" also said: \"The bread which I shall give is my flesh for the life of the world.\" And finally, He who said: \"He who eats my flesh and drinks my blood abides in me and I in him,\" also said: \"He who eats this bread shall live forever.\"","Namque qui dixit: \"nisi manducaveritis carnem filii hominis et biberitis eius sanguinem, non habebitis vitam in vobis,\" dixit quoque: \"si quis manducaverit ex hoc pane, vivet in aeternum.\" Et qui dixit: \"qui manducat meam carnem et bibit meum sanguinem, habet vitam aeternam,\" dixit etiam: \"panis quem ego dabo, caro mea est pro mundi vita.\" Et denique qui dixit: \"qui manducat meam carnem et bibit meum sanguinem, in me manet et ego in illo,\" dixit nihilominus: \"qui manducat hunc panem, vivet in aeternum.\""],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p241","Chapter II","Moreover, it declares that this power has always been in the church, that in the dispensation of the sacraments, their substance being preserved, it might establish or change those things which it judged to be more expedient for the utility of the recipients or for the veneration of the sacraments themselves, according to the variety of things, times, and places. Which the apostle did not obscurely seem to intimate when he said: \"Let a man so account of us as of the ministers of Christ and the dispensers of the mysteries of God.\"","Praeterea declarat hanc potestatem perpetuo in ecclesia fuisse, ut in sacramentorum dispensatione, salva illorum substantia, ea statueret vel mutaret quae suscipientium utilitati seu ipsorum sacramentorum venerationi pro rerum, temporum et locorum varietate magis expedire iudicaret. Id autem apostolus non obscure visus est innuisse cum ait: \"sic nos existimet homo ut ministros christi et dispensatores mysteriorum dei.\""],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p242","Chapter II","And that He Himself used this power is sufficiently evident, since in many other things, so also in this very sacrament, when He arranged certain things concerning its use, He said: \"Other things I will set in order when I come.\"","Atque ipsum quidem hac potestate usum esse satis constat, cum in multis aliis, tum in hoc ipso sacramento, cum ordinatis nonnullis circa eius usum, \"cetera\" inquit \"cum venero disponam.\""],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p243","Chapter II","Therefore, recognizing this authority of hers in the administration of the sacraments, holy mother church, although from the beginning of the Christian religion the use of both species was not infrequent, yet in the progress of time, that custom having been already very widely changed, having been induced by grave and just reasons, approved this custom of communicating under one species and decreed that it is to be held as law, which it is not lawful to reject or to change at will without the authority of the church itself.","Quare agnoscens sancta mater ecclesia hanc suam in administratione sacramentorum auctoritatem, licet ab initio christianae religionis non infrequens utriusque speciei usus fuisset, tamen progressu temporis, latissime iam mutata illa consuetudine, gravibus et iustis causis adducta, hanc consuetudinem sub altera specie communicandi approbavit et pro lege habendam decrevit, quam reprobare aut sine ipsius ecclesiae auctoritate pro libito mutare non licet."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p244","Chapter III","Moreover, it declares that although our Redeemer, as has been said before, instituted this sacrament in two species at that last supper and delivered it to the apostles, nevertheless it is to be confessed that even under one species alone the whole and entire Christ and a true sacrament are received, and therefore that, with respect to its fruit, those who receive one species alone are not defrauded of any grace necessary for salvation.","Insuper declarat, quavis redemptor noster, ut antea dictum est, in suprema illa coena hoc sacramentum in duabus speciebus instituerit et apostolis tradiderit, tamen fatendum esse etiam sub altera tantum specie totum atque integrum christum verum que sacramentum sumi, ac propterea, quod ad fructum attinet, nulla gratia necessaria ad salutem eos defraudari qui unam speciem solam accipiunt."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p245","Chapter IV","Finally, the same holy synod teaches that infants lacking the use of reason are bound by no necessity to sacramental communion of the Eucharist, since, having been regenerated by the laver of baptism and incorporated into Christ, they cannot in that age lose the grace of the sons of God already obtained. Nor, however, is antiquity to be condemned if it at any time observed that custom in some places.","Denique eadem sancta synodus docet parvulos usu rationis carentes nulla obligari necessitate ad sacramentalem eucharistiae communionem, siquidem per baptismi lavacrum regenerati et christo incorporati, adeptam iam filiorum dei gratiam in illa aetate amittere non possunt. Neque ideo tamen damnanda est antiquitas, si eum morem in quibusdam locis aliquando servavit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p246","Chapter IV","For just as those most holy fathers had a probable reason for their action according to the condition of that time, so it is to be believed without controversy that they did this by no necessity of salvation.","Ut enim sanctissimi illi patres sui facti probabilem causam pro illius temporis ratione habuerunt, ita certe eos nulla salutis necessitate id fecisse sine controversia credendum est."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p247","Canons on the Communion Under Both Species and the Communion of Infants","If anyone says that by a precept of God or by necessity of salvation all and each of the faithful of Christ ought to receive both species of the most holy sacrament of the Eucharist: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit ex dei praecepto vel ex necessitate salutis omnes et singulos christifideles utramque speciem sanctissimi eucharistiae sacramenti sumere debere: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p248","Canon II","If anyone says that the holy catholic church was not induced by just causes and reasons to communicate laypersons and also clerics not celebrating under the species of bread only, or that she has erred in this: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit sanctam ecclesiam catholicam non iustis causis et rationibus adductam fuisse ut laicos atque etiam clericos non conficientes sub una panis tantummodo specie communicaret, aut in eo errasse: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p249","Canon III","If anyone denies that the whole and entire Christ, the fountain and author of all graces, is received under the species of bread alone, because (as some falsely assert) it is not received according to the institution of Christ Himself under both species: let him be anathema.","Si quis negaverit totum et integrum christum, omnium gratiarum fontem et auctorem, sub una panis specie sumi, quia (ut quidam falso asserunt) non secundum ipsius christi institutionem sub utraque specie sumatur: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p250","Canon IV","If anyone says that communion of the Eucharist is necessary for infants before they have reached the years of discretion: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit parvulis, antequam ad annos discretionis pervenerint, necessariam esse eucharistiae communionem: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p251","Prorogation of the Session","The same sacred synod has established and decreed that the next future session is to be held and celebrated on the Thursday after the octave of the feast of the nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary, which will be the 17th day of the month of September next following. This however being added, that the same holy synod, according to its judgment and will, as it shall think expedient for the affairs of the council, may be able and have power freely to restrict and prorogate the said term, and each term to be prefixed in the future, even in a general congregation.","Eadem sacrosancta synodus statuit et decrevit proximam futuram sessionem habendam et celebrandam esse feria quinta post octavam festi nativitatis beatae Mariae virginis, quae erit decima septima mensis septembris proxime futuri. Hoc tamen adiecto, quod dictum terminum, ac unicuique sessioni in posterum praefigendum, ipsa sancta synodus pro eius arbitrio et voluntate, sicuti rebus concilii putaverit expedire, etiam in generali congregatione restringere et prorogare libere possit et valeat."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p252","Session 14 — 25 November 1551","The sacred, oecumenical, and general Tridentine synod, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same legates of the holy Apostolic See presiding, although in the decree on justification, on account of a certain necessary reason arising from the connection of the matters, much discourse was interposed concerning the sacrament of penance, nevertheless such a multitude of various errors concerning it exists in this our age that it would be of no little public utility to have delivered a more exact and full definition of it, in which, all errors having been demonstrated and refuted with the help of the Holy Spirit, the catholic truth might be made clear and illustrious; which the holy synod now proposes to be perpetually observed by all Christians.","Sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis Tridentina synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, praesidentibus in ea eisdem sanctae sedis apostolicae legato et nuntiis, quamvis in decreto de iustificatione, propter locorum cognationem, necessaria quadam ratione multus de poenitentiae sacramento sermo interpositus fuerit, tanta nihilominus circa illud nostra hac aetate diversorum errorum est multitudo, ut non parum publicae utilitatis retulerit de eo exactiorem et pleniorem diffinitionem tradidisse, in qua demonstratis et convulsis, spiritus sancti praesidio, universis erroribus, catholica veritas perspicua et illustris fieret; quam nunc sancta haec synodus christianis omnibus perpetuo servandam proponit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p253","Chapter I","If such gratitude toward God were in all the regenerate that they would constantly defend the justice received in baptism by His benefit and grace, there would not have been need for another sacrament besides baptism itself to be instituted for the remission of sins. But since God, rich in mercy, has known our frame, He has also bestowed a remedy for life upon those who afterwards delivered themselves into the servitude of sin and into the power of the devil, namely the sacrament of penance, by which the benefit of the death of Christ is applied to those who have fallen after baptism.","Si ea regeneratis omnibus gratitudo erga deum esset, ut iustitiam in baptismo ipsius beneficio et gratia susceptam constanter tuerentur, non fuisset opus aliud ab ipso baptismo sacramentum ad peccatorum remissionem esse institutum. Quoniam autem deus, dives in misericordia, cognovit figmentum nostrum, illis etiam vitae remedium contulit qui sese postea in peccati servitutem et daemonis potestatem tradidissent, sacramentum videlicet poenitentiae, quo lapsis post baptismum beneficium mortis christi applicatur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p254","Chapter I","Penance, indeed, was necessary for all men who had defiled themselves with any mortal sin at any time, for attaining grace and justice, even for those who had sought to be washed by the sacrament of baptism, so that, their perversity having been cast aside and amended, they might detest so great an offense against God with hatred of sin and with a pious sorrow of mind.","Fuit quidem poenitentia universis hominibus, qui se mortali aliquo peccato inquinassent, quovis tempore, ad gratiam et iustitiam assequendam necessaria, illis etiam qui baptismi sacramento ablui petivissent, ut, perversitate abiecta et emendata, tantam dei offensionem cum peccati odio et pio animi dolore detestarentur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p255","Chapter I","Whence the prophet says: \"Turn yourselves and do penance for all your iniquities, and iniquity shall not be your ruin.\" The Lord also said: \"Unless you do penance, you shall all likewise perish.\" And the prince of the apostles, Peter, commending penance to sinners about to be initiated by baptism, said: \"Do penance and be baptized every one of you.\"","Unde propheta ait: \"convertimini et agite poenitentiam ab omnibus iniquitatibus vestris, et non erit vobis in ruinam iniquitas.\" Dominus etiam dixit: \"nisi poenitentiam egeritis, omnes similiter peribitis.\" Et princeps apostolorum Petrus, peccatoribus baptismo initiandis poenitentiam commendans, dicebat: \"poenitentiam agite et baptizetur unusquisque vestrum.\""],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p256","Chapter I","Moreover, before the coming of Christ, penance was not a sacrament, nor is it for anyone after His coming before baptism.","Porro nec ante adventum christi poenitentia erat sacramentum, nec est post adventum illius cuiquam ante baptismum."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p257","Chapter I","But the Lord instituted the sacrament of penance then especially, when, raised from the dead, He breathed upon His disciples saying: \"Receive the Holy Spirit: whose sins you shall forgive, they are forgiven them; and whose sins you shall retain, they are retained.\" By which signal deed and so clear words the consent of all the fathers has always understood that the power of remitting and retaining sins was communicated to the apostles and their lawful successors for reconciling the faithful who have fallen after baptism. And the catholic church, with good reason, drove out and condemned the Novatians, who formerly pertinaciously denied the power of remission.","Dominus autem sacramentum poenitentiae tunc praecipue instituit, cum a mortuis excitatus insufflavit in discipulos suos dicens: \"accipite spiritum sanctum: quorum remiseritis peccata, remittuntur eis; et quorum retinueritis, retenta sunt.\" Quo tam insigni facto et verbis tam perspicuis potestatem remittendi et retinendi peccata, ad reconciliandos fideles post baptismum lapsos, apostolis et eorum legitimis successoribus fuisse communicatam universorum patrum consensus semper intellexit, et Novatianos, remittendi potestatem olim pertinaciter negantes, magna ratione ecclesia catholica, tanquam haereticos, explosit atque condemnavit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p258","Chapter I","Therefore this holy synod, approving and receiving this most true sense of those words of the Lord, condemns the fictitious interpretations of those who, against the institution of this sacrament, falsely twist those words to the power of preaching the word of God and of announcing the gospel of Christ.","Quare verissimum hunc illorum verborum domini sensum sancta haec synodus probans et recipiens, damnat eorum commentitias interpretationes, qui verba illa ad potestatem praedicandi verbum dei et christi evangelium annunciandi contra huiusmodi sacramenti institutionem falso detorquent."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p259","Chapter II","Moreover, this sacrament is known to differ from baptism in many respects. For besides that the matter and form by which the essence of the sacrament is perfected differ very widely, it is certain that the minister of baptism is not required to be a judge, since the church exercises judgment on no one who has not first entered into it through the door of baptism. \"For what have I to do,\" says the apostle, \"with those who are outside?\" It is otherwise with those of the household of faith, whom Christ the Lord once made members of His own body by the laver of baptism.","Caeterum hoc sacramentum multis rationibus a baptismo differre dignoscitur. Nam praeterquam quod materia et forma, quibus sacramenti essentia perficitur, longissime dissidet, constat certe baptismi ministrum iudicem esse non oportere, cum ecclesia in neminem iudicium exerceat, qui non prius in ipsam per baptismi ianuam fuerit ingressus. \"Quid enim mihi\" (inquit apostolus) \"de iis qui foris sunt iudicare?\" Secus est de domesticis fidei, quos christus dominus lavacro baptismi sui corporis membra semel effecit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p260","Chapter II","For these, if they afterwards stain themselves with some crime, He willed not to be cleansed by a repeated baptism (since this in the catholic church is by no means allowed), but to be brought as defendants before this tribunal, that by the sentence of the priests they might be able to be absolved not once but as often as, penitent, they should flee to it from their sins committed.","Nam hos, si se postea crimine aliquo contaminaverint, non iam repetito baptismo ablui (cum id in ecclesia catholica nulla ratione liceat), sed ante hoc tribunal tanquam reos sisti voluit, ut per sacerdotum sententiam non semel, sed quoties ab admissis peccatis ad ipsum poenitentes confugerent, possent liberari."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p261","Chapter II","Moreover, the fruit of baptism is one; that of penance, another. For through baptism, putting on Christ, we are made altogether a new creature in Him, obtaining a full and entire remission of all sins; to which newness and integrity, however, through the sacrament of penance, we cannot arrive without great tears and labors on our part, divine justice so demanding, so that penance has rightly been called by the holy fathers a laborious kind of baptism. But this sacrament of penance is necessary for salvation for those who have fallen after baptism, as baptism itself is for those not yet regenerated.","Alius praeterea est baptismi et alius poenitentiae fructus. Per baptismum enim, christum induentes, nova prorsus in illo efficimur creatura, plenam et integram peccatorum omnium remissionem consequentes; ad quam tamen novitatem et integritatem per sacramentum poenitentiae sine magnis nostris fletibus et laboribus, divina id exigente iustitia, pervenire nequaquam possumus, ut merito poenitentia laboriosus quidam baptismus a sanctis patribus dictus fuerit. Est autem hoc sacramentum poenitentiae lapsis post baptismum ad salutem necessarium, ut nondum regeneratis ipse baptismus."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p262","Chapter III","Moreover, the holy synod teaches that the form of the sacrament of penance, in which its power chiefly consists, is placed in those words of the minister: \"I absolve you,\" and certain others, to which, according to the custom of the holy church, certain prayers are laudably joined, which nevertheless do not pertain to the essence of that form nor are necessary for the administration of the sacrament.","Docet praeterea sancta synodus sacramenti poenitentiae formam, in qua praecipue ipsius vis sita est, in illis ministri verbis positam esse: \"Ego absolvo te,\" et cetera, quibus, de ecclesiae sanctae more, preces quaedam laudabiliter adiunguntur, ad ipsius tamen formae essentiam nequaquam spectant neque ad ipsius sacramenti administrationem sunt necessariae."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p263","Chapter III","But the acts of the penitent himself, namely contrition, confession, and satisfaction, are as it were the matter of this sacrament. Which, insofar as they are required in the penitent for the integrity of the sacrament and for full and perfect remission of sins by the institution of God, are for this reason called the parts of penance.","Sunt autem quasi materia huius sacramenti ipsius poenitentis actus, nempe contritio, confessio et satisfactio. Qui, quatenus in poenitente ad integritatem sacramenti et ad plenam que et perfectam peccatorum remissionem ex dei institutione requiruntur, hac ratione poenitentiae partes dicuntur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p264","Chapter III","But the thing and effect of this sacrament, as far as it pertains to its power and efficacy, is reconciliation with God, which sometimes in pious men and those receiving this sacrament with devotion is accustomed to be followed by peace and serenity of conscience with vehement consolation of the spirit.","Sane vero res et effectus huius sacramenti, quantum ad eius vim et efficaciam pertinet, reconciliatio est cum deo, quam interdum in viris piis et cum devotione hoc sacramentum percipientibus conscientiae pax ac serenitas cum vehementi spiritus consolatione consequi solet."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p265","Chapter IV","Contrition, which holds the first place among the aforesaid acts of the penitent, is a sorrow of soul and a detestation for sin committed, with the purpose of not sinning in the future.","Contritio, quae primum locum inter dictos poenitentis actus habet, animi dolor ac detestatio est de peccato commisso, cum proposito non peccandi de caetero."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p266","Chapter IV","This movement of contrition was necessary at any time for obtaining the remission of sins, and in a man fallen after baptism, it then at length prepares him for the remission of sins if it be joined with trust in divine mercy and with the desire of performing the remaining things which are required for rightly receiving this sacrament.","Fuit autem quovis tempore ad impetrandam veniam peccatorum hic contritionis motus necessarius, et in homine post baptismum lapso, ita demum ad remissionem peccatorum praeparat, si cum fiducia divinae misericordiae et voto praestandi reliqua coniunctus sit, quae ad rite suscipiendum hoc sacramentum requiruntur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p267","Chapter IV","Therefore the holy synod declares that this contrition contains not only a cessation from sin and a purpose and beginning of a new life, but also a hatred of the old, according to that: \"Cast away from you all your iniquities by which you have transgressed, and make to yourselves a new heart and a new spirit.\" And certainly whoever has considered the cries of the saints: \"To You only have I sinned, and have done evil before You\"; \"I have labored in my groaning, I will wash my bed every night\"; \"I will recount to You all my years in the bitterness of my soul\"—and other such sayings, will easily understand that they flowed from a vehement hatred of their past life and a great detestation of sins.","Declarat igitur sancta synodus hanc contritionem non solum cessationem a peccato et vitae novae propositum et inchoationem, sed veteris etiam odium continere, iuxta illud: \"proiicite a vobis omnes iniquitates vestras in quibus praevaricati estis, et facite vobis cor novum et spiritum novum.\" Et certe qui illos sanctorum clamores consideravert: \"tibi soli peccavi et malum coram te feci\"; \"laboravi in gemitu meo, lavabo per singulas noctes lectum meum\"; \"recogitabo tibi omnes annos meos in amaritudine animae meae\"—et alios huius generis, facile intelliget eos ex vehementi quodam anteactae vitae odio et ingenti peccatorum detestatione manasse."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p268","Chapter IV","It teaches moreover that although contrition may sometimes be perfected by charity and reconcile man to God before this sacrament is actually received, nevertheless that reconciliation is not to be ascribed to the contrition itself without the desire of the sacrament, which is included in it.","Docet praeterea, etsi contritionem hanc aliquando charitate perfectam esse contingat hominem que deo reconciliare priusquam hoc sacramentum actu suscipiatur, ipsam nihilominus reconciliationem ipsi contritioni sine sacramenti voto (quod in illa includitur) non esse adscribendam."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p269","Chapter IV","But that imperfect contrition, which is called attrition, because it is commonly conceived either from the consideration of the baseness of sin or from the fear of hell and of punishments, if it excludes the will to sin, with hope of pardon, the synod declares not only does not make a man a hypocrite and a greater sinner, but is even a gift of God and an impulse of the Holy Spirit, not yet indeed indwelling, but only moving, by which the penitent, being helped, prepares a way for himself towards justice.","Illam vero contritionem imperfectam, quae attritio dicitur, quoniam vel ex turpitudinis peccati consideratione vel ex gehennae et poenarum metu communiter concipitur, si voluntatem peccandi excludat cum spe veniae, declarat non solum non facere hominem hypocritam et magis peccatorem, verum etiam donum dei esse et spiritus sancti impulsum, non adhuc quidem inhabitantis, sed tantum moventis, quo poenitens adiutus viam sibi ad iustitiam parat."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p270","Chapter IV","And although without the sacrament of penance it cannot by itself lead the sinner to justification, yet it disposes him to obtain the grace of God in the sacrament of penance. For the Ninevites, profitably shaken by this fear, at the preaching of Jonah, full of terrors, did penance and obtained mercy from the Lord.","Et quamvis sine sacramento poenitentiae per se ad iustificationem perducere peccatorem nequeat, tamen eum ad dei gratiam in sacramento poenitentiae impetrandam disponit. Hoc enim timore utiliter concussi Ninivitae, ad Ionae praedicationem, plenam terroribus, poenitentiam egerunt et misericordiam a domino impetrarunt."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p271","Chapter IV","Wherefore, some falsely calumniate catholic writers as if they had handed down that the sacrament of penance confers grace without a good movement of those receiving it, which the church of God never taught nor thought.","Quamobrem falso quidam calumniantur catholicos scriptores, quasi tradiderint sacramentum poenitentiae absque bono motu suscipientium gratiam conferre, quod nunquam ecclesia dei docuit neque sensit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p272","Chapter IV","But they also falsely teach that contrition is forced and coerced, not free and voluntary.","Sed et falso docent contritionem esse extortam et coactam, non liberam et voluntariam."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p273","Chapter V","From the institution of the sacrament of penance, already explained, the whole church has always understood that a full confession of sins was also instituted by the Lord, and is necessary by divine law for all who have fallen after baptism, because our Lord Jesus Christ, about to ascend from earth to heaven, left priests as His own vicars, as presidents and judges, to whom all mortal crimes into which the faithful of Christ may have fallen are to be brought, so that, according to the power of the keys, they may pronounce the sentence of remission or retention of sins.","Ex institutione sacramenti poenitentiae iam explicata, universa ecclesia semper intellexit institutam etiam esse a domino integram peccatorum confessionem et omnibus post baptismum lapsis iure divino necessariam existere, quia dominus noster Iesus christus, e terris ascensurus ad coelos, sacerdotes sui ipsius vicarios reliquit tanquam praesides et iudices, ad quos omnia mortalia crimina, in quae christi fideles ceciderint, deferantur, quo pro potestate clavium remissionis aut retentionis peccatorum sententiam pronuncient."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p274","Chapter V","For it is evident that priests could not have exercised this judgment without a known cause, nor could they preserve equity in imposing punishments, if they had declared their sins only in general and not rather specifically and one by one. Whence it follows that penitents are bound to recount in confession all mortal sins of which, after a diligent self-examination, they have consciousness, even if they are most secret and committed only against the last two precepts of the decalogue, which sometimes wound the soul more grievously and are more dangerous than those which are committed openly.","Constat enim sacerdotes iudicium hoc incognita causa exercere non potuisse, nec aequitatem quidem illos in poenis iniungendis servare potuisse, si in genere dumtaxat et non potius in specie ac sigillatim sua ipsi peccata declarassent. Ex his colligitur oportere a poenitentibus omnia peccata mortalia, quorum post diligentem sui discussionem conscientiam habent, in confessione recenseri, etiamsi occultissima illa sint et tantum adversus duo ultima decalogi praecepta commissa, quae nonnunquam animum gravius sauciant et periculosiora sunt iis quae in manifesto admittuntur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p275","Chapter V","For venial sins, by which we are not excluded from the grace of God and into which we fall more frequently, although they may be rightly and usefully and without any presumption told in confession, as the practice of pious persons shows, may yet be omitted without fault and be expiated by many other remedies.","Nam venialia, quibus a gratia dei non excludimur et in quae frequentius labimur, quanquam recte et utiliter citra que omnem praesumptionem in confessione dicantur (quod piorum hominum usus demonstrat), taceri tamen citra culpam multis que aliis remediis expiari possunt."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p276","Chapter V","But since all mortal sins, even those of thought, make men children of wrath and enemies of God, it is necessary to seek pardon for all also with an open and modest confession.","Verum cum universa mortalia peccata, etiam cogitationis, homines irae filios et dei inimicos reddant, necessum est omnium etiam veniam cum aperta et verecunda confessione a deo quaerere."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p277","Chapter V","Thus, while the faithful of Christ strive to confess all the sins that come to memory, without doubt they lay open all to divine mercy to be forgiven. But those who do otherwise and knowingly conceal some, lay nothing before divine goodness to be forgiven by the priest.","Itaque dum omnia quae memoriae occurrunt peccata christi fideles confiteri student, procul dubio omnia misericordiae divinae ignoscenda exponunt. Qui vero secus faciunt et scienter aliqua retinent, nihil divinae bonitati per sacerdotem remittendum proponunt."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p278","Chapter V","For if the sick person is ashamed to show his wound to the physician, the medicine does not cure what it does not know.","Si enim erubescat aegrotus vulnus medico detegere, quod ignorat medicina non curat."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p279","Chapter V","Moreover, it is gathered that even those circumstances which change the species of sin are to be explained in confession, because without them the sins themselves are neither fully set forth by the penitents nor become known to the judges, and it cannot be that they can rightly judge of the gravity of the crimes and impose the fitting penalty upon the penitents.","Colligitur praeterea etiam eas circunstantias in confessione explicandas esse quae speciem peccati mutant, quod sine illis peccata ipsa nec a poenitentibus integre exponantur nec iudicibus innotescant, et fieri nequeat ut de gravitate criminum recte censere possint et poenam quam oportet pro illis poenitentibus imponere."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p280","Chapter V","Wherefore it is foreign to reason to teach that these circumstances have been devised by idle men, or that only one circumstance is to be confessed, namely that one has sinned against a brother.","Unde alienum a ratione est docere circunstantias has ab hominibus ociosis excogitatas fuisse, aut unam tantum circunstantiam confitendam esse, nempe peccasse in fratrem."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p281","Chapter V","But it is also impious to call the confession which is commanded to be done in this way impossible, or a torture of consciences; for it is evident that nothing else is required in the church from penitents than that, after each has more diligently examined himself and explored all the folds and recesses of his conscience, he confess those sins by which he remembers that he has mortally offended his Lord and God; but that the remaining sins, which do not occur to one thinking diligently, are understood to be included in the same confession in general; for which we faithfully say with the prophet: \"Cleanse me from my secret sins, O Lord.\"","Sed et impium est confessionem, quae hac ratione fieri praecipitur, impossibilem dicere aut carnificinam illam conscientiarum appellare; constat enim nihil aliud in ecclesia a poenitentibus exigi, quam ut postquam quisque diligentius se excusserit et conscientiae suae sinus omnes et latebras exploraverit, ea peccata confiteatur quibus se dominum et deum suum mortaliter offendisse meminerit; reliqua autem peccata, quae diligenter cogitanti non occurrunt, in universum eadem confessione inclusa esse intelliguntur; pro quibus fideliter cum propheta dicimus: \"ab occultis meis munda me, domine.\""],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p282","Chapter V","But the difficulty itself of such confession and the shame of revealing sins could indeed seem grave, if it were not lightened by so many and such great benefits and consolations which are most certainly conferred by absolution upon all who worthily approach this sacrament.","Ipsa vero huiusmodi confessionis difficultas ac peccata detegendi verecundia gravis quidem videri posset, nisi tot tantis que commodis et consolationibus levaretur, quae omnibus digne ad hoc sacramentum accedentibus per absolutionem certissime conferuntur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p283","Chapter VI","Concerning the minister of this sacrament, the holy synod declares that false are all doctrines which perniciously extend the ministry of the keys to any other men besides bishops and priests, thinking that the words of the Lord: \"Whatsoever you shall bind upon earth shall be bound also in heaven, and whatsoever you shall loose upon earth shall be loosed also in heaven\"; and: \"Whose sins you shall forgive, they are forgiven them; and whose sins you shall retain, they are retained\"—were so said to all the faithful of Christ indifferently and promiscuously, contrary to the institution of this sacrament, that everyone has the power of remitting sins: public indeed by correction, if the one corrected acquiesces; secret, however, by spontaneous confession made to anyone.","Circa ministrum autem huius sacramenti declarat sancta synodus falsas esse et a veritate evangelii penitus alienas doctrinas omnes, quae ad alios quosvis homines praeter episcopos et sacerdotes clavium ministerium perniciose extendunt, putantes verba illa domini: \"quaecumque alligaveritis super terram, erunt ligata et in coelo, et quaecumque solveritis super terram, erunt soluta et in coelo\"; et: \"quorum remiseritis peccata, remittuntur eis, et quorum retinueritis, retenta sunt\"—ad omnes christifideles indifferenter et promiscue, contra institutionem huius sacramenti, ita fuisse dicta, ut quivis potestatem habeat remittendi peccata: publica quidem per correptionem, si correptus acquieverit; secreta vero per spontaneam confessionem cuicunque factam."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p284","Chapter VI","It also teaches that even priests who are held by mortal sin, by virtue of the Holy Spirit conferred in ordination, as ministers of Christ exercise the function of remitting sins, and that they think wrongly who contend that this power does not exist in evil priests.","Docet quoque etiam sacerdotes qui peccato mortali tenentur, per virtutem spiritus sancti in ordinationem collatam, tanquam christi ministros, functionem remittendi peccata exercere, eos que prave sentire qui in malis sacerdotibus hanc potestatem non esse contendunt."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p285","Chapter VI","Moreover, although the absolution of a priest is a dispensation of another's benefit, it is not, however, a mere ministry of announcing the gospel or of declaring that sins are remitted, but in the manner of a judicial act, by which sentence is pronounced by him as by a judge.","Quamvis autem absolutio sacerdotis alieni beneficii sit dispensatio, tamen non est solum nudum ministerium vel annunciandi evangelium vel declarandi remissa esse peccata, sed ad instar actus iudicialis, quo ab ipso velut a iudice sententia pronunciatur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p286","Chapter VI","And therefore the penitent ought not so to flatter himself on his own faith that, even if there is no contrition in him nor a serious intention on the part of the priest to act and truly absolve, he should nevertheless think that he is truly and before God absolved on account of his faith alone. For faith without penance would not confer any remission of sins, nor would he be anything but most neglectful of his own salvation who should know that a priest was absolving him in jest and should not diligently seek another who acts seriously.","Atque ideo non debet poenitens adeo sibi de sua ipsius fide blandiri, ut etiamsi nulla illi adsit contritio aut sacerdoti animus serio agendi et vere absolvendi desit, putet tamen se propter suam solam fidem vere et coram deo esse absolutum. Nec enim fides sine poenitentia remissionem ullam peccatorum praestaret, nec is esset nisi salutis suae negligentissimus, qui sacerdotem ioco se absolventem cognosceret et non alium serio agentem sedulo requireret."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p287","Chapter VI","Since, therefore, the nature and reason of judgment demand that sentence be pronounced only on subjects, it has always been persuaded in the church of God, and this synod confirms it as most true, that that absolution is of no effect which a priest pronounces upon one over whom he has not ordinary or subdelegated jurisdiction.","Quoniam igitur natura et ratio iudicii illud exposcit, ut sententia in subditos dumtaxat feratur, persuasum semper in ecclesia dei fuit et verissimum esse synodus haec confirmat, nullius momenti absolutionem eam esse debere, quam sacerdos in eum profert in quem ordinariam aut subdelegatam non habet iurisdictionem."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p288","Chapter VII","It seemed to our most holy fathers to pertain greatly to the discipline of the Christian people that certain more atrocious and more grave crimes be absolved not by any priests but only by the highest priests; whence the supreme pontiffs, by the supreme power entrusted to them in the universal church, have been able to reserve certain more grave cases of crimes to their own special judgment. Nor is it to be doubted (since all things that are from God are ordained) that this same is permitted to all bishops in their respective dioceses, for edification however, not for destruction, by the authority over their subjects handed down to them above the inferior priests, especially with respect to those to which a censure of excommunication is attached.","Magnopere vero ad christiani populi disciplinam pertinere sanctissimis patribus nostris visum est, ut atrociora quaedam et graviora crimina non a quibusvis sed a summis dumtaxat sacerdotibus absolverentur; unde merito pontifices maximi, pro suprema potestate sibi in ecclesia universa tradita, causas aliquas criminum graviores suo potuerunt peculiari iudicio reservare. Nec dubitandum est (quando omnia quae a deo sunt ordinata sunt) quin hoc idem episcopis omnibus in sua cuique dioecesi, in aedificationem tamen non in destructionem, liceat pro illis in subditos tradita supra reliquos inferiores sacerdotes auctoritate, praesertim quoad illa quibus excommunicationis censura annexa est."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p289","Chapter VII","Moreover, it is consonant with divine authority that this reservation of crimes have force not only in external polity but also before God.","Hanc autem delictorum reservationem consonum est divinae auctoritati non tantum in externa politia sed etiam coram deo vim habere."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p290","Chapter VII","Nevertheless, it has been piously preserved in the same church of God, that there be no reservation in the article of death, and therefore that all priests are able to absolve any penitents from any sins and censures; outside that article, however, since priests can do nothing in reserved cases, let them strive to persuade this one thing to penitents, that they go to their superiors and lawful judges for the benefit of absolution.","Veruntamen pie admodum, ne hac ipsa occasione aliquis pereat, in eadem ecclesia dei custoditum semper fuit, ut nulla sit reservatio in articulo mortis, atque ideo omnes sacerdotes quoslibet poenitentes a quibusvis peccatis et censuris absolvere possunt; extra quem articulum sacerdotes, cum nihil possint in casibus reservatis, id unum poenitentibus persuadere nitantur, ut ad superiores et legitimos iudices pro beneficio absolutionis accedant."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p291","Chapter VIII","Finally, concerning satisfaction, which, of all the parts of penance, has been commended to the Christian people by our fathers at all times, and especially is impugned in this our age by those who, having the appearance of piety but having denied its power, under the greatest pretext of piety, the holy synod declares that it is altogether false and foreign to the word of God that the fault is never remitted by the Lord without the whole punishment also being remitted.","Demum, quoad satisfactionem, quae ex omnibus poenitentiae partibus quemadmodum a patribus nostris christiano populo fuit perpetuo tempore commendata, ita una maxime nostra aetate summo pietatis praetextu impugnatur ab iis qui speciem pietatis habent, virtutem autem eius abnegarunt, sancta synodus declarat falsum omnino esse et a verbo dei alienum, culpam a domino nunquam remitti quin universa etiam poena condonetur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p292","Chapter VIII","For clear and illustrious examples are found in the sacred writings by which, besides divine tradition, this error is most manifestly refuted. Indeed the reason of divine justice seems to demand that those who sinned before baptism through ignorance be received into grace in one way; those who, having once been freed from the servitude of sin and of the devil, and having received the gift of the Holy Spirit, knowingly have not feared to violate the temple of God and to grieve the Holy Spirit, in another way.","Perspicua enim et illustria in sacris litteris exempla reperiuntur, quibus praeter divinam traditionem hic error quam manifestissime revincitur. Sane et divinae iustitiae ratio exigere videtur, ut aliter ab eo in gratiam recipiantur qui ante baptismum per ignorantiam deliquerint; aliter vero qui, semel a peccati et daemonis servitute liberati et accepto spiritus sancti dono, scientes templum dei violare et spiritum sanctum contristare non formidaverint."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p293","Chapter VIII","And it befits divine clemency that sins be not so remitted to us without any satisfaction, that, taking occasion, thinking sins lighter, as it were injurious and contumelious to the Holy Spirit, we should fall into more grievous ones, treasuring up for ourselves wrath in the day of wrath. For beyond doubt these satisfactory punishments greatly recall from sin and coerce as with a certain bridle, and make penitents more cautious and vigilant for the future; they also heal the remnants of sins and remove vicious habits acquired by evil living by actions of contrary virtues.","Et divinam clementiam decet, ne ita nobis absque ulla satisfactione peccata dimittantur, ut occasione accepta, peccata leviora putantes, velut iniurii et contumeliosi spiritui sancto, in graviora labamur, thesaurizantes nobis iram in die irae. Procul dubio enim magnopere a peccato revocant et quasi freno quodam cohercent hae satisfactoriae poenae, cautiores que et vigilantiores in futurum poenitentes efficiunt; medentur quoque peccatorum reliquiis et vitiosos habitus, male vivendo comparatos, contrariis virtutum actionibus tollunt."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p294","Chapter VIII","Nor has any safer way ever been thought in the church of God for averting the penalty impending from the Lord than that men frequently perform these works of penance with true sorrow of soul.","Neque vero securior ulla via in ecclesia dei unquam existimata fuit ad amovendam imminentem a domino poenam, quam ut haec poenitentiae opera homines cum vero animi dolore frequentent."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p295","Chapter VIII","To this is added that, while we make satisfaction by suffering for sins, we are made conformed to Christ Jesus, who made satisfaction for our sins, from whom is all our sufficiency, having also a most certain pledge that, if we suffer with Him, we shall also be glorified with Him.","Accedit ad haec quod, dum satisfaciendo patimur pro peccatis, christo Iesu, qui pro peccatis nostris satisfecit, ex quo omnis nostra sufficientia est, conformes efficimur, certissimam quoque inde arrham habentes quod, si compatimur, et conglorificabimur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p296","Chapter VIII","Nor, however, is this our satisfaction which we pay for our sins so our own that it is not through Christ Jesus. For we who from ourselves can do nothing, with Him cooperating who strengthens us, can do all things. Thus man has not whereof to glory, but all our glorying is in Christ, in whom we live, in whom we merit, in whom we make satisfaction, bringing forth fruits worthy of penance, which have their power from Him, are offered to the Father by Him, and through Him are accepted by the Father.","Neque vero ita nostra est satisfactio haec, quam pro peccatis nostris exsolvimus, ut non sit per christum Iesum; nam qui ex nobis tanquam ex nobis nihil possumus, eo cooperante qui nos confortat, omnia possumus. Ita non habet homo unde glorietur, sed omnis gloriatio nostra in christo est, in quo vivimus, in quo meremur, in quo satisfacimus, facientes fructus dignos poenitentiae, qui ex illo vim habent, ab illo offeruntur patri et per illum acceptantur a patre."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p297","Chapter VIII","Therefore the priests of the Lord ought, as the Spirit and prudence suggest, to enjoin salutary and fitting satisfactions according to the quality of the crimes and the ability of the penitents, lest, if they perhaps connive at sins and deal too indulgently with penitents, by enjoining very light works for very grievous crimes, they become partakers of the sins of others.","Debent ergo sacerdotes domini, quantum spiritus et prudentia suggesserit, pro qualitate criminum et poenitentium facultate, salubres et convenientes satisfactiones iniungere, ne si forte peccatis conniveant et indulgentius cum poenitentibus agant, levissima quaedam opera pro gravissimis delictis iniungendo, alienorum peccatorum participes efficiantur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p298","Chapter VIII","But let them have before their eyes that the satisfaction which they impose be not only for the keeping of a new life and a medicine for infirmity, but also for the vengeance and chastisement of past sins. For the ancient fathers both believe and teach that the keys of the priests are given not only for loosing but also for binding.","Habeant autem prae oculis, ut satisfactio quam imponunt non sit tantum ad novae vitae custodiam et infirmitatis medicamentum, sed etiam ad praeteritorum peccatorum vindictam et castigationem; nam claves sacerdotum non ad solvendum dumtaxat, sed et ad ligandum concessas etiam antiqui patres et credunt et docent."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p299","Chapter VIII","Nor for this reason did they think that the sacrament of penance is a forum of wrath or of punishments, just as no Catholic ever thought that by our satisfactions the virtue and satisfaction of our Lord Jesus Christ is obscured or in any part diminished; which the innovators, unwilling to understand, so teach that the best penance is a new life, that they take away all the power and use of satisfaction.","Nec propterea existimarunt sacramentum poenitentiae esse forum irae vel poenarum, sicut nemo unquam catholicus sensit ex huiusmodi nostris satisfactionibus vim meriti et satisfactionis domini nostri Iesu christi vel obscurari vel aliqua ex parte imminui; quod dum novatores intelligere nolunt, ita optimam poenitentiam novam vitam esse docent, ut omnem satisfactionis vim et usum tollant."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p300","Chapter VIII","Moreover, it teaches that so great is the bounty of divine munificence, that we are able to satisfy before God the Father through Christ Jesus not only by punishments voluntarily undertaken by us for avenging sin, or imposed by the priest according to the measure of the offense, but also (which is the greatest proof of love) by temporal afflictions inflicted by God and patiently borne by us.","Docet praeterea tantam esse divinae munificentiae largitatem, ut non solum poenis sponte a nobis pro vindicando peccato susceptis aut sacerdotis arbitrio pro mensura delicti impositis, sed etiam (quod maximum amoris argumentum est) temporalibus flagellis a deo inflictis et a nobis patienter toleratis, apud deum patrem per christum Iesum satisfacere valeamus."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p301","Chapter IX","It has seemed good to the holy synod to add to the preceding doctrine on penance those things which follow concerning the sacrament of extreme unction, which has been esteemed by the fathers not only as the completion of penance but also of the whole Christian life, which ought to be perpetual penance.","Visum est autem sanctae synodo praecedenti doctrinae de poenitentia adiungere ea quae sequuntur de sacramento extremae unctionis, quod non modo poenitentiae, sed et totius christianae vitae, quae perpetua poenitentia esse debet, consummativum existimatum est a patribus."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p302","Chapter IX","First, therefore, concerning its institution, it declares and teaches that our most merciful Redeemer, who wished at all times to have provision for His servants with salutary remedies against all the weapons of all enemies, as He prepared the greatest helps in the other sacraments by which Christians may be able to keep themselves whole while they live from all graver spiritual disadvantage, so He fortified the end of life with the sacrament of extreme unction as with a most firm defense.","Primum itaque circa illius institutionem declarat et docet quod clementissimus redemptor noster, qui servis suis quovis tempore voluit de salutaribus remediis adversus omnia omnium hostium tela esse prospectum, quemadmodum auxilia maxima in sacramentis aliis praeparavit, quibus christiani conservare se integros dum viverent ab omni graviori spiritus incommodo possint, ita extremae unctionis sacramento finem vitae tanquam firmissimo quodam praesidio munivit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p303","Chapter IX","For although our adversary seeks and snatches opportunities through the whole life to devour our souls in any way he can, yet there is no time in which he more vehemently strains all the sinews of his cunning to ruin us utterly and to drive us from trust in divine mercy if he can, than when he sees the end of life approaching us.","Nam etsi adversarius noster occasiones per omnem vitam quaerat et captet ut devorare animas nostras quoquo modo possit, nullum tamen tempus est quo vehementius ille omnes suae versutiae nervos intendat ad perdendos nos penitus et a fiducia etiam si possit divinae misericordiae deturbandos, quam cum impendere nobis exitum vitae prospicit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p304","Chapter IX","Moreover, this holy unction of the infirm was instituted as a true and proper sacrament of the New Testament by our Lord Christ, insinuated indeed in Mark, but commended and promulgated to the faithful by James the apostle and brother of the Lord.","Instituta est autem sacra haec unctio infirmorum tanquam vere et proprie sacramentum novi testamenti a christo domino nostro, apud Marcum quidem insinuatum, per Iacobum autem apostolum ac domini fratrem fidelibus commendatum ac promulgatum."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p305","Chapter IX","\"Is anyone sick among you?\" he says. \"Let him bring in the priests of the church, and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord; and the prayer of faith shall save the sick man, and the Lord shall raise him up; and if he be in sins, they shall be forgiven him.\" By which words, as the church learned from the apostolic tradition received by hand, it teaches the matter, the form, the proper minister, and the effect of this salutary sacrament.","\"Infirmatur\" inquit \"quis in vobis? inducat presbyteros ecclesiae et orent super eum, ungentes eum oleo in nomine domini; et oratio fidei salvabit infirmum et alleviabit eum dominus; et si in peccatis sit, dimittentur ei.\" Quibus verbis, ut ex apostolica traditione per manus accepta ecclesia didicit, docet materiam, formam, proprium ministrum et effectum huius salutaris sacramenti."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p306","Chapter IX","For the church understood that the matter is oil blessed by a bishop; for the unction most fittingly represents the grace of the Holy Spirit with which the soul of the sick person is invisibly anointed; the form, then, is those words: \"Through this anointing and His most tender mercy, may the Lord forgive you whatever you have done wrong,\" etc.","Intellexit enim ecclesia materiam esse oleum ab episcopo benedictum; nam unctio aptissime spiritus sancti gratiam, qua invisibiliter anima aegrotantis inungitur, repraesentat; formam deinde esse illa verba: \"per istam unctionem et suam piissimam misericordiam, indulgeat tibi dominus quidquid deliquisti,\" etc."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p307","Chapter IX","Moreover, the thing and effect of this sacrament are explained by those words: \"And the prayer of faith shall save the sick man, and the Lord shall raise him up; and if he be in sins, they shall be forgiven him.\" For the thing itself is the grace of the Holy Spirit, whose unction blots out sins, if any still remain to be expiated, and the remnants of sin, and raises up and confirms the soul of the sick man, exciting great confidence in divine mercy in him, by which the sick man, being lifted up, bears more lightly the inconveniences and labors of the disease and more easily resists the temptations of the devil lying in wait for his heel, and sometimes, when it is expedient for the salvation of the soul, obtains health of the body.","Res porro et effectus huius sacramenti illis verbis explicatur: \"et oratio fidei salvabit infirmum et alleviabit eum dominus; et si in peccatis sit, dimittentur ei.\" Res etenim haec gratia est spiritus sancti, cuius unctio delicta, si qua sint adhuc expianda, ac peccati reliquias abstergit et aegroti animam alleviat et confirmat, magnam in eo divinae misericordiae fiduciam excitando, qua infirmus sublevatus et morbi incommoda ac labores levius fert et tentationibus daemonis, calcaneo insidiantis, facilius resistit, et sanitatem corporis interdum, ubi saluti animae expedierit, consequitur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p308","Chapter IX","Now, concerning the prescription of those who ought both to receive and to minister this sacrament, that also was not obscurely handed down in the aforesaid words. For it is shown there that the proper ministers of this sacrament are the priests of the church, by which name in that place are to be understood not older men by age or chief men among the people, but either bishops or priests rightly ordained by them through the imposition of the hands of the priesthood.","Iam vero quod attinet ad praescriptionem eorum qui et suscipere et ministrare hoc sacramentum debent, haud obscure fuit illud etiam in verbis praedictis traditum. Nam et ostenditur illic proprios huius sacramenti ministros esse ecclesiae presbyteros, quo nomine eo loco non aetate seniores aut primores in populo intelligendi veniunt, sed aut episcopi aut sacerdotes ab ipsis rite ordinati per impositionem manuum presbyterii."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p309","Chapter IX","It is also declared that this unction is to be applied to the infirm, especially to those who lie so dangerously that they are seen to be in the end of life; whence it is also called the sacrament of the dying.","Declaratur etiam esse hanc unctionem infirmis adhibendam, illis vero praesertim qui tam periculose decumbunt ut in exitu vitae constituti videantur; unde et sacramentum exeuntium nuncupatur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p310","Chapter IX","But if the sick, after receiving this unction, recover, they can be helped by the aid of this sacrament again when they fall into another similar peril of life.","Quod si infirmi post susceptam hanc unctionem convaluerint, iterum huius sacramenti subsidio iuvari poterunt, cum in aliud simile vitae discrimen inciderint."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p311","Chapter IX","Wherefore, by no means are they to be heard who, against so open and clear a sentence of the apostle James, teach that this unction is either a human invention or a rite received from the fathers, having neither the command of God nor the promise of grace; and who assert that it has now ceased, as if it were to be referred only to the grace of healings in the primitive church; and who say that the rite and use which the holy Roman church observes in the administration of this sacrament is contrary to the sentence of the apostle James and therefore to be changed into another; and finally, who affirm that this extreme unction can be despised by the faithful without sin.","Quare nulla ratione audiendi sunt qui contra tam apertam et dilucidam apostoli Iacobi sententiam docent hanc unctionem vel figmentum esse humanum vel ritum a patribus acceptum, nec mandatum dei nec promissionem gratiae habentem; et qui illam iam cessasse asserunt, quasi ad gratiam curationum dumtaxat in primitiva ecclesia referenda esset; et qui dicunt ritum et usum quem sancta Romana ecclesia in huius sacramenti administratione observat, Iacobi apostoli sententiae repugnare atque ideo in alium commutandum esse; et denique qui hanc extremam unctionem a fidelibus sine peccato contemni posse affirmant."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p312","Chapter IX","For all these things most manifestly fight with such clear words of so great an apostle. Nor, indeed, does the Roman church, the mother and teacher of all other churches, observe anything else in administering this unction, as far as pertains to those things which perfect the substance of this sacrament, than what blessed James prescribed. Nor could the contempt of so great a sacrament be without enormous crime and injury to the Holy Spirit Himself.","Haec enim omnia manifestissime pugnant cum perspicuis tanti apostoli verbis. Nec profecto ecclesia Romana, aliarum omnium mater et magistra, aliud in hac administranda unctione (quantum ad ea quae huius sacramenti substantiam perficiunt) observat quam quod beatus Iacobus praescripsit. Neque vero tanti sacramenti contemptus absque ingenti scelere et ipsius spiritus sancti iniuria esse posset."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p313","Canons on the Sacrament of Penance","If anyone says that in the catholic church penance is not truly and properly a sacrament, instituted by Christ our Lord for reconciling the faithful to God as often as they fall into sin after baptism: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit in catholica ecclesia poenitentiam non esse vere et proprie sacramentum, pro fidelibus quoties post baptismum in peccata labuntur ipsi deo reconciliandis a christo domino nostro institutum: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p314","Canon II","If anyone, confounding the sacraments, says that baptism itself is the sacrament of penance, as if these two sacraments are not distinct, and therefore that penance is not rightly called the second plank after shipwreck: let him be anathema.","Si quis, sacramenta confundens, ipsum baptismum poenitentiae sacramentum esse dixerit, quasi haec duo sacramento distincta non sint, atque ideo poenitentiam non recte secundam post naufragium tabulam appellari: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p315","Canon III","If anyone says that those words of the Lord the Savior: \"Receive the Holy Spirit: whose sins you shall forgive, they are forgiven them; and whose sins you shall retain, they are retained,\" are not to be understood concerning the power of remitting and retaining sins in the sacrament of penance, as the catholic church has always understood from the beginning, but twists them, contrary to the institution of this sacrament, to the authority of preaching the gospel: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit verba illa domini salvatoris: \"accipite spiritum sanctum: quorum remiseritis peccata, remittuntur eis; et quorum retinueritis, retenta sunt,\" non esse intelligenda de potestate remittendi et retinendi peccata in sacramento poenitentiae, sicut ecclesia catholica ab initio semper intellexit, detorserit autem, contra institutionem huius sacramenti, ad auctoritatem praedicandi evangelium: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p316","Canon IV","If anyone denies that for full and perfect remission of sins three acts in the penitent, namely contrition, confession, and satisfaction, are required, as the matter of the sacrament of penance, which are called the three parts of penance; or says that there are only two parts of penance, namely the terrors of a stricken conscience when sin is recognized, and the faith conceived from the gospel or from absolution, by which one believes that sins are remitted to him through Christ: let him be anathema.","Si quis negaverit ad integram et perfectam peccatorum remissionem requiri tres actus in poenitente, quasi materiam sacramenti poenitentiae, videlicet contritionem, confessionem et satisfactionem, quae tres poenitentiae partes dicuntur; aut dixerit duas tantum esse poenitentiae partes, terrores scilicet incussos conscientiae agnito peccato et fidem conceptam ex evangelio vel absolutione, qua credit quis sibi per christum remissa peccata: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p317","Canon V","If anyone says that that contrition which is prepared by the discussion, collection, and detestation of sins, by which one recounts his years in the bitterness of his soul, weighing the gravity, multitude, foulness of his sins, the loss of eternal beatitude, and the incurrence of eternal damnation, with the purpose of a better life, is not a true and useful sorrow, nor prepares for grace, but makes a man a hypocrite and a greater sinner; finally, that it is a coerced and forced, not free and voluntary, sorrow: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit eam contritionem quae paratur per discussionem, collectionem et detestationem peccatorum, qua quis recogitat annos suos in amaritudine animae suae, ponderando peccatorum suorum gravitatem, multitudinem, foeditatem, amissionem aeternae beatitudinis et aeternae damnationis incursum, cum proposito melioris vitae, non esse verum et utilem dolorem, nec praeparare ad gratiam, sed facere hominem hypocritam et magis peccatorem; demum illam esse dolorem coactum et non liberum ac voluntarium: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p318","Canon VI","If anyone denies that sacramental confession was instituted or is necessary for salvation by divine law; or says that the manner of confessing secretly to a priest alone, which the catholic church has always observed from the beginning and observes, is foreign to the institution and command of Christ and is a human invention: let him be anathema.","Si quis negaverit confessionem sacramentalem vel institutam vel ad salutem necessariam esse iure divino; aut dixerit modum secrete confitendi soli sacerdoti, quem ecclesia catholica ab initio semper observavit et observat, alienum esse ab institutione et mandato christi et inventum esse humanum: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p319","Canon VII","If anyone says that in the sacrament of penance for the remission of sins it is not necessary by divine law to confess all and each mortal sin of which memory with due and diligent meditation is had, even those which are secret and those which are against the last two precepts of the decalogue, and the circumstances which change the species of the sin; but that such confession is only useful for instructing and consoling the penitent, and was formerly observed only for imposing canonical satisfaction; or says that those who strive to confess all sins wish to leave nothing to divine mercy to be forgiven; or finally, that it is not lawful to confess venial sins: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit in sacramento poenitentiae ad remissionem peccatorum necessarium non esse iure divino confiteri omnia et singula peccata mortalia, quorum memoria cum debita et diligenti praemeditatione habeatur, etiam occulta et quae sunt contra duo ultima decalogi praecepta, et circumstantias quae peccati speciem mutant; sed eam confessionem tantum esse utilem ad erudiendum et consolandum poenitentem, et olim observatam fuisse tantum ad satisfactionem canonicam imponendam; aut dixerit eos qui omnia peccata confiteri student nihil relinquere velle divinae misericordiae ignoscendum; aut demum non licere confiteri peccata venialia: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p320","Canon VIII","If anyone says that the confession of all sins, such as the church observes, is impossible and a human tradition to be abolished by the pious; or that all and each of the faithful of both sexes are not bound to it according to the constitution of the great Lateran council once a year, and therefore that the faithful are to be persuaded not to confess during Lent: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit confessionem omnium peccatorum, qualem ecclesia servat, esse impossibilem et traditionem humanam a piis abolendam; aut ad eam non teneri omnes et singulos utriusque sexus christifideles iuxta magni consilii lateranensis constitutionem semel in anno, et ob id suadendum esse christifidelibus ut non confiteantur tempore quadragesimae: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p321","Canon IX","If anyone says that the sacramental absolution of a priest is not a judicial act, but a mere ministry of pronouncing and declaring that sins are forgiven to the confessing person, provided he only believe that he is absolved, or that the priest absolve not seriously but in jest; or says that the confession of the penitent is not required for the priest to be able to absolve him: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit absolutionem sacramentalem sacerdotis non esse actum iudicialem, sed nudum ministerium pronunciandi et declarandi remissa esse peccata confitenti, modo tantum credat se esse absolutum, aut sacerdos non serio sed ioco absolvat; aut dixerit non requiri confessionem poenitentis, ut sacerdos ipsum absolvere possit: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p322","Canon X","If anyone says that priests who are in mortal sin do not have the power of binding and loosing; or that not only priests are the ministers of absolution, but that it was said to all and each of the faithful of Christ: \"Whatsoever you shall bind upon earth shall be bound also in heaven, and whatsoever you shall loose upon earth shall be loosed also in heaven\"; and: \"Whose sins you shall forgive, they are forgiven them; and whose sins you shall retain, they are retained\"—by the force of which words anyone can absolve sins: public indeed by correction only, if the one corrected acquiesces; secret, however, by spontaneous confession made to anyone: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit sacerdotes qui in peccato mortali sunt potestatem ligandi et solvendi non habere; aut non solos sacerdotes esse ministros absolutionis, sed omnibus et singulis christifidelibus esse dictum: \"quaecumque ligaveritis super terram, erunt ligata et in coelo, et quaecumque solveritis super terram, erunt soluta et in coelo\"; et: \"quorum remiseritis peccata, remittuntur eis; et quorum retinueritis, retenta sunt\"—quorum verborum virtute quilibet absolvere possit peccata: publica quidem per correptionem dumtaxat, si correptus acquieverit; secreta vero per spontaneam confessionem: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p323","Canon XI","If anyone says that bishops do not have the right to reserve cases to themselves, except in respect of external polity, and therefore that the reservation of cases does not prevent a priest from truly absolving from reserved cases: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit episcopos non habere ius reservandi sibi casus, nisi quoad externam politiam, atque ideo casuum reservationem non prohibere quominus sacerdos a reservatis vere absolvat: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p324","Canon XII","If anyone says that the whole punishment is always remitted together with the guilt by God, and that the satisfaction of penitents is nothing other than the faith by which they apprehend that Christ has satisfied for them: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit totam poenam simul cum culpa remitti semper a deo, satisfactionem que poenitentium non esse aliam quam fidem qua apprehendunt christum pro eis satisfecisse: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p325","Canon XIII","If anyone says that for sins, as regards temporal punishment, satisfaction is by no means made to God through the merits of Christ by penalties inflicted by Him and patiently borne, or by those enjoined by the priest, nor even by those spontaneously undertaken, such as fasts, prayers, alms, or other works of piety, and therefore that the best penance is only a new life: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit pro peccatis, quoad poenam temporalem, minime deo per christi merita satisfieri poenis ab eo inflictis et patienter toleratis vel a sacerdote iniunctis, neque sponte susceptis, ut ieiuniis, orationibus, eleemosynis vel aliis etiam pietatis operibus, atque ideo optimam poenitentiam esse tantum novam vitam: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p326","Canon XIV","If anyone says that the satisfactions by which penitents redeem their sins through Christ Jesus are not a cult of God, but traditions of men obscuring the doctrine of grace and the true cult of God and the very benefit of the death of Christ: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit satisfactiones, quibus poenitentes per christum Iesum peccata redimunt, non esse cultus dei, sed traditiones hominum, doctrinam de gratia et verum dei cultum atque ipsum beneficium mortis christi obscurantes: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p327","Canon XV","If anyone says that the keys of the church are given only for loosing, not also for binding, and therefore that priests, when they impose penalties on those confessing, act against the end of the keys and against the institution of Christ, and that it is a fiction that when the eternal punishment is removed by the virtue of the keys, a temporal punishment often remains to be paid: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit claves ecclesiae esse datas tantum ad solvendum, non etiam ad ligandum, et propterea sacerdotes, dum imponunt poenas confitentibus, agere contra finem clavium et contra institutionem christi; et fictionem esse quod virtute clavium, sublata poena aeterna, poena temporalis plerunque exsolvenda remaneat: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p328","Canons on Extreme Unction","If anyone says that extreme unction is not truly and properly a sacrament instituted by our Lord Christ and promulgated by blessed James the apostle, but only a rite received from the fathers or a human invention: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit extremam unctionem non esse vere et proprie sacramentum, a christo domino nostro institutum et a beato Iacobo apostolo promulgatum, sed ritum tantum acceptum a patribus aut figmentum humanum: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p329","Canon II","If anyone says that the sacred unction of the infirm does not confer grace, nor remit sins, nor relieve the infirm, but has already ceased, as if it was formerly only the grace of healings: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit sacram infirmorum unctionem non conferre gratiam, nec remittere peccata, nec alleviare infirmos, sed iam cessasse, quasi olim tantum fuerit gratia curationum: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p330","Canon III","If anyone says that the rite and use of extreme unction which the holy Roman church observes is repugnant to the sentence of blessed James the apostle, and therefore that it ought to be changed, and that it can be despised by Christians without sin: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit extremae unctionis ritum et usum quem observat sancta Romana ecclesia repugnare sententiae beati Iacobi apostoli, ideo que eum mutandum posse que a christianis absque peccato contemni: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p331","Canon IV","If anyone says that the priests of the church, whom blessed James exhorts to be brought to anoint the sick, are not priests ordained by bishops, but older men by age in any community, and for this reason that the proper minister of extreme unction is not a priest alone: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit presbyteros ecclesiae, quos beatus Iacobus adducendos esse ad infirmum inungendum hortatur, non esse sacerdotes ab episcopo ordinatos, sed aetate seniores in quavis communitate, ob id que proprium extremae unctionis ministrum non esse solum sacerdotem: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p332","Decree on the Reformation","Since it is properly the office of bishops to rebuke the vices of all their subjects, this will be especially to be guarded by them, that clerics, especially those constituted over the care of souls, be not criminal, nor lead a dishonest life, with them conniving. For if they permit them to be of depraved and corrupt morals, how will they rebuke laypersons concerning their vices, who could be convicted by one word from them that they suffer clerics to be worse? With what freedom, also, will priests be able to correct laypersons, when they silently answer to themselves that they themselves have committed the same things which they correct? Therefore bishops will admonish their clerics, in whatever order they may be, that in conversation, speech, and knowledge they go before the people of God committed to them, mindful of that which is written: \"Be holy, because I also am holy.\" And according to the voice of the apostle: \"Give no offense to anyone, that their ministry be not blamed; but in all things let them show themselves as ministers of God,\" lest that saying of the prophet be fulfilled in them: \"The priests of God pollute the holy things and make the law void.\"","Cum proprie episcoporum munus sit subditorum omnium vitia redarguere, hoc illis praecipue cavendum erit, ne clerici, praesertim ad animarum curam constituti, criminosi sint, neve inhonestam vitam ipsis conniventibus ducant. Nam si eos pravis et corruptis moribus esse permittant, quo pacto laicos de ipsorum vitiis redarguent, qui uno ab eis sermone convinci possent quod clericos ipsis patiantur esse deteriores? Qua etiam libertate laicos corripere poterunt sacerdotes, cum tacite sibi ipsi respondeant eadem se admisisse quae corripiunt? Monebunt propterea episcopi suos clericos, in quocumque ordine fuerint, ut conversatione, sermone et scientia commisso sibi dei populo praeeant, memores eius quod scriptum est: \"sancti estote, quia et ego sanctus sum.\" Et iuxta apostoli vocem: \"nemini dent ullam offensionem, ut non vituperetur ministerium eorum; sed in omnibus exhibeant se sicut ministros dei,\" ne illud prophetae dictum impleatur in eis: \"sacerdotes dei contaminant sancta et reprobant legem.\""],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p333","Decree on the Reformation","So that the bishops themselves may be able to do this more freely and by no pretext be hindered above, the same sacred, oecumenical, and general Tridentine synod, the same legate and nuncios of the Apostolic See presiding, has judged that the following canons are to be established and decreed.","Ut autem ipsi episcopi id liberius exequi ac quoquam praetextu desuper impediri nequeant, eadem sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis Tridentina synodus, praesidentibus in ea eisdem apostolicae sedis legato et nunciis, hos qui sequuntur canones statuendos et decernendos duxit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p334","Decree on the Reformation","Since it is more honest and safer to serve in a lower ministry by paying due obedience to superiors than to seek the dignity of higher grades with scandal of superiors, to him whose ascent to sacred orders has been forbidden by his prelate for any cause, even on account of a hidden crime, in any way even extrajudicially, or who has been suspended from his orders or grades or ecclesiastical dignities, no license granted against the will of the prelate for promoting himself or for restoration to former orders, grades, dignities, or honors shall avail.","Cum honestius ac tutius sit subiecto debitam praepositis obedientiam impendendo in inferiori ministerio deservire, quam cum praepositorum scandalo graduum altiorum appetere dignitatem, ei cui ascensus ad sacros ordines a suo praelato ex quacunque causa, etiam ob occultum crimen, quomodolibet etiam extraiudicialiter, fuerit interdictus, aut qui a suis ordinibus seu gradibus vel dignitatibus ecclesiasticis fuerit suspensus, nulla contra ipsius praelati voluntatem concessa licentia de se promoveri faciendo aut ad priores ordines, gradus, dignitates sive honores restitutio suffragetur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p335","Decree on the Reformation","And since some bishops of churches situated in the lands of the infidels, lacking clergy and Christian people, since they are almost vagabonds and have no permanent seat, seeking not the things of Jesus Christ but the sheep of others, the own pastor not knowing, while they see themselves forbidden by this holy synod to exercise pontifical offices in another's diocese without the express license of the ordinary of the place and only upon persons subject to the same ordinary, in fraud and contempt of the law, as if with rashness they choose an episcopal see in a place of no diocese, and presume to mark with the clerical character and to promote even to sacred orders of presbyterate any who come to them, even if they have no commendatory letters from their own bishops or prelates, whence it often comes to pass that the less suitable, rude, ignorant, and those rejected by their own bishop as unfit and unworthy are ordained, and can neither perform divine offices nor rightly administer ecclesiastical sacraments: let no bishop who are called titular, even if they reside or stay in a place of no diocese, however exempt, or in any monastery of any order, by the force of any privilege granted to them for promoting any who come to them for the time, be able to promote or ordain a subject of another, even under pretext of continuous familiarity and table fellowship, to any sacred or minor orders or first tonsure, without the express consent or dimissory letters of his own proper prelate.","Et quoniam nonnulli episcopi ecclesiarum quae in partibus infidelium consistunt, clero carentes et populo christiano, cum fere vagabundi sint et permanentem sedem non habeant, non quae Iesu christi sed alienas oves, inscio proprio pastore, quaerentes, dum per hanc sanctam synodum se pontificalia officia in alterius dioecesi nisi de loci ordinarii expressa licentia et in personas eidem ordinario subiectas tantum exercere prohibitos vident, in legis fraudem et contemptum, quasi episcopalem cathedram in loco nullius dioecesis sua temeritate eligunt, et quoscunque ad se venientes, etiam si suorum episcoporum seu praelatorum literas commendatitias non habeant, clericali charactere insignire et ad sacros etiam presbyteratus ordines promovere praesumunt, quo plerunque fit ut minus idonei et rudes ac ignari et qui a suo episcopo tanquam inhabiles et indigni reiecti fuerint ordinentur, nec divina officia peragere nec ecclesiastica sacramenta recte valeant ministrare: nemo episcoporum, qui titulares vocantur, etiam si in loco nullius dioecesis etiam exempto aut aliquo monasterio cuiusvis ordinis resederint aut moram traxerint, vigore cuiusvis privilegii sibi de promovendo quoscunque ad se venientes pro tempore concessi, alterius subditum, etiam praetextu familiaritatis continuae, commensalitatis suae, absque sui proprii praelati expresso consensu aut literis dimissoriis, ad aliquos sacros aut minores ordines vel primam tonsuram promovere seu ordinare valeat."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p336","Decree on the Reformation","He who acts contrary shall be suspended from the exercise of pontificals for a year, and he thus promoted from the execution of the orders thus received, until it shall seem good to his prelate, by the law itself.","Contrafaciens ab exercitio pontificalium per annum, taliter vero promotus ab executione ordinum sic susceptorum, donec suo praelato visum fuerit, ipso iure sint suspensi."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p337","Decree on the Reformation","A bishop shall be able to suspend from the exercise of the orders received for a time, according to his judgment, and to forbid them to minister at the altar or in any order, any of his clerics, especially those constituted in sacred orders, promoted by any authority without his prior examination and commendatory letters, although approved as suitable by him from whom they were ordained, whom he shall have found to be less suitable and capable for celebrating divine offices or administering ecclesiastical sacraments.","Episcopus quoscunque suos clericos, praesertim in sacris constitutos, absque suo praecedenti examine et commendatitiis literis quacumque auctoritate promotos, licet tanquam habiles ab eo a quo ordinati sunt probatos, quos tamen ad divina officia celebranda seu ecclesiastica sacramenta ministranda minus idoneos et capaces repererit, a susceptorum ordinum exercitio ad tempus, de quo ei videbitur, suspendere et illis ne in altari aut aliquo ordine ministrent interdicere possit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p338","Decree on the Reformation","All prelates of churches, who ought diligently to attend to correcting the excesses of subjects, and from whom no cleric, by the statutes of this holy synod, is deemed safe under pretext of any privilege, so that he cannot be visited, punished, and corrected according to canonical sanctions, if they reside in their churches, shall have the faculty of correcting and chastising, even outside visitation, as often and when there shall be need, any secular clerics, however exempt, who would otherwise be subject to their jurisdiction, concerning their excesses, crimes, and offenses, as in this matter delegated by the Apostolic See, any exemptions, declarations, customs, sentences, oaths, agreements (which bind only their authors) by no means availing the same clerics and their blood relatives, chaplains, familiars, proctors, and any others for the consideration and regard of those exempt.","Omnes ecclesiarum praelati, qui ad corrigendos subditorum excessus diligenter intendere debent et a quibus nullus clericus per huius sanctae synodi statuta cuiusvis privilegii praetextu tutus censetur quominus iuxta canonicas sanctiones visitari, puniri et corrigi possit, si in ecclesiis suis resederint, quoscunque saeculares clericos, qualitercunque exemptos, qui alias suae iurisdictioni subessent, de eorum excessibus, criminibus et delictis, quoties et quando opus fuerit, etiam extra visitationem, tanquam ad hoc apostolicae sedis delegati, corrigendi et castigandi facultatem habeant; quibuscunque exemptionibus, declarationibus, consuetudinibus, sententiis, iuramentis, concordiis (quae suos tantum obligent auctores) ipsis clericis ac eorum consanguineis, capellanis, familiaribus, procuratoribus et aliis quibuslibet, ipsorum exemptorum contemplatione et intuitu, minime suffragantibus."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p339","Decree on the Reformation","Moreover, since some who, under the pretext that various injuries and molestations are inflicted upon them concerning their goods, things, and rights, obtain certain judges by letters called conservatory letters, to be deputed, who may protect and defend them from such molestations and injuries, and may hold and preserve them in possession or quasi-possession of their goods, things, and rights, and may not permit them to be molested concerning them, twist such letters, in most cases, contrary to the mind of the one granting, into a reprobate sense; therefore, let letters of conservation, with whatever clauses or decrees and the deputation of any judges, granted under any other pretext or color, by no means avail anyone of whatsoever dignity and condition, even if it be a chapter, for this purpose: that he cannot be accused and sued before his bishop or other superior ordinary in criminal and mixed causes, and that inquisition and proceedings cannot be held against him, or that, if any rights may have belonged to him by concession, he may not be able freely to sue concerning them before an ordinary judge. Moreover, in civil causes, if he himself shall be the actor, let it by no means be lawful for him to draw anyone to judgment before his conservatory judges. But if in those causes in which he shall be the defendant it shall happen that the conservator elected by him is said to be suspect by the actor, or if any controversy concerning the competence of jurisdiction shall have arisen between the conservator judges themselves and the ordinary, the cause is by no means to be proceeded in until it shall have been judged by arbiters elected in the form of law concerning the suspicion or the competence of jurisdiction. As to his familiars who are accustomed to protect themselves by such conservatory letters, they shall profit them nothing except for two only, provided, however, that they have lived at his own expense. No one also may enjoy the benefit of similar letters beyond five years. Nor may conservator judges have any tribunal erected. In causes, however, of salaries or of miserable persons, let the decree of this holy synod concerning this remain in its force. Universities, however, general colleges of doctors or scholars, and regular places, and also hospitals actually keeping hospitality, and the persons of such universities, colleges, places, and hospitals, are by no means comprehended in this present canon, but are altogether to be exempt and to be understood as exempt.","Insuper, cum nonnulli qui sub praetextu quod super bonis et rebus ac iuribus suis diversae eis iniuriae ac molestiae inferantur, certos iudices per literas conservatorias deputari obtinent, qui illos a molestiis et iniuriis huiusmodi tueantur ac defendant et in possessione seu quasi bonorum, rerum ac iurium suorum manu teneant et conservent, neque super illis eos molestari permittant, eiusmodi literas in plerisque contra concedentis mentem in reprobum sensum detorqueant; idcirco nemini omnino, cuiuscunque dignitatis et conditionis sit, etiam si capitulum fuerit, conservatoriae literae cum quibuscunque clausulis aut decretis et quorumcunque iudicum deputatione, quocunque etiam alio praetextu aut colore concessae, suffragentur ad hoc, ut coram suo episcopo sive alio superiore ordinario in criminalibus et mixtis causis accusari et conveniri ac contra eum inquiri et procedi non possit, aut quominus, si qua iura ei ex concessione competierint, super illis libere valeat apud iudicem ordinarium conveniri. In civilibus etiam causis, si ipse actor exstiterit, aliquem ei apud suos conservatores iudices in iudicium trahere minime liceat. Quod si in iis causis in quibus ipse reus fuerit contigerit, ut electus ab eo conservator ab actore suspectus esse dicatur, aut si qua inter ipsos iudices, conservatorem et ordinarium, controversia super competentia iurisdictionis orta fuerit, nequaquam in causa procedatur, donec per arbitros in forma iuris electos super suspicione aut iurisdictionis competentia fuerit iudicatum. Familiaribus vero eius qui huiusmodi literis conservatoriis tueri se solent, nihil illae prosint, praeterquam duobus dumtaxat, si tamen illi propriis eius sumptibus vixerint. Nemo etiam similium literarum beneficio ultra quinquennium gaudere possit. Non liceat quoque conservatoribus iudicibus ullum habere tribunal erectum. In causis vero mercedum aut miserabilium personarum, huius sanctae synodi super hoc decretum in suo robore permaneat. Universitates autem generales ac collegia doctorum seu scholarium et regularia loca, nec non hospitalia actu hospitalitatem servantia, ac universitatum, collegiorum, locorum et hospitalium huiusmodi personae, in praesenti canone minime comprehensae, sed exemptae omnino sint et esse intelligantur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p340","Decree on the Reformation","Since, however, even if the habit does not make the monk, yet it is fitting that clerics always wear clothes suitable to their order, that by the decency of external habit they may show the honesty of internal morals, such great temerity and contempt of religion has grown strong in some today that, esteeming their own dignity and clerical honor lightly, they even publicly wear lay garments, placing their feet in different places, one in divine things, the other in carnal: therefore all ecclesiastical persons, however exempt, who either have been constituted in sacred orders or have obtained any dignities, personates, offices, or benefices of any kind, if after they have been warned by their bishop even by public edict they shall not have worn an honest clerical habit suitable to their order and dignity and according to the ordination and command of the bishop, they may and ought to be coerced by suspension from orders, office, and benefice and from the fruits, incomes, and proceeds of those benefices, and if, having been once corrected, they shall have again offended in this, also by deprivation of such offices and benefices, renewing and amplifying the constitution of Clement V issued in the council of Vienne which begins: \"Quoniam.\"","Quia vero, etsi habitus non facit monachum, oportet tamen clericos vestes proprio ordini congruentes semper deferre, ut per decentiam habitus extrinseci morum honestatem intrinsecam ostendant, tanta autem hodie aliquorum inolevit temeritas religionis que contemptus, ut propriam dignitatem et honorem clericalem parvi pendentes, vestes etiam publice deferant laicales, pedes in diversis ponentes, unum in divinis, alterum in carnalibus: propterea omnes ecclesiasticae personae, quantumcunque exemptae, quae aut in sacris fuerint aut dignitates, personatus, officia aut beneficia qualiacunque ecclesiastica obtinuerint, si postea quam ab episcopo suo etiam per edictum publicum moniti fuerint, honestum habitum clericalem illorum ordini ac dignitati congruentem et iuxta episcopi ordinationem et mandatum non detulerint, per suspensionem ab ordinibus, ac officio et beneficio, ac fructibus, redditibus et proventibus ipsorum beneficiorum, nec non si semel correpti denuo in hoc deliquerint, etiam per privationem officiorum et beneficiorum huiusmodi coerceri possint et debeant, constitutionem Clementis V in concilio Viennensi editam quae incipit: \"Quoniam\" innovando et ampliando."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p341","Decree on the Reformation","Also, he who has killed his neighbor by industry and by ambush ought to be taken away from the altar; he who has voluntarily committed homicide, even if that crime has not been proven by judicial order nor public in any other way but is secret, cannot be promoted to sacred orders at any time, nor may any ecclesiastical benefices, even if they have no cure of souls, be conferred upon him, but let him lack every order, benefice, and ecclesiastical office perpetually.","Cum etiam qui per industriam occiderit proximum suum et per insidias ab altari avelli debeat, qui sua voluntate homicidium perpetraverit, etiam si crimen id nec ordine iudiciario probatum nec alia ratione publicum, sed occultum fuerit, nullo tempore ad sacros ordines promoveri possit, nec illi aliqua ecclesiastica beneficia, etiam si cura non habeant animarum, conferri liceat, sed omni ordine ac beneficio et officio ecclesiastico perpetuo careat."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p342","Decree on the Reformation","If, however, homicide is reported to have been committed not of set purpose but by chance or by repelling force, as when one would defend himself from death, on account of which cause some dispensation is in a manner due by law for sacred orders, for the ministry of the altar, and for any benefices and dignities, let it be entrusted to the ordinary of the place, or from cause to the metropolitan or the nearest bishop, who may dispense only after the cause has been known and the prayers have been proved and the facts set forth, and not otherwise.","Si vero homicidium non ex proposito sed casu vel vim vi repellendo, ut quis se a morte defenderet, fuisse commissum narretur, quam ob causam etiam ad sacrorum ordinum et altaris ministerium et beneficia quaecunque ac dignitates iure quodammodo dispensatio debeatur, committatur loci ordinario, aut ex causa metropolitano seu viciniori episcopo, qui non nisi causa cognita et probatis precibus ac narratis, nec aliter, dispensare possit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p343","Decree on the Reformation","Moreover, some, of whom even some are true pastors and have their own sheep, seek to be over also the sheep of others, and thus sometimes attend to subjects of others that they neglect the care of their own. Whoever, also, endowed with episcopal dignity has had the privilege of punishing subjects of others, is by no means to proceed against clerics not subject to him, especially those constituted in sacred orders, even those guilty of any atrocious crimes, unless with the intervention of the bishop of the clerics themselves, if he resides in his church, or of a person to be deputed by the bishop himself; otherwise, the process and whatever follows from it shall altogether lack force.","Praeterea qui nonnulli, quorum etiam aliqui veri sunt pastores ac proprias oves habent, alienis etiam ovibus praeesse quaerunt, et ita alienis subditis quandoque intendunt ut suorum curam negligant. Quicunque etiam episcopali praeditus dignitate, qui alienos subditos puniendi privilegium habuerit, contra clericos sibi non subiectos, praesertim in sacris constitutos, quorumcunque etiam atrocium criminum reos, nisi cum proprii ipsorum clericorum episcopi, si apud ecclesiam suam resederit, aut personae ab ipso episcopo deputandae interventu, nequaquam procedere debeat; alias processus et inde secuta quaecunque viribus omnino careant."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p344","Decree on the Reformation","And because by best right dioceses and parishes have been distinguished, and to each flock its own pastors have been assigned, and rectors of inferior churches, each having the care of his own sheep, lest the ecclesiastical order be confused, or one and the same church become in a manner of two dioceses, not without grave disadvantage to those who are subject to them, let benefices of one diocese, even if they be parish churches, perpetual vicarages, or simple benefices, or prebends or prebendal portions, even for the sake of increasing divine worship or the number of beneficiaries or any other cause, not be perpetually united to a benefice, monastery, college, or pious place of another diocese; the decree of this holy synod concerning unions of this kind in this declaring.","Et quia iure optimo distinctae fuerunt dioeceses et parochiae, ac unicuique gregi proprii attributi pastores et inferiorum ecclesiarum rectores, qui suarum quisque ovium curam habeant, ut ordo ecclesiasticus non confundatur, aut una et eadem ecclesia duarum quodammodo dioecesum fiat non sine gravi eorum incommodo qui illi subditi fuerint, beneficia unius dioecesis, etiam si parochiales ecclesiae, vicariae perpetuae aut simplicia beneficia seu praestimonia aut praestimoniales portiones fuerint, etiam ratione augendi cultum divinum aut numerum beneficiatorum aut alia quacunque de causa, alterius dioecesis beneficio aut monasterio seu collegio vel loco etiam pio perpetuo non uniantur; decretum huius sanctae synodi super huiusmodi unionibus in hoc declarando."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p345","Decree on the Reformation","Regular benefices, accustomed to be provided in title only to regulars who have made profession, when it shall happen that they become vacant by death or resignation or otherwise of the one holding them in title, are to be conferred only on religious of that order or on those who are bound to receive the habit altogether and to make profession, and not on others (lest they wear a garment woven of linen and wool).","Regularia beneficia, in titulum regularibus professis provideri consueta, cum per obitum aut resignationem vel alias illa in titulum obtinentis vacare contigerit, religiosis tantum illius ordinis vel iis qui habitum omnino suscipere et professionem emittere teneantur, et non aliis (ne vestem lino lana que contextam induant) conferantur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p346","Decree on the Reformation","Since, however, regulars transferred from one order to another are accustomed easily to obtain license from their superior to stay outside the monastery, from which occasion of wandering and apostatizing is given, let no prelate or superior of any order, by virtue of any faculty, be able to admit anyone to habit and profession unless that he remain in cloister perpetually under the obedience of his superior in the order itself to which he is transferred; and let one thus transferred, even if he be of the regular canons, be altogether incapable of secular benefices, even those with cure.","Quia vero regulares de uno ad alium ordinem translati facile a suo superiore licentiam standi extra monasterium obtinere solent, ex quo vagandi et apostatandi occasio tribuitur, nemo cuiuscunque ordinis praelatus vel superior, vigore cuiusvis facultatis, aliquem ad habitum et professionem admittere possit, nisi ut in ordine ipso ad quem transfertur, sub sui superioris obedientia, in claustro perpetuo maneat; ac taliter translatus, etiamsi canonicorum regularium fuerit, ad beneficia saecularia, etiam curata, omnino incapax exsistat."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p347","Decree on the Reformation","Nor may anyone, of any ecclesiastical or secular dignity, by any reason, unless he has newly founded and built a church, benefice, or chapel, or has competently endowed from his own and patrimonial goods one already erected which yet is without sufficient dowry, be able or ought to obtain or acquire the right of patronage. In the case of such foundation or endowment, however, the institution is reserved to the bishop and not to another inferior. Moreover, it shall not be lawful for a patron, under pretext of any privilege, to present anyone to benefices of his right of patronage in any way to a bishop who is not the ordinary of the place to whom the provision or institution of that benefice would by right pertain, the privilege ceasing; otherwise, the presentation and the institution that may follow shall be null and be understood to be null.","Nemo etiam cuiusvis dignitatis ecclesiasticae vel saecularis, quacunque ratione, nisi ecclesiam, beneficium aut cappellam de novo fundaverit et construxerit, seu iam erectam, quae tamen sine sufficienti dote fuerit, de suis propriis et patrimonialibus bonis competenter dotaverit, ius patronatus impetrare aut obtinere possit aut debeat. In casu autem fundationis aut dotationis huiusmodi, institutio episcopo et non alteri inferiori reservetur. Non liceat praeterea patrono, cuiusvis privilegii praetextu, aliquem ad beneficia sui iuris patronatus nisi episcopo loci ordinario, ad quem provisio seu institutio ipsius beneficii, cessante privilegio, iure pertineret, quoquo modo praesentare; alias praesentatio ac institutio forsan secutae nullae sint et esse intelligantur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p348","Decree on the Reformation","Moreover, the holy synod declares that in the future session, which it has already decreed to be held on the 25th day of January of the following year 1552, together with the sacrifice of the mass, it is also to treat and discuss concerning the sacrament of order, and to pursue the matter of reformation.","Declarat praeterea sancta synodus in futura sessione, quam ad 25 diem ianuarii subsequentis anni 1552 habendam esse iam decrevit, una cum sacrificio missae agendum et tractandum etiam esse de sacramento ordinis et prosequendam esse materiam reformationis."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p349","Session 15 — 25 January 1552","Since from that which was decreed in the last sessions, this holy and universal synod has in these days most accurately and diligently treated those things which pertain to the most holy sacrifice of the mass and to the sacrament of order, that in today's session, as the Holy Spirit would have suggested, it might publish decrees concerning these matters and also the four articles pertaining to the most holy sacrament of the Eucharist, deferred at length in this session; and since it thought that those who call themselves Protestants would be present at this sacred council, for whose cause it had deferred the publication of the articles, and that they might come freely and without any delay, it had granted them public faith or safe-conduct; yet since they have not yet come, and it has been besought of this holy synod in their name that the publication which was to have been made today be deferred to the next session, certain hope having been brought that they will be present altogether before that session, a safe-conduct of ampler form having been received in the meantime, the same sacred synod, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same legate and nuncios presiding, wishing nothing more than to remove all dissensions and schisms concerning religion from the most excellent German nation and to consult for its quiet, peace, and repose, ready, if they come, humanely to receive them and benignly to hear them, and trusting that they will come not for pertinaciously impugning the catholic faith but for the study of knowing the truth, and (as befits studious persons of evangelical truth) that they will at length acquiesce in the decrees and discipline of holy mother church, has deferred the next session for publishing and making known those things which are mentioned above to the feast of St. Joseph, which will be the 19th day of the month of March, that they may have sufficient time and space not only for coming but also for setting forth what they wish before that day comes. To whom, that it may remove all cause of longer delay, it freely gives and grants public faith or safe-conduct of the tenor and sentence which will be recited. In the meantime, however, it establishes and decrees that concerning the sacrament of matrimony it is to be treated and defined in the same session, beyond the publication of the superior decrees, and the matter of reformation is to be pursued.","Cum ex eo quod proximis sessionibus decretum fuit, sancta haec et universalis synodus per hos dies accuratissime diligentissime que tractaverit ea quae ad sanctissimum missae sacrificium et ad sacramentum ordinis spectant, ut hodierna sessione, quemadmodum spiritus sanctus suggessisset, decreta de his rebus et quatuor praeterea articulos ad sanctissimum eucharistiae sacramentum pertinentes in hac tandem sessione dilatos publicaret; atque interim affuturos esse putaverit ad hoc sacrosanctum concilium eos qui se protestantes vocant, quorum causa eorum publicationem articulorum distulerat, et ut libere ac sine cunctatione ulla huc venirent, fidem eis publicam sive salvumconductum concesserat; tamen cum illi nondum venerint, et eorum nomine supplicatum huic sanctae synodo fuerit, ut publicatio quae hodierna die facienda fuerat in sequentem sessionem differatur, certa spe allata affuturos eos esse omnino multo ante illam sessionem, salvoconductu amplioris formae interim accepto, eadem sancta synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, iisdem legato et nuntiis praesidentibus, nihil magis optans quam ex praestantissima natione Germanica omnes de religione dissensiones et schismata tollere ac eius quieti, paci otio que consulere, parata ipsos, si venerint, humaniter excipere et benigne audire, confidens que eos non fidei catholicae pertinaciter oppugnandae sed veritatis cognoscendae studio esse venturos, et (ut evangelicae veritatis studiosos decet) sanctae matris ecclesiae decretis et disciplinae ad extremum esse acquieturos, sequentem sessionem ad edenda et publicanda ea quae supra commemorata sunt in diem festum sancti Iosephi, qui erit die decima nona mensis martii, distulit, ut illi satis temporis et spatii habeant non solum ad veniendum, verum etiam ad ea quae voluerint antequam is dies veniat proponenda. Quibus, ut omnem diutius cunctandi causam adimat, fidem publicam sive salvumconductum eius qui recitabitur tenoris et sententiae libenter dat et concedit. Interea vero de matrimonii sacramento agendum et de eo praeter superiorum decretorum publicationem definiendum esse eadem sessione statuit et decernit, et prosequendam esse materiam reformationis."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p350","Session 16 — 28 April 1552","The sacred, oecumenical, and general Tridentine synod, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same legate and nuncios of the holy Apostolic See presiding, although it had decreed in the last session that the present session was to be held today and that further matters were to be proceeded to, nevertheless, because of the absence of the most invincible emperor and of the kings, princes, and nations, and because of the great dangers threatening from the war recently stirred up, the fathers have judged that it is expedient that the further prosecution of the council be prorogated; and it has therefore established and decreed that the council itself be prorogated until the first day of November next following, and that it will prosecute further matters on that day. And it admonishes all who are obliged to be present at general councils that they come on that day.","Sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis Tridentina synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, praesidentibus in ea eisdem apostolicis legato et nuntiis, cum in praecedenti sessione decreverit hodierna die sessionem habendam et ad ulteriora procedendum esse, tamen propter absentiam invictissimi imperatoris et regum, principum et nationum, et propter gravia pericula imminenta ex bello nuper excitato, patribus visum est expedire ut ulterior prosecutio concilii prorogetur; ideoque statuit et decrevit ut concilium ipsum usque ad primam diem mensis novembris proxime futuri prorogetur, eaque die ad ulteriora processurum. Monensque omnes qui interesse debent generalibus conciliis, ut eo die conveniant."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p351","Session 17 — 18 January 1562","Most reverend and illustrious lords, reverend fathers, does it please you that for the praise and glory of the holy and undivided Trinity, Father and Son and Holy Spirit, for the increase and exaltation of the faith and the Christian religion, the sacred, oecumenical, and general Council of Trent, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, from this day which is the 18th day of January of the year from the nativity of the Lord 1562, consecrated to the chair of the Roman see of blessed Peter, the prince of the apostles, all suspension having been removed, is to be celebrated according to the form and tenor of the letters of our most holy lord, Pope Pius IV, the supreme pontiff, and in it, the due order being preserved, those things are to be treated which, to the proposing legates and presidents, shall seem apt and suitable for relieving the calamities of these times, for settling controversies concerning religion, for restraining deceitful tongues, for correcting the abuses of depraved morals, for procuring true and Christian peace for the church?","Illustrissimi et reverendissimi domini, reverendi que patres, placet ne vobis ad laudem et gloriam sanctae individuae trinitatis, patris et filii et spiritus sancti, ad incrementum et exaltationem fidei et religionis christianae, sacrum oecumenicum et generale concilium Tridentinum, in spiritu sancto legitime congregatum, ab hodierno die qui est decimus octavus mensis ianuarii anni a nativitate domini 1562, cathedrae Romanae b(eati) Petri apostolorum principis consecrato, sublata quacumque suspensione, iuxta formam et tenorem litterarum sanctissimi domini nostri Pii IV pontificis maximi celebrari, et in eo, ea debito servato ordine tractari, quae proponentibus legatis ac praesidentibus ad horum temporum levandas calamitates, sedandas de religione controversias, coercendas linguas dolosas, depravatorum morum abusus corrigendos, ecclesiae veram atque christianam pacem conciliandam apta et idonea ipsi sanctae synodo videbuntur?"],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p352","Session 17 — 18 January 1562","Most reverend and illustrious lords, reverend fathers, does it please you that the next future session be held and celebrated on Thursday after the second Sunday of Lent, which will be the 26th day of February?","Illustrissimi et reverendissimi domini, reverendi que patres, placet ne vobis proximam futuram sessionem habendam et celebrandam esse feria quinta post secundam dominicam quadragesimae, quae erit dies 26 mensis februarii?"],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p353","Session 18 — 26 February 1562","The sacred, oecumenical, and general Tridentine synod, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same legates of the Apostolic See presiding, although it has come together for that end not without the particular guidance and governance of the Holy Spirit, to set forth the true and ancient doctrine concerning faith and the sacraments, and to bring a remedy for all heresies and other very grievous disadvantages with which the church of God is now miserably agitated and rent into many and various parts, especially this, even from the beginning, it had in mind to pluck up by the roots the tares of execrable errors and schisms which the enemy man has oversown in these calamitous times in the doctrine of faith, in the use and cult of the most holy Eucharist, which otherwise our Savior left in His church as a symbol of that unity and charity by which He wished all Christians to be joined and bound together among themselves.","Sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis Tridentina synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, praesidentibus in ea eisdem apostolicae sedis legatis, etsi in eum finem non absque peculiari spiritus sancti ductu et gubernatione convenerit, ut veram et antiquam de fide et sacramentis doctrinam exponeret et ut haeresibus omnibus et aliis gravissimis incommodis quibus dei ecclesia misere nunc exagitatur et in multas ac varias partes scinditur remedium afferret, hoc praesertim iam inde a principio in votis habuit, ut stirpitus convelleret zizania execrabilium errorum et schismatum quae inimicus homo his nostris calamitosis temporibus in doctrina fidei, usu et cultu sacrosanctae eucharistiae superseminavit, quam alioqui salvator noster in ecclesia sua tanquam symbolum reliquit eius unitatis et charitatis, qua christianos omnes inter se coniunctos et copulatos esse voluit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p354","Session 18 — 26 February 1562","It has judged that men chosen for this examination concerning the censures and books should diligently consider what is to be done concerning the censures and books, and that they should refer to the same holy synod in its own time.","Censuit ut delecti ad hanc disquisitionem patres de censuris libris que quid facto opus esset diligenter considerarent, atque etiam ad eandem sanctam synodum suo tempore referrent."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p355","Session 18 — 26 February 1562","All these things, however, it wishes to be brought to the knowledge of any persons, as also by the present decree it brings them, that if anyone thinks that anything pertains to him concerning this business of books and censures or concerning other things which it has said are to be treated in this general council, let him not doubt that he will be benignantly heard by the holy synod.","Haec autem omnia ad notitiam quorumcumque deducta esse vult, prout etiam praesenti decreto deducit, ut si quis ad se pertinere aliquo modo putaverit quae vel de hoc librorum et censurarum negotio vel de aliis quae in hoc generali concilio tractanda praedixit, non dubitet a sancta synodo se benigne auditum iri."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p356","Session 18 — 26 February 1562","Since, however, the same holy synod from the heart desires and earnestly prays to God for those things which are for the peace of the church, that all, acknowledging the common mother on earth, who cannot forget those whom she has begotten, may with one mind and one mouth glorify God and the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, by the bowels of the mercy of the same God and our Lord it invites and exhorts all who do not have communion with us to concord and reconciliation, and that they come to this holy synod, and that they embrace charity, which is the bond of perfection, and that they bear before themselves the peace of Christ rejoicing in their hearts, into which they have been called in one body.","Quoniam vero eadem sancta synodus ex corde optat, deum que enixe rogat quae ad pacem sunt ecclesiae, ut universi communem matrem in terris agnoscentes, quae quos peperit oblivisci non potest, unanimes uno ore glorificemus deum et patrem domini nostri Iesu christi, per viscera misericordiae eiusdem dei et domini nostri omnes qui nobis cum communionem non habent ad concordiam et reconciliationem et ut ad hanc sanctam synodum veniant invitat atque hortatur, ut que charitatem, quod est vinculum perfectionis, amplectantur, pacem que christi exsultantem in cordibus suis prae se ferant, in quam vocati sunt in uno corpore."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p357","Session 18 — 26 February 1562","Hearing therefore this voice, not of men but of the Holy Spirit, let them not harden their hearts, nor walking in their own sense nor pleasing themselves, let them be aroused and converted to so pious and salutary an admonition of their mother. For with all offices of charity the holy synod, as it invites them, so it will embrace them.","Hanc ergo, non humanam sed spiritus sancti vocem audientes, ne obdurent corda sua, sed in suo sensu non ambulantes neque sibi placentes, ad tam piam et salutarem suae matris admonitionem excitentur et convertantur. Omnibus enim charitatis officiis sancta synodus eos, ut invitat, ita complectetur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p358","Session 18 — 26 February 1562","Moreover, the same holy synod has decreed that public faith can be granted in a general congregation, and that it will have the same force and be of the same strength and moment as if it had been given and decreed in a public session.","Insuper eadem sancta synodus decrevit fidem publicam in congregatione generali concedi posse et eandem vim habituram eiusdem que roboris et momenti futuram ac si in publica sessione data et decreta fuisset."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p359","Session 18 — 26 February 1562","Likewise, the same sacred Tridentine synod, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same legates of the Apostolic See presiding, has established and decreed that the next future session be held and celebrated on the Thursday after the most sacred feast of the Ascension of the Lord, which will be the 14th day of May.","Eadem sacrosancta Tridentina synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, praesidentibus in ea eisdem apostolicae sedis legatis, statuit et decernit proximam futuram sessionem habendam et celebrandam esse feria quinta post sacratissimum festum ascensionis domini, quae erit quartadecima mensis maii."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p360","Session 19 — 14 May 1562","The sacred, oecumenical, and general Tridentine synod, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same legates of the Apostolic See presiding, makes known to all the faithful... The same sacred, oecumenical, and general Tridentine synod, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same legates of the Apostolic See presiding, gives public faith or safe-conduct to all and each of the others who do not have communion with us in those things which are of faith, from whatsoever kingdoms, nations, provinces, cities, and places in which the contrary of what the holy Roman church thinks is publicly and with impunity preached or taught or believed, under the same form and in the same words in which it is given to the Germans.","Sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis Tridentina synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, praesidentibus in ea eisdem apostolicae sedis legatis, universis fidem facit.... Eadem sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis Tridentina synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, praesidentibus in ea eisdem sedis apostolicae legatis, omnibus et singulis aliis qui nobis cum in iis quae sunt fidei communionem non habent, ex quibuscumque regnis, nationibus, provinciis, civitatibus ac locis in quibus publice et impune praedicatur vel docetur sive creditur contrarium eius quod sancta Romana sentit ecclesia, dat fidem publicam sive salvumconductum sub eadem forma et eisdem verbis quibus datur Germanis."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p361","Session 19 — 14 May 1562","The sacred, oecumenical, and general Tridentine synod, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same legates of the Apostolic See presiding, has judged that the decrees which were to be established and sanctioned in this present session, for certain just and honest causes, are to be prorogated to the Thursday after the next solemnity of the Body of Christ, which will be the day before the Nones of June, and it prorogates them, and appoints that on the said day a session is to be held and celebrated, and commands all.","Sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis Tridentina synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, praesidentibus in ea eisdem apostolicae sedis legatis, decreta ea quae hodie in praesenti sessione statuenda et sancienda erant, iustis nonnullis ac honestis causis, in feriam quintam post proximam solemnitatem corporis christi, quae erit pridie nonas iunii, proroganda esse censuit ac prorogat, dicta que die sessionem habendam esse ac celebrandam omnibus indicit. In the meantime, God and the Father of our Lord, the author of peace, is to be prayed to that He may sanctify the hearts of all, so that the holy synod, with Him helping, may be able both now and always to meditate and perform those things which pertain to His praise and glory."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p362","Session 19 — 14 May 1562","The sacred, oecumenical, and general Tridentine synod, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same legates of the Apostolic See presiding, on account of various difficulties arising from diverse causes, and also that all things may proceed more fittingly and with greater deliberation, namely that dogmas together with those things which pertain to reformation may be treated and sanctioned together, has decreed that those things which shall seem to be established, both concerning reformation and concerning dogmas, are to be defined in the next session, which it appoints for all on the 16th day of the following month of July.","Interea rogandus est deus et pater domini nostri, auctor pacis, ut sanctificet corda omnium, quo adiuvante sancta synodus et nunc et semper meditari ac peragere valeat quae ad eius laudem et gloriam pertineant."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p363","Session 20 — 4 June 1562","This however being added, that the said term, the same holy synod, according to its judgment and will, as it shall think expedient for the affairs of the council, may be able and have power freely to restrict and prorogate, even in a general congregation.","Sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis Tridentina synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, praesidentibus in ea eisdem sedis apostolicae legatis, propter varias difficultates ex diversis causis exortas, atque etiam ut congruentius maiori que cum deliberatione omnia procedant, nempe ut dogmata cum iis quae ad reformationem spectant simul tractentur et sanciantur, ea quae statuenda videbuntur tam de reformatione quam de dogmatibus in proxima sessione, quam omnibus indicit in diem sextam decimam subsequentis mensis iulii, definienda esse decrevit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p364","Session 20 — 4 June 1562","The sacred, oecumenical, and general Tridentine synod, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same legates of the Apostolic See presiding, since concerning the tremendous and most holy sacrament of the Eucharist various monsters of errors are carried about in different places by the arts of the most wicked demon, on account of which in some provinces many seem to have departed from the faith and obedience of the catholic church, has judged that those things which pertain to communion under both species and to infants are to be set forth in this place.","Hoc tamen adiecto, quod dictum terminum, ipsa sancta synodus pro eius arbitrio et voluntate, sicuti rebus concilii putaverit expedire, etiam in generali congregatione, restringere et prorogare libere possit et valeat."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p365","Session 21 — 16 July 1562","Wherefore it forbids all the faithful of Christ that henceforth they dare to believe, teach, or preach concerning them otherwise than is explained and defined in these decrees.","Sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis Tridentina synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, praesidentibus in ea eisdem apostolicae sedis legatis, cum de tremendo et sanctissimo eucharistiae sacramento varia diversis in locis errorum monstra nequissimi daemonis artibus circumferantur, ob quae in nonnullis provinciis multi a catholicae ecclesiae fide atque oboedientia videantur discessisse, censuit ea quae ad communionem sub utraque specie et parvulorum pertinent hoc loco exponenda esse."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p366","Session 21 — 16 July 1562","Therefore the same holy synod, taught by the Holy Spirit, who is the Spirit of wisdom and understanding, the Spirit of counsel and piety, and following the judgment and custom of the church itself, declares and teaches that laypersons and clerics not celebrating are not bound by any divine precept to receive the sacrament of the Eucharist under both species, nor that it can be doubted in any way (faith being preserved) that communion under the species of bread alone suffices for them for salvation.","Quapropter cunctis christifidelibus interdicit, ne posthac de iis aliter vel credere vel docere vel praedicare audeant quam est iis decretis explicatum atque definitum."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p367","Chapter I","For although Christ the Lord instituted this venerable sacrament in the species of bread and wine at the last supper and delivered it to the apostles, nevertheless that institution and delivery do not tend to this, that all the faithful of Christ are bound by the Lord's decree to receive both species. Nor is it rightly gathered from that discourse in John, chapter six, that communion under both species was commanded by the Lord, however it is understood according to the various interpretations of the holy fathers and doctors.","Itaque sancta ipsa synodus, a spiritu sancto qui spiritus est sapientiae et intellectus, spiritus consilii et pietatis edocta atque ipsius ecclesiae iudicium et consuetudinem secuta, declarat ac docet nullo divino praecepto laicos et clericos non conficientes obligari ad eucharistiae sacramentum sub utraque specie sumendum, neque ullo pacto (salva fide) dubitari posse quin illis alterius speciei communio ad salutem sufficiat."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p368","Chapter I","For He who said: \"Unless you eat the flesh of the Son of man and drink His blood, you shall not have life in you,\" also said: \"If anyone eats of this bread, he shall live forever.\" And He who said: \"He who eats my flesh and drinks my blood has eternal life,\" also said: \"The bread which I shall give is my flesh for the life of the world.\" And finally, He who said: \"He who eats my flesh and drinks my blood abides in me and I in him,\" also said: \"He who eats this bread shall live forever.\"","Nam etsi christus dominus in ultima coena venerabile hoc sacramentum in panis et vini speciebus instituit et apostolis tradidit, non tamen illa institutio et traditio eo tendunt, ut omnes christifideles statuto domini ad utramque speciem accipiendam adstringantur. Sed neque ex sermone illo apud Ioannem sexto recte colligitur utriusque speciei communionem a domino praeceptam esse, utcumque iuxta varias sanctorum patrum et doctorum interpretationes intelligatur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p369","Chapter I","Moreover, it declares that this power has always been in the church, that in the dispensation of the sacraments, their substance being preserved, it might establish or change those things which it judged to be more expedient for the utility of the recipients or for the veneration of the sacraments themselves, according to the variety of things, times, and places. Which the apostle did not obscurely seem to intimate when he said: \"Let a man so account of us as of the ministers of Christ and the dispensers of the mysteries of God.\"","Namque qui dixit: \"nisi manducaveritis carnem filii hominis et biberitis eius sanguinem, non habebitis vitam in vobis,\" dixit quoque: \"si quis manducaverit ex hoc pane, vivet in aeternum.\" Et qui dixit: \"qui manducat meam carnem et bibit meum sanguinem, habet vitam aeternam,\" dixit etiam: \"panis quem ego dabo, caro mea est pro mundi vita.\" Et denique qui dixit: \"qui manducat meam carnem et bibit meum sanguinem, in me manet et ego in illo,\" dixit nihilominus: \"qui manducat hunc panem, vivet in aeternum.\""],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p370","Chapter II","And that He Himself used this power is sufficiently evident, since in many other things, so also in this very sacrament, when He arranged certain things concerning its use, He said: \"Other things I will set in order when I come.\"","Praeterea declarat hanc potestatem perpetuo in ecclesia fuisse, ut in sacramentorum dispensatione, salva illorum substantia, ea statueret vel mutaret quae suscipientium utilitati seu ipsorum sacramentorum venerationi pro rerum, temporum et locorum varietate magis expedire iudicaret. Id autem apostolus non obscure visus est innuisse cum ait: \"sic nos existimet homo ut ministros christi et dispensatores mysteriorum dei.\""],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p371","Chapter II","Therefore, recognizing this authority of hers in the administration of the sacraments, holy mother church, although from the beginning of the Christian religion the use of both species was not infrequent, yet in the progress of time, that custom having been already very widely changed, having been induced by grave and just reasons, approved this custom of communicating under one species and decreed that it is to be held as law, which it is not lawful to reject or to change at will without the authority of the church itself.","Atque ipsum quidem hac potestate usum esse satis constat, cum in multis aliis, tum in hoc ipso sacramento, cum ordinatis nonnullis circa eius usum, \"cetera\" inquit \"cum venero disponam.\""],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p372","Chapter II","Moreover, it declares that although our Redeemer, as has been said before, instituted this sacrament in two species at that last supper and delivered it to the apostles, nevertheless it is to be confessed that even under one species alone the whole and entire Christ and a true sacrament are received, and therefore that, with respect to its fruit, those who receive one species alone are not defrauded of any grace necessary for salvation.","Quare agnoscens sancta mater ecclesia hanc suam in administratione sacramentorum auctoritatem, licet ab initio christianae religionis non infrequens utriusque speciei usus fuisset, tamen progressu temporum, latissime iam mutata illa consuetudine, gravibus et iustis causis adducta, hanc consuetudinem sub altera specie communicandi approbavit et pro lege habendam decrevit, quam reprobare aut sine ipsius ecclesiae auctoritate pro libito mutare non licet."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p373","Chapter III","Finally, the same holy synod teaches that infants lacking the use of reason are bound by no necessity to sacramental communion of the Eucharist, since, having been regenerated by the laver of baptism and incorporated into Christ, they cannot in that age lose the grace of the sons of God already obtained. Nor, however, is antiquity to be condemned if it at any time observed that custom in some places.","Insuper declarat, quavis redemptor noster, ut antea dictum est, in suprema illa coena hoc sacramentum in duabus speciebus instituerit et apostolis tradiderit, tamen fatendum esse etiam sub altera tantum specie totum atque integrum christum verum que sacramentum sumi, ac propterea, quod ad fructum attinet, nulla gratia necessaria ad salutem eos defraudari qui unam speciem solam accipiunt."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p374","Chapter IV","For just as those most holy fathers had a probable reason for their action according to the condition of that time, so it is to be believed without controversy that they did this by no necessity of salvation.","Denique eadem sancta synodus docet parvulos usu rationis carentes nulla obligari necessitate ad sacramentalem eucharistiae communionem, siquidem per baptismi lavacrum regenerati et christo incorporati, adeptam iam filiorum dei gratiam in illa aetate amittere non possunt. Neque ideo tamen damnanda est antiquitas, si eum morem in quibusdam locis aliquando servavit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p375","Chapter IV","If anyone says that by a precept of God or by necessity of salvation all and each of the faithful of Christ ought to receive both species of the most holy sacrament of the Eucharist: let him be anathema.","Ut enim sanctissimi illi patres sui facti probabilem causam pro illius temporis ratione habuerunt, ita certe eos nulla salutis necessitate id fecisse sine controversia credendum est."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p376","Canons on the Communion Under Both Species and the Communion of Infants","If anyone says that the holy catholic church was not induced by just causes and reasons to communicate laypersons and also clerics not celebrating under the species of bread only, or that she has erred in this: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit ex dei praecepto vel ex necessitate salutis omnes et singulos christifideles utramque speciem sanctissimae eucharistiae sacramenti sumere debere: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p377","Canon II","If anyone denies that the whole and entire Christ, the fountain and author of all graces, is received under the species of bread alone, because (as some falsely assert) it is not received according to the institution of Christ Himself under both species: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit sanctam ecclesiam catholicam non iustis causis et rationibus adductam fuisse ut laicos atque etiam clericos non conficientes sub una panis tantummodo specie communicaret, aut in eo errasse: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p378","Canon III","If anyone says that communion of the Eucharist is necessary for infants before they have reached the years of discretion: let him be anathema.","Si quis negaverit totum et integrum christum, omnium gratiarum fontem et auctorem, sub una panis specie sumi, quia (ut quidam falso asserunt) non secundum ipsius christi institutionem sub utraque specie sumatur: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p379","Canon IV","The same holy synod, two true articles otherwise proposed but not yet examined, namely: \"Whether the reasons by which the holy catholic church was induced to communicate laypersons and also non-celebrating priests under the species of bread only are so to be retained that by no reason the use of the chalice is to be permitted to anyone\"; and: \"Whether, if the use of the chalice seems to be granted to any nation or kingdom on account of reasons honorable and consistent with Christian charity, it is to be granted under some conditions, and what those are,\" the same holy synod reserves to be examined and defined at another time, as soon as opportunity shall offer itself.","Si quis dixerit parvulis, antequam ad annos discretionis pervenerint, necessariam esse eucharistiae communionem: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p380","Canon IV","The sacred, oecumenical, and general Tridentine synod has established and decreed that the next future session is to be held and celebrated on the Thursday after the octave of the feast of the nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary, which will be the 17th day of the month of September next following. This however being added, that the said term, and each term to be prefixed in the future, the same holy synod, according to its judgment and will, as it shall think expedient for the affairs of the council, may be able and have power freely to restrict and prorogate, even in a general congregation.","Eadem sancta synodus, duos veros articulos alias propositos, nondum tamen excussos, videlicet: \"an rationes quibus sancta catholica ecclesia adducta fuit ut communicaret laicos atque etiam non celebrantes sacerdotes sub una tantum panis specie ita sint retinendae, ut nulla ratione calicis usus cuiquam sit permittendus\"; et: \"an, si honestis et christianae charitati consentaneis rationibus concedendus alicui vel nationi vel regno calicis usus videatur, sub aliquibus conditionibus concedendus sit et quaenam sint illae\", in aliud tempus, oblata sibi quam primum occasione, examinandos atque definiendos reservat."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p381","Decree on the Prorogation of the Session","The sacred, oecumenical, and general Tridentine synod, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, the same legates of the Apostolic See presiding, in order that the ancient, absolute, and in every way perfect faith and doctrine concerning the great mystery of the Eucharist may be retained in the holy catholic church and, errors and heresies having been driven out, may be preserved in its purity, taught by the illumination of the Holy Spirit concerning it in so far as it is a true and singular sacrifice, teaches, declares, and decrees that the following are to be preached to the faithful people.","Sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis Tridentina synodus statuit et decrevit proximam futuram sessionem habendam et celebrandam esse feria quinta post octavam festi nativitatis beatae Mariae virginis, quae erit decima septima mensis septembris proxime futuri. Hoc tamen adiecto, quod dictum terminum, ac unicuique sessioni in posterum praefigendum, ipsa sancta synodus pro eius arbitrio et voluntate, sicuti rebus concilii putaverit expedire, etiam in generali congregatione restringere et prorogare libere possit et valeat."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p382","Session 22 — 17 September 1562","Since under the former testament, (as the apostle Paul testifies), on account of the weakness of the levitical priesthood, there was no perfection, it was necessary (God the Father of mercies so ordaining) that another priest should arise according to the order of Melchisedech, our Lord Jesus Christ, who could perfect and bring to perfection all those who are to be sanctified. He therefore, our God and Lord, although He was about to offer Himself once on the altar of the cross, death intervening, to God the Father, that He might work eternal redemption for them, yet because His priesthood was not to be extinguished by death, in the last supper, on the night He was betrayed, that He might leave to His beloved spouse the church a visible sacrifice (as the nature of men requires) by which that bloody sacrifice once to be accomplished on the cross might be represented, and its memory might remain until the end of the age, and its saving virtue might be applied for the remission of those sins which we daily commit, declaring Himself constituted a priest forever according to the order of Melchisedech, He offered His body and blood under the species of bread and wine to God the Father, and under the symbols of the same things He gave them to the apostles (whom He then constituted priests of the new testament) to receive, and to their successors in the priesthood He commanded that they should offer them, by these words: \"Do this in memory of me,\" and so on, as the catholic church has always understood and taught.","Sacrosancta oecumenica et generalis Tridentina synodus in spiritu sancto legitime congregata, praesidentibus in ea eisdem apostolicae sedis legatis, ut vetus, absoluta atque omni ex parte perfecta de magno eucharistiae mysterio in sancta catholica ecclesia fides atque doctrina retineatur et in sua puritate, propulsatis erroribus atque haeresibus, conservetur, de ea quatenus verum et singulare sacrificium est, spiritus sancti illustratione edocta, haec quae sequuntur docet, declarat et fidelibus populis praedicanda decernit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p383","Chapter I","For having celebrated the old pasch which the multitude of the children of Israel immolated in memory of the departure from Egypt, He instituted the new pasch, Himself to be immolated by the church through priests under visible signs, in memory of His own passage from this world to the Father, when by the shedding of His blood He redeemed us and delivered us from the power of darkness and translated us into His kingdom.","Quoniam sub priori testamento, (teste apostolo Paulo), propter levitici sacerdotii imbecillitatem, consummatio non erat, oportuit (deo patre misericordiarum ita ordinante) sacerdotem alium secundum ordinem Melchisedech surgere, dominum nostrum Iesum christum, qui posset omnes quotquot sanctificandi essent consummare et ad perfectum adducere. Is igitur, deus et dominus noster, etsi semel se ipsum in ara crucis, morte intercedente, deo patri oblaturus erat, ut aeternam illis redemptionem operaretur, quia tamen per mortem sacerdotium eius exstinguendum non erat, in coena novissima, qua nocte tradebatur, ut dilectae sponsae suae ecclesiae visibile (sicut hominum natura exigit) relinqueret sacrificium, quo cruentum illud semel in cruce peragendum repraesentaretur, eius que memoria in finem usque saeculi permaneret atque illius salutaris virtus in remissionem eorum quae a nobis quotidie committuntur peccatorum applicaretur, sacerdotem secundum ordinem Melchisedech se in aeternum constitutum declarans, corpus et sanguinem suum sub speciebus panis et vini deo patri obtulit ac sub earundem rerum symbolis apostolis (quos tunc novi testamenti sacerdotes constituebat) ut sumerent tradidit, et eisdem eorum que in sacerdotio successoribus ut offerent praecepit, per haec verba: \"hoc facite in meam commemorationem,\" et cetera, uti semper catholica ecclesia intellexit et docuit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p384","Chapter I","And since in this divine sacrifice which is performed in the Mass, that same Christ is contained and is immolated in an unbloody manner, who once offered Himself in a bloody manner on the altar of the cross, the holy synod teaches that this sacrifice is truly propitiatory, and that by it, if we approach to God with a true heart and right faith, with fear and reverence, contrite and penitent, we may obtain mercy and find grace in opportune aid.","Nam celebrato veteri pascha, quod in memoriam exitus de Aegypto multitudo filiorum Israel immolabat, novum instituit pascha, se ipsum ab ecclesia per sacerdotes sub signis visibilibus immolandum in memoriam transitus sui ex hoc mundo ad patrem, quando per sui sanguinis effusionem nos redemit eripuit que de potestate tenebrarum et in regnum suum transtulit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p385","Chapter II","For the Lord, placated by this oblation, granting grace and the gift of penance, remits crimes and sins, even great ones. For it is one and the same victim, the same now offering by the ministry of priests who offered Himself then on the cross, the manner only of offering being different. The fruit of this oblation (the bloody one, I say) is most abundantly received through this unbloody oblation; so far is it that this in any way derogates from that.","Et quoniam in divino hoc sacrificio quod in missa peragitur, idem ille christus continetur et incruente immolatur, qui in ara crucis semel se ipsum cruente obtulit, docet sancta synodus sacrificium istud vere propitiatorium esse, per ipsumque fieri, ut si cum vero corde et recta fide, cum metu ac reverentia, contriti ac poenitentes ad deum accedamus, misericordiam consequamur et gratiam inveniamus in auxilio opportuno."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p386","Chapter II","Wherefore, it is rightly offered not only for the sins, punishments, satisfactions, and other necessities of the faithful living, but also for the dead in Christ not yet fully purged, according to the tradition of the apostles.","Huius quippe oblatione placatus dominus, gratiam et donum poenitentiae concedens, crimina et peccata etiam ingentia dimittit. Una enim eadem que est hostia, idem nunc offerens sacerdotum ministerio, qui se ipsum tunc in cruce obtulit, sola ratione diversa. Cuius quidem oblationis (cruentae inquam) fructus per hanc incruentam uberrime percipiuntur; tantum abest ut illi per hanc quovis modo derogetur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p387","Chapter II","And although the church has been accustomed sometimes to celebrate masses in honor and memory of the saints, it does not teach that sacrifice is offered to them, but to God alone who crowned them. Whence neither does the priest usually say: \"I offer sacrifice to you, Peter or Paul,\" but, giving thanks to God for their victories, he implores their patronage that they may deign to intercede for us in heaven whose memory we make on earth.","Quare non solum pro fidelium vivorum peccatis, poenis, satisfactionibus et aliis necessitatibus, sed et pro defunctis in christo nondum ad plenum purgatis, rite iuxta apostolorum traditionem offertur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p388","Chapter III","And since it is fitting that it be administered holily, and this is the most holy of all sacrifices, the catholic church, that it might be offered and received worthily and reverently, instituted the sacred canon many centuries ago, so pure from all error that nothing is contained in it which does not in the highest degree savor of sanctity and piety and raise the minds of those offering to God. For it consists both of the very words of the Lord and of the traditions of the apostles and also of the pious institutions of holy pontiffs.","Et quamvis in honorem et memoriam sanctorum nonnullas interdum missas ecclesia celebrare consueverit, non tamen illis sacrificium offerri docet, sed deo soli qui illos coronavit. Unde nec sacerdos dicere solet: \"offero tibi sacrificium, Petre vel Paule,\" sed deo de illorum victoriis gratias agens, eorum patrocinia implorat, ut ipsi pro nobis intercedere dignentur in coelis, quorum memoriam facimus in terris."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p389","Chapter IV","And since the nature of men is such that it cannot easily be raised to the meditation of divine things without external aids, therefore pious mother church has instituted certain rites, namely that some things in the Mass be pronounced in a subdued voice, others in a louder voice; it has also employed ceremonies, such as mystical blessings, lights, incense, vestments, and many other things of that kind from apostolic discipline and tradition, that by these visible signs of religion and piety the majesty of so great a sacrifice might be commended, and the minds of the faithful might be excited by these visible signs to the contemplation of the most exalted things which lie hidden in this sacrifice.","Et cum sancte administrari conveniat, sit que hoc omnium sanctissimum sacrificium, ecclesia catholica, ut digne reverenter que offerretur ac perciperetur, sacrum canonem multis ante saeculis instituit, ita ab omni errore purum, ut nihil in eo contineatur quod non maxime sanctitatem ac pietatem quandam redoleat mentes que offerentium in deum erigat. Is enim constat cum ex ipsis domini verbis tum ex apostolorum traditionibus ac sanctorum quoque pontificum piis institutionibus."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p390","Chapter V","The sacred synod would indeed wish that at each Mass the faithful who are present should communicate not only spiritually in affection but also sacramentally by the reception of the Eucharist, that the more abundant fruit of this most holy sacrifice might come to them; but it does not, however, if this is not always done, for that reason condemn those Masses in which the priest alone communicates sacramentally as private and illicit, but approves and indeed commends them; since those Masses also ought to be considered truly common, partly because in them the people communicate spiritually, partly because they are celebrated by a public minister of the church not for himself alone but for all the faithful who belong to the body of Christ.","Cum que natura hominum ea sit, ut non facile queat sine adminiculis exterioribus ad rerum divinarum meditationem sustolli, propterea pia mater ecclesia ritus quosdam, ut scilicet quaedam submissa voce, alia vero elatiore in missa pronuntiarentur, instituit, caeremonias item adhibuit, ut mysticas benedictiones, lumina, thymiamata, vestes, alia que id genus multa, ex apostolica disciplina et traditione, quo et maiestas tanti sacrificii commendaretur, mentes fidelium per haec visibilia religionis ac pietatis signa ad rerum altissimarum quae in hoc sacrificio latent contemplationem excitarentur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p391","Chapter VI","Moreover, the holy synod admonishes that it is a precept of the church for priests to mix water with wine in offering the chalice, both because Christ the Lord is believed to have done this, and also because water came forth from His side together with blood, which sacrament is recalled by this mixture; and since in the Apocalypse of blessed John, the waters are said to be peoples, the union of the faithful people with Christ the head is represented.","Optaret quidem sacrosancta synodus ut in singulis missis fideles adstantes non solum spirituali affectu sed sacramentali etiam eucharistiae perceptione communicarent, quo ad eos sanctissimi huius sacrificii fructus uberior proveniret; nec tamen, si id non semper fiat, propterea missas illas in quibus solus sacerdos sacramentaliter communicat, ut privatas et illicitas, damnat, sed probat atque adeo commendat; siquidem illae quoque missae vere communes censeri debent, partim quod in eis populus spiritualiter communicat, partim vero quod a publico ecclesiae ministro non pro se tantum sed pro omnibus fidelibus qui ad corpus christi pertinent celebrentur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p392","Chapter VII","Although the Mass contains great instruction for the faithful people, it has not seemed expedient to the fathers that it be celebrated everywhere in the vulgar tongue. Wherefore, the ancient rite of each church, approved by the holy Roman church, the mother and teacher of all churches, having been retained everywhere, lest the sheep of Christ be hungry, and lest the little ones ask for bread and there be none to break it to them, the holy synod commands pastors and each one having the care of souls that frequently during the celebration of Mass, either by themselves or by others, they explain something of those things which are read in the Mass, and among other things declare some mystery of this most holy sacrifice, especially on the Lord's days and feast days.","Monet deinde sancta synodus praeceptum esse ab ecclesia sacerdotibus ut aquam vino in calice offerendo miscerent, tum quod christum dominum ita fecisse credatur, tum etiam quia e latere eius aqua simul cum sanguine exierit, quod sacramentum hac mixtione recolitur; et cum aquae in apocalypsi beati Ioannis populi dicantur, ipsius populi fidelis cum capite christo unio repraesentatur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p393","Chapter VIII","If anyone says that the Mass does not offer a true and proper sacrifice to God, or that what is offered is nothing other than that Christ is given to us to eat: let him be anathema.","Etsi missa magnam contineat populi fidelium eruditionem, non tamen expedire visum est patribus ut vulgari passim lingua celebraretur. Quamobrem, retento ubique cuiusque ecclesiae antiquo et a sancta Romana ecclesia, omnium ecclesiarum matre et magistra, probato ritu, ne oves christi esuriant neve parvuli panem petant et non sit qui frangat eis, mandat sancta synodus pastoribus et singulis curam animarum gerentibus, ut frequenter inter missarum celebrationem, vel per se vel per alios, ex his quae in missa leguntur aliquid exponant, atque inter cetera sanctissimi huius sacrificii mysterium aliquod declarent, diebus praesertim dominicis et festis."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p394","The Canons on the Sacrifice of the Mass","If anyone says that by those words: \"Do this in memory of me,\" Christ did not institute the apostles priests, or did not ordain that they and other priests should offer His body and blood: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit missa non offerri deo verum et proprium sacrificium, aut quod offerri non sit aliud quam nobis christum ad manducandum dari: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p395","Canon II","If anyone says that the sacrifice of the Mass is only a sacrifice of praise and thanksgiving, or a bare commemoration of the sacrifice performed on the cross, and not propitiatory; or that it profits only the one receiving it, and ought not to be offered for the living and the dead, for sins, punishments, satisfactions, and other necessities: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit illis verbis: \"hoc facite in meam commemorationem,\" christum non instituisse apostolos sacerdotes, aut non ordinasse ut ipsi alii que sacerdotes offerent corpus et sanguinem suum: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p396","Canon III","If anyone says that blasphemy is offered to the most holy sacrifice of Christ performed on the cross by the sacrifice of the Mass, or that it is derogated from by this: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit missae sacrificium tantum esse laudis et gratiarum actionis, aut nudam commemorationem sacrificii in cruce peracti, non autem propitiatorium; vel soli prodesse sumenti, neque pro vivis et defunctis, pro peccatis, poenis, satisfactionibus et aliis necessitatibus offerri debere: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p397","Canon IV","If anyone says that it is an imposture to celebrate Masses in honor of the saints and for obtaining their intercession with God, as the church intends: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit blasphemiam irrogari sanctissimo christi sacrificio in cruce peracto per missae sacrificium, aut illi per hoc derogari: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p398","Canon V","If anyone says that the canon of the Mass contains errors, and therefore that it is to be abolished: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit imposturam esse missas celebrari in honorem sanctorum et pro illorum intercessione apud deum obtinenda, sicut ecclesia intendit: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p399","Canon VI","If anyone says that the ceremonies, vestments, and outward signs which the catholic church uses in the celebration of Mass are incitements to impiety rather than offices of piety: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit canonem missae errores continere, ideo que abrogandum esse: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p400","Canon VII","If anyone says that Masses in which the priest alone communicates sacramentally are illicit and therefore to be abolished: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit caeremonias, vestes et externa signa quibus in missarum celebratione ecclesia catholica utitur, irritabula impietatis esse magis quam officia pietatis: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p401","Canon VIII","If anyone says that the rite of the Roman church, in which part of the canon and the words of consecration are pronounced in a subdued voice, is to be condemned; or that the Mass ought to be celebrated in the vulgar tongue only; or that water ought not to be mixed with the wine to be offered in the chalice, because it is against the institution of Christ: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit missas in quibus solus sacerdos sacramentaliter communicat illicitas esse, ideo que abrogandas: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p402","Canon IX","How great care ought to be taken that the most holy sacrifice of the Mass be celebrated with all religious cult and veneration, anyone can easily estimate who has considered that in the sacred writings he is called cursed who does the work of God negligently. If we must necessarily confess that no other work can be treated by the faithful of Christ so holy and divine as this tremendous mystery, in which that life-giving victim by which we were reconciled to God the Father is daily immolated on the altar by priests, it is sufficiently evident also that all effort and diligence ought to be used in it, that it be performed with the greatest possible interior purity of heart and exterior devotion and piety.","Si quis dixerit ecclesiae Romanae ritum, quo submissa voce pars canonis et verba consecrationis proferuntur, damnandum esse; aut lingua tantum vulgari missam celebrari debere; aut aquam non miscendam esse vino in calice offerendo, eo quod sit contra christi institutionem: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p403","Decree on the Reformation","Since therefore many things seem to have crept in, whether through the vice of the times or through the negligence and wickedness of men, which are alien to the dignity of so great a sacrifice, that due honor and cult may be restored to the glory of God and the edification of the faithful people, the holy synod decrees that the ordinaries of places, the bishops, diligently take care and be bound to prohibit and remove from the midst all those things which either avarice (the servitude of idols), or irreverence (which can scarcely be separated from impiety), or superstition (the false imitator of true piety), have introduced.","Quanta cura adhibenda sit ut sacrosanctum missae sacrificium omni religionis cultu ac veneratione celebretur, quivis facile existimare poterit qui cogitarit maledictum in sacris litteris eum vocari qui facit opus dei negligenter. Quodsi necessario fatemur nullum aliud opus adeo sanctum ac divinum a christifidelibus tractari posse quam hoc ipsum tremendum mysterium, quo vivifica illa hostia qua deo patri reconciliati sumus in altari per sacerdotes quotidie immolatur, satis etiam apparet omnem operam et diligentiam in eo ponendam esse, ut quanta maxima fieri potest interiori cordis munditia et puritate atque exterioris devotionis ac pietatis specie peragatur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p404","Decree on the Reformation","And that many things may be included in a few, first with respect to avarice, the conditions of wages of any kind, pacts, and whatever is given for celebrating new Masses, and also importunate and illiberal exactions of alms rather than requests, and other such things which are not far from simoniacal stain or certainly from filthy gain, let them altogether forbid.","Cum igitur multa iam sive temporum vitio sive hominum incuria et improbitate irrepsisse videantur quae a tanti sacrificii dignitate aliena sunt, ut et debitus honor et cultus ad dei gloriam et fidelis populi aedificationem restituatur, decernit sancta synodus ut ordinarii locorum episcopi ea omnia prohibere atque e medio tollere sedulo curent ac teneantur quae vel avaritia (idolorum servitus), vel irreverentia (quae ab impietate vix seiuncta esse potest), vel superstitio (verae pietatis falsa imitatrix), induxit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p405","Decree on the Reformation","Then, that irreverence may be avoided, let each one in his own diocese forbid that it be lawful for any wandering and unknown priest to celebrate Masses. Moreover, let them not permit anyone who is publicly and notoriously criminal to minister at the holy altar or to be present at sacred things, nor suffer that this holy sacrifice be performed in private houses and altogether outside the church and the oratories dedicated to divine worship only, to be designated and visited by the same ordinaries, by any secular or regular priests, unless those who are present first declare that they are present with their mind and devout affection of heart, not only with their body, having decently composed the habit of their body.","Atque ut multa paucis comprehendantur, in primis quod ad avaritiam pertinet, cuiusvis generis mercedum conditiones, pacta et quidquid pro missis novis celebrandis datur, necnon importunas atque illiberales eleemosynarum exactiones potius quam postulationes, alia que huiusmodi quae a simoniaca labe vel certe a turpi quaestu non longe absunt, omnino prohibeant."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p406","Decree on the Reformation","From churches, moreover, let them banish those musical practices in which either organ or singing mixes anything lascivious or impure; likewise all secular actions, vain and indeed profane conversations, walking about, noise, clamor, that the house of God may truly seem and be able to be called the house of prayer.","Deinde, ut irreverentia vitetur, singuli in suis dioecesibus interdicant ne cui vago et ignoto sacerdoti missas celebrare liceat. Neminem praeterea cui publice et notorie criminosus sit aut sancto altari ministrare aut sacris interesse permittant, neve patiantur privatis in domibus atque omnino extra ecclesiam et ad divinum tantum cultum dedicata oratoria, ab eisdem ordinariis designanda et visitanda, sanctum hoc sacrificium a saecularibus aut regularibus quibuscumque peragi, ac nisi prius qui intersint decenter composito corporis habitu declaraverint se mente etiam ac devoto cordis affectu non solum corpore adesse."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p407","Decree on the Reformation","Finally, that no place be given to superstition, let them take care by edict and proposed penalties that priests celebrate not at other than the due hours, nor that they use other rites or other ceremonies and prayers in the celebration of Mass besides those which have been approved by the church and received by frequent and laudable use.","Ab ecclesiis vero musicas eas ubi sive organo sive cantu lascivum aut impurum aliquid miscetur, item saeculares omnes actiones, vana atque adeo profana colloquia, deambulationes, strepitus, clamores arceant, ut domus dei vere domus orationis esse videatur ac dici possit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p408","Decree on the Reformation","Let them altogether remove from the church a certain number of masses and candles, which has been invented more from superstitious cult than from true religion, and let them teach the people what is and from whom especially proceeds this so precious and heavenly fruit of this most holy sacrifice.","Postremo, ne superstitioni locus aliquis detur, edicto et poenis propositis caveant ne sacerdotes aliis quam debitis horis celebrent, neve ritus alios aut alias caeremonias et preces in missarum celebratione adhibeant praeter eas quae ab ecclesia probatae ac frequenti et laudabili usu receptae fuerint."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p409","Decree on the Reformation","Let them also admonish the same people that they frequently come to their own parishes, at least on the Lord's days and the greater feasts.","Quarundam vero missarum et candelarum certum numerum, qui magis a superstitioso cultu quam a vera religione inventus est, omnino ab ecclesia removeant, doceant que populum quis sit et a quo potissimum proveniat huius sanctissimi sacrificii tam pretiosus ac coelestis fructus."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p410","Decree on the Reformation","All these things, therefore, which have been enumerated summarily, are so set forth to all ordinaries of places that not only those same things, but whatever other things shall seem to pertain to this, they themselves, by the power given to them by the sacred synod and also as delegates of the Apostolic See, may prohibit, command, correct, establish, and by ecclesiastical censures and other penalties which shall be determined by their judgment, compel the faithful people to observe them inviolably, any privileges, exemptions, appeals, and customs whatsoever notwithstanding.","Moneant etiam eundem populum ut frequenter ad suas parochias, saltem diebus dominicis et maioribus festis, accedant."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p411","Decree on the Reformation","The sacrifice and priesthood are so conjoined by the ordinance of God that both have existed in every law. Since therefore in the New Testament the catholic church has received the visible sacrifice of the holy Eucharist from the institution of the Lord, it must also be confessed that there is in it a new, visible, and external priesthood, into which the old has been translated.","Haec igitur omnia quae summatim enumerata sunt omnibus locorum ordinariis ita proponuntur ut non solum ea ipsa, sed quaecumque alia huc pertinere visa fuerint, ipsi pro data sibi a sacrosancta synodo potestate, ac etiam ut delegati sedis apostolicae, prohibeant, mandent, corrigant, statuant, atque ad ea inviolate servanda censuris ecclesiasticis aliis que poenis quae illorum arbitrio constituentur, fidelem populum compellant, non obstantibus privilegiis, exemptionibus, appellationibus et consuetudinibus quibuscumque."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p412","Session 23 — 15 July 1563","Moreover, the sacred scriptures show, and the tradition of the catholic church has always taught, that this priesthood was instituted by the same Lord our Savior, and that the power of consecrating, offering, and ministering His body and blood, as well as of remitting and retaining sins, was handed down to the apostles and their successors in the priesthood.","Sacrificium et sacerdotium ita dei ordinatione coniuncta sunt, ut utrumque in omni lege exsisterit. Cum igitur in novo testamento sanctum eucharistiae sacrificium visibile ex domini institutione catholica ecclesia acceperit, fateri etiam oportet in ea novum esse visibile et externum sacerdotium, in quod vetus translatum est."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p413","Session 23 — 15 July 1563","But since the ministry of so holy a priesthood is a divine matter, it was fitting that, so that it might be exercised more worthily and with greater veneration, there should be in the most ordered disposition of the church many and diverse orders of ministers who serve the priesthood by office, so distributed that those who are already marked with the clerical tonsure might ascend through the minor to the major orders.","Hoc autem ab eodem domino salvatore nostro institutum esse atque apostolis eorum que successoribus in sacerdotio potestatem traditam consecrandi, offerendi et ministrandi corpus et sanguinem eius, nec non et peccata dimittendi et retinendi, sacrae litterae ostendunt et catholicae ecclesiae traditio semper docuit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p414","Session 23 — 15 July 1563","For the sacred scriptures make open mention not only of priests but also of deacons, and teach in most grave words what is especially to be observed in their ordination; and from the very beginning of the church, the names of the following orders and the proper ministries of each, namely subdeacons, acolytes, exorcists, readers, and doorkeepers, are known to have been in use, although not of equal grade. For subdiaconate is referred by the fathers and sacred councils to the major orders, in which also we read very frequently concerning the other minor orders.","Cum autem divina res sit tam sancti sacerdotii ministerium, consentaneum fuit, quo dignius et maiori cum veneratione exerceri posset, ut in ecclesiae ordinatissima dispositione plures et diversi essent ministrorum ordines, qui sacerdotio ex officio deservirent, ita distributi, ut qui iam clericali tonsura insigniti essent, per minores ad maiores ascenderent."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p415","Session 23 — 15 July 1563","Since it is clear from the testimony of scripture, apostolic tradition, and the unanimous consent of the fathers that by sacred ordination, which is effected by words and outward signs, grace is conferred, no one ought to doubt that order is truly and properly one of the seven sacraments of the holy church. For the apostle says: \"I admonish you to stir up the grace of God which is in you by the imposition of my hands.\" For \"God has not given us the spirit of fear, but of power and of love and of sobriety.\"","Nam non solum de sacerdotibus sed et de diaconis sacrae litterae apertam mentionem faciunt, et quae maxime in illorum ordinatione attendenda sunt, gravissimis verbis docent; et ab ipso ecclesiae initio sequentium ordinum nomina atque uniuscuiusque eorum propria ministeria, subdiaconi scilicet, acolythi, exorcistae, lectoris et ostiarii, in usu fuisse cognoscuntur, quamvis non pari gradu. Nam subdiaconatus ad maiores ordines a patribus et sacris conciliis refertur, in quibus et de aliis inferioribus frequentissime legimus."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p416","Session 23 — 15 July 1563","Since, however, in the sacrament of order, as also in baptism and confirmation, a character is imprinted which can neither be destroyed nor taken away, the holy synod rightly condemns the opinion of those who say that the priests of the New Testament have only temporary power, and that once rightly ordained they can again become laymen if they do not exercise the ministry of the word of God.","Cum scripturae testimonio, apostolica traditione et patrum unanimi consensu perspicuum sit per sacram ordinationem, quae verbis et signis exterioribus perficitur, gratiam conferri, dubitare nemo debet ordinem esse vere et proprie unum ex septem sanctae ecclesiae sacramentis. Inquit enim apostolus: \"admoneo te ut resuscites gratiam dei quae est in te per impositionem manuum mearum.\" \"Non enim dedit nobis deus spiritum timoris, sed virtutis et dilectionis et sobrietatis.\""],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p417","Session 23 — 15 July 1563","But if anyone affirms that all Christians indiscriminately are priests of the New Testament, or that all are endowed with equal spiritual power among themselves, he seems to do nothing else than confound the ecclesiastical hierarchy, which is as an army set in order, just as if against the teaching of blessed Paul \"all are apostles, all prophets, all evangelists, all pastors, all are teachers.\"","Quoniam vero in sacramento ordinis, sicut et in baptismo et confirmatione, character imprimitur, qui nec deleri nec auferri potest, merito sancta synodus damnat eorum sententiam, qui asserunt novi testamenti sacerdotes temporariam tantummodo potestatem habere et semel rite ordinatos iterum laicos effici posse, si verbi dei ministerium non exerceant."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p418","Session 23 — 15 July 1563","Therefore the holy synod declares that besides the other ecclesiastical grades, bishops, who have succeeded in the place of the apostles, especially pertain to this hierarchical order, and that they are placed (as the same apostle says) by the Holy Spirit to rule the church of God, that they are superior to priests, and that they can confer the sacrament of confirmation, ordain ministers of the church, and perform many other functions, of which the rest of the lower orders have no power.","Quodsi quis omnes christianos promiscue novi testamenti sacerdotes esse, aut omnes pari inter se potestate spirituali praeditos affirmet, nihil aliud facere videtur quam ecclesiasticam hierarchiam, quae est \"ut castrorum acies ordinata,\" confundere, perinde ac si contra beati Pauli doctrinam \"omnes apostoli, omnes prophetae, omnes evangelistae, omnes pastores, omnes sint doctores.\""],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p419","Session 23 — 15 July 1563","Moreover, the holy synod teaches that in the ordination of bishops, priests, and the other orders, neither the consent nor the vocation or authority of the people, or of any secular power and magistrate, is so required that without it the ordination is void; rather it decrees that those who are only called and instituted by the people, or by secular power and magistrate, to ascend to these ministries to be exercised, and who take them to themselves by their own rashness, are all to be held not as ministers of the church but as thieves and robbers who have not entered by the door.","Proinde sancta synodus declarat praeter ceteros ecclesiasticos gradus, episcopos qui in apostolorum locum successerunt, ad hunc hierarchicum ordinem praecipue pertinere et positos (sicut idem apostolus ait) a spiritu sancto regere ecclesiam dei, eos que presbyteris superiores esse, ac sacramentum confirmationis conferre, ministros ecclesiae ordinare atque alia pleraque peragere ipsos posse, quarum functionum potestatem reliqui inferioris ordinis nullam habent."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p420","Session 23 — 15 July 1563","These are the things which in general it has seemed good to the sacred synod to teach the faithful of Christ concerning the sacrament of order. But it has determined to condemn those things which are contrary to these in certain and proper canons in the following manner, that all, using the rule of faith with Christ's help, may more easily recognize and hold the catholic truth in so many errors of darkness.","Docet insuper sancta synodus in ordinatione episcoporum, sacerdotum et ceterorum ordinum, nec populi nec cuiusvis saecularis potestatis et magistratus consensum sive vocationem sive auctoritatem ita requiri, ut sine ea irrita sit ordinatio; quin potius decernit eos qui tantummodo a populo aut saeculari potestate ac magistratu vocati et instituti ad haec ministeria exercenda ascendunt, et qui ea propria temeritate sibi sumunt, omnes non ecclesiae ministros sed fures et latrones per ostium non ingressos habendos esse."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p421","Session 23 — 15 July 1563","If anyone says that there is not in the New Testament a visible and external priesthood, or that there is no power of consecrating and offering the true body and blood of the Lord and of remitting and retaining sins, but only an office and bare ministry of preaching the gospel; or that those who do not preach are not priests at all: let him be anathema.","Haec sunt quae generatim sacrae synodo visum est christifideles de sacramento ordinis docere. His autem contraria certis et propriis canonibus in hunc qui sequitur modum damnare constituit, ut omnes, adiuvante christo, fidei regula utentes in tot errorum tenebris catholicam veritatem facilius agnoscere et tenere possint."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p422","The Canons on the Sacrament of Order","If anyone says that besides the priesthood there are not in the catholic church other orders, both greater and minor, by which as by certain steps one tends toward the priesthood: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit non esse in novo testamento sacerdotium visibile et externum, vel non esse potestatem aliquam consecrandi et offerendi verum corpus et sanguinem domini et peccata remittendi et retinendi, sed officium tantum et nudum ministerium praedicandi evangelium; vel eos qui non praedicant prorsus non esse sacerdotes: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p423","Canon II","If anyone says that order or sacred ordination is not truly and properly a sacrament instituted by Christ the Lord, or that it is some human fiction devised by men ignorant of ecclesiastical matters, or that it is only a certain rite of choosing ministers of the word of God and of the sacraments: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit praeter sacerdotium non esse in ecclesia catholica alios ordines, et maiores et minores, per quos velut per gradus quosdam in sacerdotium tendatur: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p424","Canon III","If anyone says that by sacred ordination the Holy Spirit is not given, and that therefore the bishops say in vain: \"Receive the Holy Spirit\"; or that by it no character is imprinted; or that he who has once been a priest can become a layman again: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit ordinem sive sacram ordinationem non esse vere et proprie sacramentum a christo domino institutum, vel esse figmentum quoddam humanum excogitatum a viris rerum ecclesiasticarum imperitis, aut esse tantum ritum quendam eligendi ministros verbi dei et sacramentorum: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p425","Canon IV","If anyone says that the sacred unction which the church uses in holy ordination is not only not required, but is to be despised and is pernicious; likewise the other ceremonies of order: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit per sacram ordinationem non dari spiritum sanctum, ac proinde frustra episcopos dicere: \"accipe spiritum sanctum\"; aut per eam non imprimi characterem; vel eum qui sacerdos semel fuit laicum rursus fieri posse: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p426","Canon V","If anyone says that there is not in the catholic church a hierarchy instituted by divine ordination, which consists of bishops, priests, and ministers: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit sacram unctionem qua ecclesia in sancta ordinatione utitur non tantum non requiri, sed contemnendam et perniciosam esse, similiter et alias ordinis caeremonias: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p427","Canon VI","If anyone says that bishops are not superior to priests, or that they do not have the power of confirming and ordaining, or that the power which they have is common to them with priests; or that orders conferred by them without the consent or vocation of the people or of secular power are void; or that those who are neither rightly ordained nor sent by ecclesiastical and canonical power, but come from elsewhere, are legitimate ministers of the word and the sacraments: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit in ecclesia catholica non esse hierarchiam divina ordinatione institutam, quae constat ex episcopis, presbyteris et ministris: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p428","Canon VII","If anyone says that bishops who are assumed by the authority of the Roman pontiff are not legitimate and true bishops, but a human fiction: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit episcopos non esse presbyteris superiores, vel non habere potestatem confirmandi et ordinandi, vel eam quam habent illis esse cum presbyteris communem; aut ordines ab ipsis collatos sine populi vel potestatis saecularis consensu aut vocatione irritos esse; aut eos qui nec ab ecclesiastica et canonica potestate rite ordinati nec missi sunt, sed aliunde veniunt, legitimos esse verbi et sacramentorum ministros: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p429","Canon VIII","The same sacred Tridentine synod, pursuing the matter of reformation, has established and decrees that the following be decreed in the present session.","Si quis dixerit episcopos qui auctoritate Romani pontificis assumuntur non esse legitimos et veros episcopos, sed figmentum humanum: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p430","Decree on the Reformation","Since it is commanded by divine precept to all to whom the care of souls is committed to know their sheep, to offer sacrifice for them, to feed them by the preaching of the divine word, the administration of the sacraments, and the example of all good works, to bear paternal care for the poor and other miserable persons, and to attend to the other pastoral duties, all of which cannot be performed and fulfilled by those who do not watch over their flock nor attend to it, but desert it after the manner of hirelings, the sacred synod admonishes and exhorts them, mindful of the divine precepts and made a form of the flock, to feed and rule in judgment and truth.","Eadem sacrosancta Tridentina synodus, reformationis materiam prosequens, haec quae sequuntur in praesenti decernenda esse statuit ac decernit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p431","Decree on the Reformation","Lest, however, those things which were holily and usefully previously enacted concerning residence under Paul III of happy memory be twisted into a sense alien from the mind of the sacred synod, as if by the force of that decree it were lawful to be absent for five continuous months, adhering to them, the sacred synod declares that all placed over patriarchal, primatial, metropolitan, and cathedral churches, under whatsoever name and title, even if they are cardinals of the holy Roman church, are bound to personal residence in their own church or diocese where they are bound to perform the office enjoined upon them, nor can they be absent except for the causes and in the manners written below.","Cum praecepto divino mandatum sit omnibus quibus animarum cura commissa est oves suas agnoscere, pro his sacrificium offerre, verbi que divini praedicatione, sacramentorum administratione ac bonorum omnium operum exemplo pascere, pauperum aliarum que miserabilium personarum curam paternam gerere et in cetera munia pastoralia incumbere, quae omnia nequaquam ab his praestari et impleri possunt qui gregi suo non invigilant neque assistunt, sed mercenariorum more deserunt, sacrosancta synodus eos admonet et hortatur, ut divinorum praeceptorum memores, facti que forma gregis, in iudicio et veritate pascant et regant."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p432","Decree on the Reformation","For since Christian charity, urgent necessity, due obedience, and evident utility of the church or of the commonwealth sometimes demand and require that some be absent, the same sacred synod decrees that these legitimate causes of absence are to be approved in writing by the most blessed Roman pontiff, or by the metropolitan, or, in his absence, by the senior resident suffragan bishop, who ought to prove the absence of the metropolitan, unless the absence shall have occurred on account of some duty and public office annexed to bishoprics, the causes of which, since they are notorious and sometimes sudden, it will not be necessary even to signify to the metropolitan.","Ne vero ea quae de residentia sancte et utiliter iam antea sub felicis recordationis Paulo tertio sancita fuerunt in sensus a sacrosanctae synodi mente alienos trahantur, ac si vigore illius decreti quinque mensibus continuis abesse liceat, illis inhaerendo, declarat sacrosancta synodus omnes patriarchalibus, primatialibus, metropolitanis ac cathedralibus ecclesiis quibuscumque quocumque nomine et titulo praefectos, etiam si sanctae Romanae ecclesiae cardinales sint, obligari ad personalem in sua ecclesia vel dioecesi residentiam, ubi iniuncto sibi officio defungi teneantur, neque abesse posse nisi ex causis et modis infrascriptis."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p433","Decree on the Reformation","Nevertheless, it will pertain to the same metropolitan with the provincial council to judge concerning the licenses given by him or by the suffragan, and to see that no one abuses that right and that those who err may be punished with canonical penalties.","Nam cum christiana charitas, urgens necessitas, debita obedientia ac evidens ecclesiae vel rei publicae utilitas aliquos nonnumquam abesse postulent et exigant, decernit eadem sacrosancta synodus has legitimae absentiae causas a beatissimo Romano pontifice, aut a metropolitano, vel eo absente, suffraganeo episcopo antiquiori residente, qui idem metropolitani absentiam probare debebit, in scriptis esse approbandas, nisi cum absentia inciderit propter aliquod munus et rei publicae officium episcopatibus adiunctum, cuius quoniam causae sunt notoriae et interdum repentinae, ne eas quidem significari metropolitano necesse erit."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p434","Decree on the Reformation","In the meantime, let those about to depart remember so to provide for their sheep that, as far as can be done, they receive no loss from their absence.","Ad eundem tamen cum concilio provinciali spectabit iudicare de licentiis a se vel a suffraganeo datis, et videre ne quis eo iure abutatur, et ut poenis canonicis errantes puniantur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p435","Decree on the Reformation","Since, however, according to the judgment of the ancient canons, those who are absent for only a short time are not considered to be absent, because they are about to return immediately, the sacred synod wills that that space of absence each year, whether continuous or interrupted, beyond the aforesaid causes, should by no means exceed two, or at most three, months, and that regard be had that this be done for a just cause and without any detriment to the flock.","Interea meminerint discessuri ita ovibus suis providendum, ut quantum fieri poterit ex ipsorum absentia nullum damnum accipiant."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p436","Decree on the Reformation","Whether this be so, it leaves to the conscience of those who are absent, which it hopes will be religious and fearful, since hearts are open to God, whose work they are bound not to do fraudulently at their own peril.","Quoniam autem qui aliquantisper tantum absunt ex veterum canonum sententia non videntur abesse, quia statim reversuri sunt, sacrosancta synodus vult illud absentiae spatium singulis annis, sive continuum sive interruptum, extra praedictas causas nullo pacto debere duos aut ad summum tres menses excedere, et haberi rationem ut id aequa ex causa fiat et absque ullo gregis detrimento."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p437","Decree on the Reformation","In the meantime, it admonishes and exhorts them in the Lord that during that space of time they be by no means absent from their own cathedral church on the days of the Lord's Advent, Lent, the Nativity, the Resurrection of the Lord, Pentecost, and also the Body of Christ, on which days the sheep ought especially to be refreshed and to rejoice in the Lord in the presence of the pastor, unless episcopal duties call them elsewhere in their own diocese.","Quod an ita sit, absentium conscientiae relinquit, quam sperat religiosam et timoratam fore, cum deo corda pateant, cuius opus non fraudulentur agere, suo periculo tenentur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p438","Decree on the Reformation","If anyone, however (which may it never happen), shall have been absent contrary to the disposition of this decree, the sacred synod establishes that besides the other penalties against non-residents imposed and renewed under Paul III, and the guilt of mortal sin which he incurs, he does not make the fruits his own in proportion to the time of absence, nor can he with a safe conscience, no other declaration having followed, retain them for himself, but is bound, the same ceasing, by the ecclesiastical superior to cause them to be distributed to the fabric of the churches or to the poor of the place, any pact or composition prohibited which is called for fruits wrongly received, by which also the aforesaid fruits would be remitted to him in whole or in part.","Eosdem interim admonet et in domino hortatur, ne per illius temporis spatium, dominici adventus, quadragesimae, nativitatis, resurrectionis domini, pentecostes item et corporis christi diebus, quibus refici maxime et in domino gaudere pastoris praesentia oves debeant, ipsi ab ecclesia sua cathedrali ullo pacto absint, nisi episcopalia munia in sua dioecesi eos alio vocent."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p439","Decree on the Reformation","The same things, with respect to guilt, loss of fruits, and penalties, the sacred synod declares and decrees also concerning inferior curates and any others who obtain any ecclesiastical benefice having the cure of souls, yet in such a way that whenever it happens that they are absent, the cause having first been known and proved by the bishop, they leave a suitable vicar to be approved by the ordinary, with the assignment of the due salary. Moreover, let them not obtain license to depart in writing, to be granted freely, beyond two months unless from a grave cause. But if, having been cited by edict, they are contumacious, even not personally, the synod wills that it be free for ordinaries to compel them by ecclesiastical censures and by sequestration and subtraction of fruits and other remedies of law, even to deprivation, and that this execution cannot be suspended by any privilege, license, familiarity, exemption, even by reason of any benefice, pact, statute even confirmed by oath or by any authority, custom even immemorial (which is rather to be considered a corruption), or by appeal or inhibition even in the Roman curia or by virtue of the Eugidian constitution.","Si quis autem (quod utinam numquam eveniat) contra huius decreti dispositionem abfuerit, statuit sacrosancta synodus praeter alias poenas adversus non residentes sub Paulo III impositas et innovatas, ac mortalis peccati reatum quem incurrit, eum pro rata temporis absentiae fructus suos non facere, nec tuta conscientia, alia etiam declaratione non secuta, illos sibi detinere posse, sed teneri, ipso cessante, per superiorem ecclesiasticum illos fabricae ecclesiarum aut pauperibus loci erogare, prohibita quacumque conventione vel compositione quae pro fructibus male perceptis appellatur, ex qua etiam praedicti fructus in totum aut pro parte ei remitterentur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p440","Decree on the Reformation","Finally, the holy synod commands both that decree under Paul III and this same decree to be published in provincial and episcopal councils. For it wishes those things which pertain so much to the duty of pastors and the salvation of souls to be frequently fixed in the ears and minds of all, that in the future, God helping, they be not abolished by any injury of times or by the oblivion or desuetude of men.","Eadem omnino etiam quoad culpam, amissionem fructuum et poenas de curatis inferioribus et aliis quibuscumque qui beneficium aliquod ecclesiasticum curam animarum habens obtinent sacrosancta synodus declarat et decernit, ita tamen ut quandocumque eos causa prius per episcopum cognita et probata abesse contigerit, vicarium idoneum ab ipso ordinario approbandum cum debita mercedis assignatione relinquant. Discedendi autem licentiam in scriptis, gratis que concedendam, ultra bimestre tempus nisi ex gravi causa non obtineant. Quodsi per edictum citati etiam non personaliter contumaces fuerint, liberum esse vult ordinariis per censuras ecclesiasticas, ac sequestrationem et subtractionem fructuum, alia que iuris remedia, etiam usque ad privationem compellere, nec exsecutionem hanc quolibet privilegio, licentia, familiaritate, exemptione etiam ratione cuiuscumque beneficii, pactione, statuto etiam iuramento vel quacumque auctoritate confirmato, consuetudine etiam immemorabili (quae potius corruptela censenda est), sive appellatione aut inhibitione, etiam in Romana curia vel vigore Eugenianae constitutionis, suspendi posse."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p441","Decree on the Reformation","Those placed over cathedral or superior churches, by whatever name and title, even if they are cardinals of the holy Roman church, if they have not received the gift of consecration within three months, shall be bound to the restitution of the fruits received; if they have neglected to do this within the same number of months thereafter, let them be deprived of the churches by the law itself. Consecration, however, if it be done outside the Roman curia, let it be celebrated in the church to which they have been promoted, or in the province, if it can conveniently be done.","Postremo tam decretum illud sub Paulo III quam hoc ipsum in conciliis provincialibus et episcopalibus publicari sancta synodus praecipit. Cupit enim quae adeo ex pastorum munere animarum que salute sunt frequenter omnium auribus mentibus que infigi, ut in posterum, deo iuvante, nulla temporum iniuria aut hominum oblivione aut desuetudine aboleantur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p442","Decree on the Reformation","Bishops shall confer orders by themselves. But if they be hindered by illness, let them send their subjects to another bishop to be ordained, only after they have been proved and examined.","Ecclesiis cathedralibus seu superioribus quocumque nomine ac titulo praefecti, etiam si sanctae Romanae ecclesiae cardinales sint, si munus consecrationis intra tres menses non susceperint, ad fructuum perceptorum restitutionem teneantur; si intra totidem menses postea id facere neglexerint, ecclesiis ipso iure sint privati. Consecratio vero, si extra curiam Romanam fiat, in ecclesia ad quam promoti fuerint, aut in provincia, si commode fieri poterit, celebretur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p443","Decree on the Reformation","Let those not be initiated with first tonsure who have not received the sacrament of confirmation and have not been taught the rudiments of faith, and who do not know how to read and write, and of whom there is not a probable conjecture that they have chosen this kind of life not for the purpose of evading secular judgment by fraud, but to render faithful cult to God.","Episcopi per semetipsos ordines conferant. Quodsi aegritudine fuerint impediti, subditos suos non aliter quam iam probatos et examinatos ad alium episcopum ordinandos dimittant."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p444","Decree on the Reformation","Those to be promoted to minor orders shall have good testimony from the parish priest and from the schoolmaster in whose school they are educated. Those, however, who are to be assumed to each of the major orders shall approach the bishop a month before ordination, who shall commit to the parish priest or to another whom it shall seem more expedient to inquire diligently concerning the names, origin, age, morals, and life of those who wish to be promoted, having been publicly proposed in the church, from trustworthy persons, and shall transmit testimonial letters containing that inquisition to the bishop himself as soon as possible.","Prima tonsura non initientur qui sacramentum confirmationis non susceperint et fidei rudimenta edocti non fuerint, qui que legere et scribere nesciant, et de quibus probabilis coniectura non sit eos non saecularis iudicii fugiendi fraude, sed ut deo fidelem cultum praestent, hoc vitae genus elegisse."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p445","Decree on the Reformation","Let no one initiated with first tonsure or even constituted in minor orders be able to obtain a benefice before the fourteenth year. Let him also not enjoy the privilege of the forum unless he have an ecclesiastical benefice, or, bearing the clerical habit and tonsure, he serve some church at the command of the bishop, or be engaged in the seminary of clerics or in some school or university by license of the bishop as if in the way of receiving major orders.","Ad minores ordines promovendi bonum a parocho et a magistro scholae in qua educantur testimonium habeant. Ii vero qui ad singulos maiores erunt assumendi per mensem ante ordinationem episcopum adeant, qui parocho aut alteri cui magis expedire videbitur committat, ut nominibus ac desiderio eorum qui volent promoveri, publice in ecclesia propositis, de ipsorum ordinandorum natalibus, aetate, moribus et vita, a fide dignis diligenter inquirat, et litteras testimoniales ipsam inquisitionem factam continentes ad ipsum episcopum quamprimum transmittat."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p446","Decree on the Reformation","In the case of married clerics, let the constitution of Boniface VIII which begins Clerici qui be observed, with the condition that only those clerics serve or minister to some church by the deputation of the bishop, and use the clerical habit and tonsure; no privilege or custom even immemorial availing anyone in this regard.","Nullus prima tonsura initiatus aut etiam in minoribus ordinibus constitutus ante quartum decimum annum beneficium possit obtinere. Is etiam fori privilegio non gaudeat, nisi beneficium ecclesiasticum habeat, aut clericalem habitum et tonsuram deferens alicui ecclesiae ex mandato episcopi inserviat, vel in seminario clericorum aut in aliqua schola vel universitate de licentia episcopi quasi in via ad maiores ordines suscipiendos versetur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p447","Decree on the Reformation","The holy synod, adhering to the footprints of the ancient canons, decrees that when the bishop intends to perform ordination, all who wish to approach the sacred ministry are to be summoned to the city on the Wednesday before the ordination itself, or when it shall seem good to the bishop. The bishop, however, having associated with himself priests and other prudent men skilled in the divine law and exercised in ecclesiastical sanctions, shall diligently investigate and examine the kind, person, age, instruction, morals, doctrine, and faith of those to be ordained.","In clericis vero coniugatis servetur constitutio Bonifatii VIII quae incipit: \"clerici qui,\" cum unicis modo hi clerici alicuius ecclesiae servitio vel ministerio ab episcopo deputati eidem ecclesiae serviant vel ministrent et clericali habitu et tonsura utantur; nemini quoad hoc privilegio vel consuetudine etiam immemorabili suffragante."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p448","Decree on the Reformation","Ordinations of sacred orders shall be celebrated publicly at the times established by law and in the cathedral church, the canons of the church having been summoned and present for that purpose. But if in another place of the diocese, let the church of the place, as far as can be done, more worthy, always be approached, the local clergy being present. Moreover, let each one be ordained by his own bishop. But if he seek to be promoted by another, let this by no means be permitted to him, even under pretext of any general or special rescript or privilege, even at the established times, unless his probity and morals be commended by the testimony of his own ordinary.","Sancta synodus antiquorum canonum vestigiis inhaerendo decernit ut, quando episcopus ordinationem facere disposuerit, omnes qui ad sacrum ministerium accedere voluerint, feria quarta ante ipsam ordinationem vel quando episcopo videbitur, ad civitatem evocentur. Episcopus autem, sacerdotibus et aliis prudentibus viris peritis divinae legis ac in ecclesiasticis sanctionibus exercitatis sibi adscitis, ordinandorum genus, personam, aetatem, institutionem, mores, doctrinam et fidem diligenter investiget et examinet."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p449","Decree on the Reformation","If it be done otherwise, let the ordaining person be suspended from the collation of orders for a year, and the ordained person from the execution of the orders received as long as it shall seem expedient to his own ordinary.","Ordinationes sacrorum ordinum statutis a iure temporibus ac in cathedrali ecclesia, vocatis praesentibus que ad id ecclesiae canonicis, publice celebrentur. Si autem in alio dioecesis loco, praesente clero loci dignior quantum fieri poterit ecclesia semper adeatur. Unusquisque autem a proprio episcopo ordinetur. Quodsi quis ab alio promoveri petat, nullatenus id ei etiam cuiusvis generalis aut specialis rescripti vel privilegii praetextu, etiam statutis temporibus, permittatur, nisi eius probitas ac mores ordinarii sui testimonio commendentur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p450","Decree on the Reformation","A bishop cannot ordain his familiar, who is not his subject, unless he has stayed with him for three years and, all fraud ceasing, immediately confer a benefice on him; any custom even immemorial to the contrary notwithstanding.","Si secus fiat, ordinans a collatione ordinum per annum, et ordinatus a susceptorum ordinum exsecutione, quamdiu proprio ordinario videbitur expedire, sit suspensus."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p451","Decree on the Reformation","It shall not be lawful for abbots and any others, however exempt, dwelling within the boundaries of any diocese, even if they are said to be of no diocese or exempt, to confer tonsure or minor orders upon anyone who is not a regular subject to them; nor let the abbots themselves and other exempt persons, or any colleges or chapters, even of cathedral churches, grant dimissory letters to any secular clerics that they may be ordained by others; but the ordination of all these, all things contained in the decrees of this holy synod being preserved, shall pertain to the bishops within whose diocesan boundaries they exist; any privileges, prescriptions, or customs, even immemorial, notwithstanding.","Episcopus familiarem suum, non subditum, ordinare non possit, nisi per triennium se cum fuerit commoratus et beneficium, quacumque fraude cessante, statim re ipsa illi conferat; consuetudine quacumque etiam immemorabili in contrarium non obstante."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p452","Decree on the Reformation","It also commands the penalty imposed on those who, against the decree of this holy synod under Paul III, obtain dimissory letters from the chapter, the episcopal see being vacant, to be extended to those who should obtain the same letters not from the chapter but from any others succeeding in the jurisdiction of the bishop in the place of the chapter when the see is vacant. Let those granting dimissory letters contrary to the form of the decree be by the law itself suspended from office and benefice for a year.","Abbatibus ac aliis quibuscumque quantumvis exemptis non liceat in posterum intra fines alicuius dioecesis consistentibus, etiam si nullius dioecesis vel exempti esse dicantur, cuiquam qui regularis subditus sibi non sit tonsuram vel minores ordines conferre, nec ipsi abbates et alii exempti aut collegia vel capitula quaecumque etiam ecclesiarum cathedralium litteras dimissorias aliquibus clericis saecularibus, ut ab aliis ordinentur, concedant; sed horum omnium ordinatio, servatis omnibus quae in huius sanctae synodi decretis continentur, ad episcopos, intra quorum dioecesis fines exsistant, pertineat; non obstantibus quibusvis privilegiis, praescriptionibus aut consuetudinibus, etiam immemorabilibus."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p453","Decree on the Reformation","Minor orders shall be conferred on those who understand at least the Latin language, through intervals of time, unless it shall seem otherwise more expedient to the bishop, that they may be able to be taught how great the weight of this discipline is; and in each duty, according to the prescription of the bishop, let them exercise themselves in that church to which they shall be assigned, unless perhaps they be absent for the sake of study; and thus let them ascend from grade to grade, that with age, merit of life and greater doctrine may increase in them. Which the example of good morals, and assiduous ministry in the church, and greater reverence for priests and higher orders, and more frequent communion of the body of Christ than before, will especially approve.","Poenam quoque impositam his qui contra huius sanctae synodi sub Paulo III decretum a capitulo, episcopali sede vacante, litteras dimissorias impetrant, mandat extendi ad illos qui easdem litteras non a capitulo, sed ab aliis quibusvis in iurisdictione episcopi loco capituli, sede vacante, succedentibus obtinerent; concedentes autem dimissorias contra formam decreti ab officio et beneficio per annum sint ipso iure suspensi."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p454","Decree on the Reformation","Since from hence is the entrance to higher grades and most sacred mysteries, let no one be initiated in them whom the hope of knowledge does not show to be worthy of the greater orders.","Minores ordines iis qui saltem latinam linguam intelligant per temporum interstitia, nisi aliud episcopo expedire magis videretur, conferantur, ut eo accuratius quantum sit huius disciplinae pondus possint edoceri; ac in unoquoque munere iuxta praescriptum episcopi se exerceant, id que in ea cui adscripti erunt ecclesia, nisi forte ex causa studiorum absint, atque ita de gradu in gradum ascendant, ut in eis cum aetate vitae meritum et doctrina maior accrescat. Quod et bonorum morum exemplum et assiduum in ecclesia ministerium atque maior erga presbyteros et superiores ordines reverentia et crebrior quam antea corporis christi communio maxime comprobabunt."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p455","Decree on the Reformation","But these shall not be promoted to sacred orders until after a year from the reception of the last grade of minor orders, unless necessity or the utility of the church, in the judgment of the bishop, demand otherwise.","Cum que hinc ad altiores gradus et sacratissima mysteria sit ingressus, nemo iis initietur quem non scientiae spes maioribus ordinibus dignum ostendat."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p456","Decree on the Reformation","Let no one in the future be promoted to the order of subdiaconate before the twenty-second year of his age, to the diaconate before the twenty-third, to the presbyterate before the twenty-fifth. Let bishops know, however, that not all constituted in that age ought to be assumed to these orders, but only the worthy, and those whose life is proved to be aged. Regulars also shall be ordained neither at a lesser age nor without diligent examination by the bishop; any privileges whatsoever being altogether excluded in this regard.","Hi vero non nisi post annum a susceptione postremi gradus minorum ordinum ad sacros ordines promoveantur, nisi necessitas aut ecclesiae utilitas, iudicio episcopi, aliud exposcat."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p457","Decree on the Reformation","Let subdeacons and deacons be ordained who have good testimony and are already proved in minor orders and instructed in letters and in those things which pertain to the exercise of the order, who hope, God being author, that they can be continent, let them serve the churches to which they shall be assigned, and know that it is most fitting that at least on the Lord's days and solemn days, if they have ministered at the altar, they receive sacred communion.","Nullus in posterum ad subdiaconatus ordinem ante vigesimum secundum, ad diaconatus ante vigesimum tertium, ad presbyteratus ante vigesimum quintum aetatis suae annum promoveatur. Sciant tamen episcopi non singulos in ea aetate constitutos debere ad hos ordines assumi, sed dignos dumtaxat et quorum probata vita senectus sit. Regulares quoque nec in minori aetate nec sine diligenti episcopi examine ordinentur; privilegiis quibuscumque quoad hoc penitus exclusis."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p458","Decree on the Reformation","Those promoted to the sacred order of subdiaconate, if they have not been engaged in it for at least a year, shall not be permitted to ascend to a higher grade, unless it shall seem otherwise to the bishop. Two sacred orders shall not be conferred on the same day, even on regulars; any privileges and indults whatsoever granted notwithstanding.","Subdiaconi et diaconi ordinentur habentes bonum testimonium et in minoribus ordinibus iam probati, ac litteris et iis quae ad ordinem exercendum pertinent instructi, qui sperent, deo auctore, se continere posse, ecclesiis quibus adscribentur inserviant, sciant que maxime decere si saltem diebus dominicis et solemnibus, cum altari ministraverint, sacram communionem perceperint."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p459","Decree on the Reformation","Those who shall have behaved piously and faithfully in the preceding ministries shall be assumed to the presbyterate order; let them have good testimony, and these are they who have not only ministered in the diaconate for at least one whole year, unless it shall seem otherwise to the bishop on account of the utility and necessity of the church, but also, a diligent examination having preceded, shall be proved suitable for teaching the people those things which are necessary for all to know for salvation and for administering the sacraments, and thus conspicuous by piety and chaste morals that a splendid example of good works and lessons of life may be able to be expected from them.","Promoti ad sacrum subdiaconatus ordinem, si per annum saltem in eo non sint versati, ad altiorem gradum, nisi aliud episcopo videatur, ascendere non permittantur. Duo sacri ordines non eodem die, etiam regularibus, conferantur; privilegiis ac indultis quibusvis concessis non obstantibus quibuscumque."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p460","Decree on the Reformation","Let the bishop take care that they celebrate Mass at least on the Lord's days and solemn feasts, but if they have care of souls, as often as they may satisfy their duty.","Qui pie et fideliter in ministeriis anteactis se gesserint ad presbyteratus ordinem assumantur; bonum habeant testimonium, et hi sint qui non modo in diaconatu ad minus annum integrum, nisi ob ecclesiae utilitatem ac necessitatem aliud episcopo videretur, ministraverint, sed etiam, ad populum docendum ea quae scire omnibus necessarium est ad salutem ac administranda sacramenta, diligenti examine praecedente, idonei comprobentur, atque ita pietate ac castis moribus conspicui, ut praeclarum bonorum operum exemplum et vitae monita ab eis possint exspectari."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p461","Decree on the Reformation","Although presbyters receive the power of absolving from sins in their ordination, the holy synod nevertheless decrees that no one, even a regular, can hear the confessions of seculars, even of priests, nor be considered suitable for that purpose, unless he hold a parochial benefice, or be judged suitable by the bishops through examination, if they shall see it to be necessary, or otherwise, and obtain approval, which is to be given freely; any privileges and custom whatsoever, even immemorial, notwithstanding.","Curet episcopus ut ii saltem diebus dominicis et festis solemnibus, si autem curam habuerint animarum, tam frequenter ut suo muneri satisfaciant, missas celebrent."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p462","Decree on the Reformation","Since no one ought to be ordained who in the judgment of his own bishop is not useful or necessary for his churches, the holy synod, adhering to the footprints of the sixth canon of the council of Chalcedon, establishes that let no one in the future be ordained who is not inscribed for that church or pious place for whose necessity or utility he is assumed, where he may perform his duties, nor wander about uncertain seats. But if he shall have left the place without consulting the bishop, let the exercise of sacred things be forbidden to him.","Quamvis presbyteri in sua ordinatione a peccatis absolvendi potestatem accipiant, decernit tamen sancta synodus nullum, etiam regularem, posse confessiones saecularium, etiam sacerdotum, audire, nec ad id idoneum reputari, nisi aut parochiale beneficium habeat, aut ab episcopis per examen, si illis videbitur esse necessarium, aut alias idoneus iudicetur et approbationem (quae gratis detur) obtineat; privilegiis et consuetudine quacumque, etiam immemorabili, non obstantibus."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p463","Decree on the Reformation","Moreover, let no foreign cleric without commendatory letters from his own ordinary be admitted by any bishop to celebrate divine things and administer the sacraments.","Cum nullus debeat ordinari qui iudicio sui episcopi non sit utilis aut necessarius suis ecclesiis, sancta synodus vestigiis sexti canonis concilii Chalcedonensis inhaerendo statuit ut nullus in posterum ordinetur qui illi ecclesiae aut pio loco pro cuius necessitate aut utilitate assumitur non adscribatur, ubi suis fungatur muneribus, nec incertis vagetur sedibus. Quodsi locum inconsulto episcopo deseruerit, ei sacrorum exercitium interdicatur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p464","Decree on the Reformation","That the functions of the holy orders from diaconate to ostiariate, laudably received in the church from the times of the apostles and for some time discontinued in many places, may be brought back into use according to the sacred canons, the holy synod, burning with the desire of restoring that ancient custom, decrees that in the future such ministries be exercised only by those constituted in the said orders, and it exhorts in the Lord all and each of the prelates of churches, and commands them, that as far as can conveniently be done in the cathedral, collegiate, and parochial churches of their diocese, if the people be numerous and the revenues of the church can bear it, they take care to have these functions restored, and that they assign stipends for those exercising these functions from the revenues of some simple benefices or from the fabric of the church, if the revenues suffice, or from both; for which if the ordinaries be neglectful, they may be mulcted in part or altogether deprived by judgment.","Nullus praeterea clericus peregrinus sine commendatitiis sui ordinarii litteris ab ullo episcopo ad divina celebranda et sacramenta administranda admittatur."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p465","Decree on the Reformation","But if clerics, unmarried, be not at hand sufficient for exercising the four ministries of the minor orders, married men of proved life may also be sufficient, provided they be not bigamists, suitable for performing those duties, and who bear the tonsure and clerical habit in the church.","Ut sanctorum ordinum a diaconatu ad ostiariatum functiones ab apostolorum temporibus in ecclesia laudabiliter receptae et pluribus in locis aliquamdiu intermissae in usum iuxta sacros canones revocentur, sancta synodus illius prisci moris restituendi desiderio flagrans decernit ut in posterum huiuscemodi ministeria non nisi per constitutos in dictis ordinibus exerceantur, omnes que et singulos praelatos ecclesiarum in domino hortatur et illis praecipit, ut quantum fieri commode poterit in ecclesiis cathedralibus, collegiatis et parochialibus suae dioecesis, si populus frequens et ecclesiae proventus id ferre queant, huiusmodi functiones curent restituendas, et ex aliqua parte redituum aliquorum simplicium beneficiorum vel fabricae ecclesiae, si proventus suppetant, aut utriusque, illas functiones exercentibus stipendia assignent; quibus si negligentes fuerint ordinarii, iudicio aut ex parte mulctari aut in totum privari possint."],["vol2/trent/part-2/index.html","p466","Decree on the Reformation","The perpetual and indissoluble bond of matrimony was pronounced by the first parent of the human race, under the impulse of the divine Spirit, when he said: \"This now is bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh. Wherefore a man shall leave his father and mother and shall cleave to his wife, and they shall be two in one flesh.\"","Quodsi ministeriis quatuor minorum ordinum exercendis clerici coelibes praesto non erunt suffici, possint etiam coniugati vitae probatae, dummodo non bigami, ad ea munia obeunda idonei et qui tonsuram et habitum clericalem in ecclesia gestent."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p467","Session 24 — 11 November 1563","But that by this bond two only are to be united and joined, our Lord Christ taught more openly when, referring to those last words as spoken by God, He said: \"Therefore now they are not two, but one flesh\"; and immediately He confirmed the firmness of the same bond, pronounced so long before by Adam, with these words: \"What therefore God has joined together, let not man separate.\"","Matrimonii perpetuum indissolubilem que nexum primus humani generis parens, divini spiritus instinctu, pronuntiavit cum dixit: \"hoc nunc os ex ossibus meis et caro de carne mea. Quamobrem relinquet homo patrem suum et matrem et adhaerebit uxori suae, et erunt duo in carne una.\""],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p468","Session 24 — 11 November 1563","Moreover, the grace which might perfect that natural love and confirm the indissoluble union and sanctify the married persons, Christ Himself, the institutor and perfecter of the venerable sacraments, merited for us by His passion. Which the apostle Paul intimates, saying: \"Men, love your wives, as Christ also loved the church and delivered Himself up for it\"; soon after adding: \"This sacrament is great, but I speak in Christ and in the church.\"","Hoc autem vinculo duos tantummodo copulari et coniungi christus dominus apertius docuit cum, postrema illa verba tamquam a deo prolata referens, dixit: \"itaque iam non sunt duo, sed una caro\"; statim que eiusdem nexus firmitatem ab adamo tanto ante pronuntiatam his verbis confirmavit: \"Quod ergo deus coniunxit, homo non separet.\""],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p469","Session 24 — 11 November 1563","Since, therefore, matrimony in the evangelical law, through Christ, excels the ancient marriages in grace, deservedly our holy fathers and councils and the tradition of the universal church have always taught that it is to be numbered among the sacraments of the new law; against which impious men of this age raging not only have wrongly thought concerning this venerable sacrament, but also, introducing carnal liberty under the pretext of the gospel after their manner, have asserted in writing and word many things alien from the sense of the catholic church and from the custom approved from the time of the apostles, not without great loss of the faithful of Christ.","Gratiam vero quae naturalem illum amorem perficeret et indissolubilem unitatem confirmaret coniuges que sanctificaret, ipse christus, venerabilium sacramentorum institutor atque perfector, sua nobis passione promeruit. Quod Paulus apostolus innuit dicens: \"viri, diligite uxores vestras, sicut christus dilexit ecclesiam et se ipsum tradidit pro ea\"; mox subiungens: \"sacramentum hoc magnum est; ego autem dico in christo et in ecclesia.\""],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p470","Session 24 — 11 November 1563","To whose rashness the holy and universal synod, wishing to meet, has judged that the more noted heresies and errors of the aforesaid schismatics are to be exterminated, lest their pernicious contagion draw many to itself, decreeing these anathematisms against those heretics and their errors.","Cum igitur matrimonium in lege evangelica, per christum, veteribus connubiis gratia praestet, merito inter novae legis sacramenta annumerandum sancti patres nostri, concilia et universalis ecclesiae traditio semper docuerunt; adversus quam impii homines huius saeculi insanientes non solum perperam de hoc venerabili sacramento senserunt, sed de more suo, praetextu evangelii, libertatem carnis introducentes, multa ab ecclesiae catholicae sensu et ab apostolorum temporibus probata consuetudine aliena scripto et verbo asseruerunt, non sine magna christifidelium iactura."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p471","Session 24 — 11 November 1563","If anyone says that matrimony is not truly and properly one of the seven sacraments of the evangelical law, instituted by Christ the Lord, but was invented by men in the church, and does not confer grace: let him be anathema.","Quorum temeritati sancta et universalis synodus cupiens occurrere, insigniores praedictorum schismaticorum haereses et errores, ne plures ad se trahat perniciosa eorum contagio, exterminandos duxit, hos in ipsos haereticos eorum que errores decernens anathematismos."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p472","Canons on the Sacrament of Matrimony","If anyone says that it is lawful for Christians to have several wives at the same time, and that this is not prohibited by any divine law: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit matrimonium non esse vere et proprie unum ex septem legis evangelicae sacramentis, a christo domino institutum, sed ab hominibus in ecclesia inventum, neque gratiam conferre: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p473","Canon II","If anyone says that those degrees of consanguinity and affinity which are expressed in Leviticus can only impede the contracting of marriage and dissolve the contract; and that the church cannot dispense in some of those degrees or establish that more degrees impede and dissolve: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit licere christianis plures simul habere uxores, et hoc nulla lege divina esse prohibitum: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p474","Canon III","If anyone says that the church could not establish impediments dissolving marriage, or that she has erred in establishing them: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit eos tantum consanguinitatis et affinitatis gradus qui Levitico exprimuntur posse impedire matrimonium contrahendum et dirimere contractum; nec posse ecclesiam in nonnullis illorum dispensare aut constituere ut plures impediant et dirimant: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p475","Canon IV","If anyone says that the bond of marriage can be dissolved on account of heresy, or troublesome cohabitation, or affected absence from the spouse: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit ecclesiam non potuisse constituere impedimenta matrimonium dirimentia, vel in iis constituendis errasse: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p476","Canon V","If anyone says that a ratified but not consummated marriage is not dissolved by the solemn religious profession of either spouse: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit propter haeresim aut molestam cohabitationem aut affectatam absentiam a coniuge dissolvi posse matrimonii vinculum: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p477","Canon VI","If anyone says that the church errs when she has taught and teaches that, according to evangelical and apostolic doctrine, the bond of marriage cannot be dissolved on account of the adultery of either spouse, and that both, or even the innocent who has given no cause for adultery, cannot contract another marriage while the other spouse is living, and that he who, having put away the adulteress, marries another, and she who, having put away the adulterer, marries another, commit adultery: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit matrimonium ratum non consummatum per solemnem religionis professionem alterius coniugum non dirimi: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p478","Canon VII","If anyone says that the church errs when she decrees that for many causes separation may be made between spouses as to bed or as to cohabitation for a certain or uncertain time: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit ecclesiam errare cum docuit et docet, iuxta evangelicam et apostolicam doctrinam, propter adulterium alterius coniugum matrimonii vinculum non posse dissolvi, et utrumque vel etiam innocentem, qui causam adulterio non dedit, non posse altero coniuge vivente aliud matrimonium contrahere, moechari que eum qui dimissa adultera aliam duxerit et eam quae dimisso adultero alii nupserit: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p479","Canon VIII","If anyone says that clerics constituted in sacred orders, or regulars who have solemnly professed chastity, can contract marriage, and that the contract is valid, the ecclesiastical law or vow notwithstanding, and that the contrary is nothing else than to condemn marriage; and that all can contract marriage who do not feel that they have the gift of chastity (even if they have vowed it): let him be anathema, since \"God does not deny that which is rightly asked, nor does He suffer us to be tempted above that which we are able.\"","Si quis dixerit ecclesiam errare cum ob multas causas separationem inter coniuges quoad thorum seu quoad cohabitationem ad certum incertum ve tempus fieri posse decernit: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p480","Canon IX","If anyone says that the conjugal state is to be preferred to the state of virginity or celibacy, and that it is not better and more blessed to remain in virginity or celibacy than to be joined in marriage: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit clericos in sacris ordinibus constitutos vel regulares castitatem solemniter professos posse matrimonium contrahere, contractum que validum esse, non obstante lege ecclesiastica vel voto, et oppositum nil aliud esse quam damnare matrimonium; posse que omnes contrahere matrimonium qui non sentiunt se castitatis (etiam si eam voverint) habere donum: anathema sit; cum \"deus id recte petentibus non deneget, nec patiatur nos supra id quod possumus tentari.\""],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p481","Canon X","If anyone says that the prohibition of the solemnity of weddings at certain seasons of the year is a superstitious tyranny proceeding from the superstition of the heathen; or condemns the blessings and other ceremonies which the church uses in them: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit statum coniugalem anteponendum esse statui virginitatis vel coelibatus, et non esse melius ac beatius manere in virginitate aut coelibatu quam iungi matrimonio: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p482","Canon XI","If anyone says that matrimonial causes do not pertain to ecclesiastical judges: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit prohibitionem solemnitatis nuptiarum certis anni temporibus superstitionem esse tyrannicam ab ethnicorum superstitione profectam; aut benedictiones et alias caeremonias quibus ecclesia in illis utitur damnaverit: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p483","Canon XII","Although it is not to be doubted that clandestine marriages, made with the free consent of those contracting, are true and real marriages as long as the church has not made them void, and therefore that those are rightly to be condemned, as the holy synod condemns them with anathema, who deny that they are true and real, and who falsely affirm that marriages contracted by children of the family without the consent of their parents are void, and that parents can make them void or valid; nevertheless holy church of God has always detested and prohibited them for most just reasons.","Si quis dixerit causas matrimoniales non spectare ad iudices ecclesiasticos: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p484","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","But since the holy synod perceives that those prohibitions, on account of the disobedience of men, are now no longer profitable, and considers the grave sins which arise from the same clandestine marriages, especially of those who remain in a state of damnation while, having left the former wife with whom they had contracted clandestinely, they contract openly with another and live with her in perpetual adultery; to which evil, since the church does not judge of hidden things, it cannot be succored unless some more efficacious remedy be applied, therefore, adhering to the footsteps of the sacred Lateran council celebrated under Innocent III, it commands that in the future, before a marriage is contracted, it be announced publicly three times by the proper parish priest of those contracting, on three continuous feast days, during the solemnities of the mass, between whom the marriage is to be contracted; which announcements having been made, if no legitimate impediment be opposed, then let the celebration of the marriage proceed before the face of the church, where the parish priest, the man and woman having been interrogated and their mutual consent understood, shall either say: \"I join you in matrimony in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit,\" or shall use other words according to the received rite of each province.","Tametsi dubitandum non est clandestina matrimonia, libero contrahentium consensu facta, rata et vera esse matrimonia, quamdiu ecclesia ea irrita non fecit, et proinde iure sint damnandi illi, ut eos sancta synodus anathemate damnat, qui ea vera ac rata esse negant, qui que falso affirmant matrimonia a filiis familias sine consensu parentum contracta irrita esse et parentes ea rata vel irrita facere posse; nihilominus sancta dei ecclesia ex iustissimis causis illa semper detestata est atque prohibuit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p485","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","But if at any time there be a probable suspicion that the marriage might be maliciously impeded if so many announcements preceded, then let either only one announcement be made, or at least let the marriage be celebrated in the presence of the parish priest and two or three witnesses; then before its consummation let announcements be made in the church, that if any impediments subsist they may be more easily discovered, unless the ordinary himself shall have judged that the aforesaid announcements are to be omitted, which the holy synod leaves to his prudence and judgment.","Verum cum sancta synodus animadvertat prohibitiones illas propter hominum inobedientiam iam non prodesse, et gravia peccata perpendat quae ex eisdem clandestinis coniugiis ortum habent, praesertim vero eorum qui in statu damnationis permanent dum priore uxore cum qua clam contraxerant relicta cum alia palam contrahunt et cum ea in perpetuo adulterio vivunt; cui malo cum ab ecclesia (quae de occultis non iudicat) succurri non possit nisi efficacius aliquod remedium adhibeatur, idcirco sacri Lateranensis concilii sub Innocentio III celebrati vestigiis inhaerendo praecipit ut in posterum, antequam matrimonium contrahatur, ter a proprio contrahentium parocho, tribus continuis diebus festivis in ecclesia inter missarum solemnia publice denuntietur inter quos matrimonium sit contrahendum; quibus denuntiationibus factis, si nullum legitimum opponatur impedimentum, ad celebrationem matrimonii in facie ecclesiae procedatur, ubi parochus, viro et muliere interrogatis et eorum mutuo consensu intellecto, vel dicat: \"ego vos in matrimonium coniungo in nomine patris et filii et spiritus sancti,\" vel aliis utatur verbis iuxta receptum uniuscuiusque provinciae ritum."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p486","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","Those who shall attempt to contract marriage otherwise than in the presence of the parish priest or of another priest with the license of the parish priest or of the ordinary, and of two or three witnesses, the holy synod renders altogether incapable of so contracting, and decrees that such contracts are void and null, just as by the present decree it makes them void and annuls them. Moreover, it commands that the parish priest or other priest who shall have assisted with a smaller number of witnesses, and the witnesses who shall have been present at such a contract without the parish priest or other priest, and also the contracting parties themselves, be severely punished at the discretion of the ordinary.","Quodsi aliquando probabilis fuerit suspicio matrimonium malitiose impediri posse si tot praecesserint denuntiationes, tunc vel una tantum denuntiatio fiat, vel saltem parocho et duobus vel tribus testibus praesentibus matrimonium celebretur; deinde ante illius consummationem denuntiationes in ecclesia fiant, ut si aliqua subsunt impedimenta facilius detegantur, nisi ordinarius ipse expedire iudicaverit ut praedictae denuntiationes remittantur, quod illius prudentiae et iudicio sancta synodus relinquit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p487","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","Moreover, the same holy synod exhorts that spouses do not cohabit in the same house before the priestly blessing is received in the temple. And it establishes that the blessing be done by the proper parish priest, nor can license for making the aforesaid blessing be granted to another priest by anyone except by the same parish priest or by the ordinary, any custom even immemorial (which is rather to be called a corruption) or privilege notwithstanding. But if any parish priest or other priest, whether regular or secular, even if he contend that it is lawful for him by privilege or immemorial custom, shall have dared to join in marriage or bless the betrothed of another parish without the license of their parish priest, let him remain suspended by the law itself until he be absolved by the ordinary of that parish priest who ought to have been present at the marriage or from whom the blessing ought to have been received.","Qui aliter quam praesente parocho vel alio sacerdote de ipsius parochi seu ordinarii licentia et duobus vel tribus testibus matrimonium contrahere attentabunt, eos sancta synodus ad sic contrahendum omnino inhabiles reddit, et huiusmodi contractus irritos et nullos esse decernit, prout eos praesenti decreto irritos facit et annullat. Insuper parochum vel alium sacerdotem qui cum minori testium numero, et testes qui sine parocho vel sacerdote huiusmodi contractui interfuerint, necnon ipsos contrahentes, graviter arbitrio ordinarii puniri praecipit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p488","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","Let the parish priest have a book in which he describes the names of the spouses and witnesses and the day and place of the contracted marriage, which he carefully keeps with himself.","Praeterea eadem sancta synodus hortatur ut coniuges ante benedictionem sacerdotalem in templo suscipiendam in eadem domo non cohabitent. Statuit que benedictionem a proprio parocho fieri, neque a quoquam nisi ab ipso parocho vel ab ordinario licentiam ad praedictam benedictionem faciendam alii sacerdoti concedi posse, quacumque consuetudine etiam immemorabili (quae potius corruptela dicenda est) vel privilegio non obstante. Quodsi quis parochus vel alius sacerdos, sive regularis sive saecularis sit, etiam si id sibi ex privilegio vel immemorabili consuetudine licere contendat, alterius parochiae sponsos sine illorum parochi licentia matrimonio coniungere aut benedicere ausus fuerit, ipso iure tamdiu suspensus maneat, quamdiu ab ordinario eius parochi qui matrimonio interesse debebat seu a quo benedictio suscipienda erat absolvatur."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p489","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","Finally, the holy synod exhorts spouses that, before they contract, or at least three days before the consummation of the marriage, they diligently confess their sins and piously approach the most holy sacrament of the Eucharist.","Habeat parochus librum in quo coniugum et testium nomina diem que et locum contracti matrimonii describat, quem diligenter apud se custodiat."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p490","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","If any provinces use other laudable customs and ceremonies in this matter besides the aforesaid, the holy synod earnestly desires that they be altogether retained.","Postremo sancta synodus coniuges hortatur, ut antequam contrahant, vel saltem triduo ante matrimonii consummationem, sua peccata diligenter confiteantur et ad sanctissimum eucharistiae sacramentum pie accedant."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p491","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","Lest, however, these so salutary precepts escape anyone, it commands all ordinaries that as soon as they can they take care to have this decree published and explained to the people in each of the parish churches of their own diocese, and that this be done as often as possible in the first year, then indeed as often as they shall see expedient. Moreover, it decrees that such a decree in each parish begin to have its force after thirty days from the day of the first publication made in that parish.","Si quae provinciae aliis ultra praedictas laudabilibus consuetudinibus et caeremoniis hac in re utuntur, eas omnino retineri sancta synodus vehementer optat."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p492","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","Experience teaches that on account of the multitude of prohibitions, marriages are often ignorantly contracted in prohibited cases, in which either they are continued not without great sin, or they are dissolved not without great scandal. Therefore the holy synod, wishing to provide for this disadvantage, and beginning from the impediment of spiritual relationship, establishes that only one, whether man or woman, or at most one and one woman, according to the institutes of the sacred canons, receive the baptized person from baptism, between whom and the baptized person himself and his father and mother, and also between the baptizer and the baptized person and the father and mother of the baptized person, only spiritual relationship be contracted.","Ne vero haec tam salubria praecepta quemquam lateant, ordinariis omnibus praecipit ut cum primum potuerint curent hoc decretum populo publicari ac explicari in singulis suarum dioecesum parochialibus ecclesiis, id que in primo anno quam saepissime fiat, deinde vero quoties expedire viderint. Decernit insuper ut huiusmodi decretum in unaquaque parochia suum robur post triginta dies habere incipiat a die primae publicationis in eadem parochia factae numerandos."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p493","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","Let the parish priest, before he proceeds to confer baptism, diligently inquire of those to whom it pertains whom they have chosen to receive the baptized person from the sacred font, and let him admit only him or them to receive him, and let him describe their names in the book, and teach them what relationship they have contracted, that they may be able to excuse themselves by no ignorance. But if others beyond those designated have touched the baptized person, let them by no means contract spiritual relationship; the constitutions to the contrary notwithstanding. If it shall have been done otherwise through the fault or negligence of the parish priest, let him be punished at the discretion of the ordinary.","Docet experientia propter multitudinem prohibitionum multoties in casibus prohibitis ignoranter contrahi matrimonia, in quibus vel non sine magno peccato perseveratur, vel ea non sine magno scandalo dirimuntur. Volens itaque sancta synodus huic incommodo providere et a cognationis spiritualis impedimento incipiens, statuit ut unus tantum sive vir sive mulier, iuxta sacrorum canonum instituta, vel ad summum unus et una, baptizatum de baptismo suscipiant, inter quos ac baptizatum ipsum et illius patrem et matrem, necnon inter baptizantem et baptizatum baptizati que patrem ac matrem tantum spiritualis cognatio contrahatur."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p494","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","Let that relationship also which is contracted from confirmation not extend beyond the confirmator and the confirmed and his father and mother and the holder; all impediments of this spiritual relationship between other persons being altogether removed.","Parochus antequam ad baptismum conferendum accedat, diligenter ab iis ad quos spectabit sciscitetur quem vel quos elegerint ut baptizatum de sacro fonte suscipiant, et eum vel eos tantum ad illum suscipiendum admittat, et in libro eorum nomina describat, doceat que eos quam cognationem contraxerint, ne ignorantia ulla excusari valeant. Quodsi alii ultra designatos baptizatum tetigerint, cognationem spiritualem nullo pacto contrahant; constitutionibus in contrarium facientibus non obstantibus. Si parochi culpa vel negligentia secus factum fuerit, arbitrio ordinarii puniatur."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p495","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","The impediment of public honesty, where the espousals in whatever way shall not be valid, the holy synod altogether removes; where they shall be valid, let it not exceed the first degree, because in further degrees such a prohibition cannot be observed without loss.","Ea quoque cognatio quae ex confirmatione contrahitur confirmantem et confirmatum illius que patrem et matrem ac tenentem non egrediatur; omnibus inter alias personas huius spiritualis cognationis impedimentis omnino sublatis."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p496","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","Moreover, the holy synod, induced by these and other most grave causes, restricts the impediment which is introduced by reason of affinity contracted from fornication and dissolves a subsequent marriage, to those only who are joined in the first and second degree; but in further degrees it establishes that such affinity does not dissolve a subsequently contracted marriage.","Iustitiae publicae honestatis impedimentum, ubi sponsalia quacumque ratione valida non erunt, sancta synodus prorsus tollit; ubi autem valida fuerint, primum gradum non excedat, quoniam in ulterioribus gradibus iam non potest huiusmodi prohibitio absque dispendio observari."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p497","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","If anyone shall have knowingly presumed to contract marriage within the prohibited degrees, let him be separated, and let him lack the hope of obtaining a dispensation; and let this take place much more in him who shall have dared not only to contract marriage but also to consummate it. But if he shall have done this ignorantly, if indeed he neglected the required solemnities in contracting marriage, let him be subject to the same penalties. For he is not worthy easily to experience the benignity of the church who has rashly despised her salutary precepts.","Praeterea sancta synodus eisdem et aliis gravissimis de causis adducta impedimentum quod propter affinitatem ex fornicatione contractam inducitur et matrimonium postea factum dirimit, ad eos tantum qui in primo et secundo gradu coniunguntur restringit; in ulterioribus vero gradibus statuit huiusmodi affinitatem matrimonium postea contractum non dirimere."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p498","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","If, however, the solemnities having been employed, some impediment be afterwards known to subsist of which he had probable ignorance, then dispensation can be more easily and freely granted with him.","Si quis intra gradus prohibitos scienter matrimonium contrahere praesumpserit, separetur, ac spe dispensationis consequendae careat, id que in eo multo magis locum habeat qui non tantum matrimonium contrahere sed etiam consummare ausus fuerit. Quodsi ignoranter id fecerit, siquidem solemnitates requisitas in contrahendo matrimonio neglexerit, eisdem subiiciatur poenis. Non enim dignus est qui ecclesiae benignitatem facile experiatur cuius salubria praecepta temere contempsit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p499","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","In contracting marriages, let either no dispensation be given at all, or rarely, and that for cause and freely. In the second degree, let it never be dispensed except between great princes and on account of a public cause.","Si vero solemnitatibus adhibitis impedimentum aliquod postea subesse cognoscatur, cuius ille probabilem ignorantiam habuit, tunc facilius cum eo et gratis dispensari poterit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p500","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","The holy synod decrees that between a ravisher and the one ravished, as long as she remains in the power of the ravisher, no marriage can exist. But if the one ravished, separated from the ravisher and placed in a safe and free place, shall consent to have him as a husband, let the ravisher have her as a wife; and nevertheless the ravisher himself and all those who gave him counsel, aid, and favor shall be by the law itself excommunicated and perpetually infamous and incapable of all dignities. And if they be clerics, let them fall from their own grade. Moreover, let the ravisher be bound decently to endow the woman ravished, whether he has taken her as a wife or not, at the discretion of the judge.","In contrahendis matrimoniis vel nulla omnino detur dispensatio, vel raro, id que ex causa et gratis concedatur. In secundo gradu numquam dispensetur, nisi inter magnos principes et ob publicam causam."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p501","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","Many are they who wander about and have uncertain abodes, and, being of wicked ingenuity, having left the first wife, take another, and often several, while she is living, in different places; to which evil the holy synod, wishing to meet, paternally warns all to whom it pertains not to easily receive this kind of wandering men to marriage. It also exhorts secular magistrates to restrain them severely; and commands parish priests not to be present at their marriages unless they shall have first made a diligent inquisition, and, the matter having been referred to the ordinary, have obtained license from him for doing this.","Decernit sancta synodus inter raptorem et raptam, quamdiu ipsa in potestate raptoris manserit, nullum posse consistere matrimonium. Quodsi rapta a raptore separata et in loco tuto et libero constituta illum in virum habere consenserit, eam raptor in uxorem habeat; et nihilominus raptor ipse ac omnes illi consilium, auxilium et favorem praebentes sint ipso iure excommunicati ac perpetuo infames, omnium que dignitatum incapaces. Et si clerici fuerint, de proprio gradu decidant. Teneatur praeterea raptor mulierem raptam, sive eam in uxorem duxerit sive non, decenter arbitrio iudicis dotare."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p502","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","It is a grave sin for unmarried men to have concubines, but most grave, and committed in singular contempt of this great sacrament, also for married men to live in this state of damnation, and to dare sometimes to keep and retain them at home even with their wives. Wherefore, that the holy synod may provide opportune remedies for so great an evil, it establishes that such concubinarians, both unmarried and married, of whatever state, dignity, and condition, if after having been three times admonished by the ordinary, even ex officio, concerning this matter, they have not ejected their concubines and separated themselves from their association, they are to be struck with excommunication, from which they are not to be absolved until they have really obeyed the admonition given. But if they shall have remained in concubinage for a year, the censures having been neglected, let them be proceeded against severely by the ordinary according to the quality of the crime.","Multi sunt qui vagantur et incertas habent sedes, et ut improbi sunt ingenii, prima uxore relicta, aliam et plerumque plures illa vivente diversis in locis ducunt; cui morbo cupiens sancta synodus occurrere, omnes ad quos spectat paterne monet ne hoc genus hominum vagantium ad matrimonium facile recipiant. Magistratus etiam saeculares hortatur ut eos severe coerceant; parochis autem praecipit ne illorum matrimoniis intersint, nisi prius diligentem inquisitionem fecerint, et re ad ordinarium delata, ab eo licentiam id faciendi obtinuerint."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p503","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","Women, whether married or unmarried, who live publicly with adulterers or concubinaries, if after being three times admonished they have not obeyed, are to be severely punished by the ordinaries of places, even without anyone requiring it, ex officio, according to the measure of the fault, and to be expelled out of the town or diocese if it shall seem good to the same ordinaries, the secular arm having been invoked if necessary; other penalties inflicted against adulterers and concubinarians remaining in their force.","Grave peccatum est homines solutos concubinas habere, gravissimum vero et in huius magni sacramenti singularem contemptum admissum, uxoratos quoque in hoc damnationis statu vivere, ac audere eas quandoque domi etiam cum uxoribus alere et retinere. Quare, ut huic tanto malo sancta synodus opportunis remediis provideat, statuit huiusmodi concubinarios, tam solutos quam uxoratos, cuiuscumque status, dignitatis et conditionis exsistant, si postquam ab ordinario etiam ex officio ter admoniti ea de re fuerint, concubinas non eiecerint se que ab earum consuetudine non seiunxerint, excommunicatione feriendos esse, a qua non absolvantur donec re ipsa admonitioni factae paruerint. Quodsi in concubinatu per annum censuris neglectis permanserint, contra eos ab ordinario severe pro qualitate criminis procedatur."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p504","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","The earthly affections and cupidities of temporal lords and magistrates so often blind the eyes of their minds that by threats and penalties they compel men and women living under their jurisdiction, especially the rich or those having hope of a great inheritance, to contract marriage unwillingly with those whom the lords or magistrates themselves have prescribed to them. Wherefore, since it is most wicked to violate the liberty of marriage, and injuries arise from those from whom rights are expected, the holy synod commands all, of whatsoever grade, dignity, and condition, under penalty of anathema which they incur by the very act, that they do not in any way, directly or indirectly, compel their subjects or any others so that they do not freely contract marriage.","Mulieres, sive coniugatae sive solutae, quae cum adulteris seu concubinariis publice vivunt, si ter admonitae non paruerint, ab ordinariis locorum, nullo etiam requirente, ex officio, graviter pro modo culpae puniantur, et extra oppidum vel dioecesim, si id eisdem ordinariis videbitur, invocato si opus fuerit brachio saeculari, eiiciantur; aliis poenis contra adulteros et concubinarios inflictis in suo robore permanentibus."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p505","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","From the advent of our Lord Jesus Christ until the day of Epiphany, and from Ash Wednesday until the octave of Easter inclusive, the holy synod commands that the ancient prohibitions of solemn weddings be diligently observed by all. In other times, however, it permits weddings to be solemnly celebrated, which bishops shall take care to be done with the modesty and honesty which befits. For matrimony is a holy thing and is to be treated holily.","Ita plerumque temporalium dominorum ac magistratuum mentis oculos terreni affectus atque cupiditates excaecant, ut viros et mulieres sub eorum iurisdictione degentes, maxime divites vel spem magnae haereditatis habentes, minis et poenis adigant cum iis matrimonium invitos contrahere quos ipsi domini vel magistratus illis praescripserint. Quare, cum maxime nefarium sit matrimonii libertatem violare et ab eis iniurias nasci a quibus iura exspectantur, praecipit sancta synodus omnibus, cuiuscumque gradus, dignitatis et conditionis exsistant, sub anathematis poena, quam ipso facto incurrant, ne quovis modo directe vel indirecte subditos suos vel quoscumque alios cogant quominus libere matrimonia contrahant."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p506","Decree on the Reformation of Marriage","Since the catholic church, taught by the Holy Spirit, from the sacred scriptures and the ancient tradition of the fathers, in sacred councils and lastly in this oecumenical synod, has taught that there is a purgatory, and that the souls detained there are helped by the suffrages of the faithful, especially by the acceptable sacrifice of the altar, the holy synod commands bishops that they diligently strive that the sound doctrine concerning purgatory, handed down by the holy fathers and sacred councils, be believed, held, taught, and everywhere preached by the faithful of Christ.","Ab adventu domini nostri Iesu christi usque in diem epiphaniae, et a feria quarta cinerum usque in octavam paschatis inclusive, antiquas solemnium nuptiarum prohibitiones diligenter ab omnibus observari sancta synodus praecipit. In aliis vero temporibus nuptias solemniter celebrari permittit, quas episcopi, ut ea qua decet modestia et honestate fiant, curabunt. Sancta enim res est matrimonium et sancte tractandum."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p507","Session 25 — 3-4 December 1563","But the more difficult and subtle questions, which do not make for edification and from which there is often no increase of piety, are to be excluded from popular sermons. Likewise, uncertain matters, or those which labor under the appearance of falsehood, are not to be published or treated. Those, however, which pertain to a certain curiosity or superstition or savor of filthy gain, as scandals and offenses of the faithful, let them forbid.","Cum catholica ecclesia, spiritu sancto edocta, ex sacris litteris et antiqua patrum traditione in sacris conciliis et novissime in hac oecumenica synodo docuerit purgatorium esse, animas que ibi detentas fidelium suffragiis, potissimum vero acceptabili altaris sacrificio, iuvari, praecipit sancta synodus episcopis ut sanam de purgatorio doctrinam a sanctis patribus et sacris conciliis traditam a christifidelibus credi, teneri, doceri et ubique praedicari diligenter studeant."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p508","Session 25 — 3-4 December 1563","Moreover, let bishops take care that the suffrages of the living faithful, namely the sacrifices of masses, prayers, alms, and other works of piety which have been accustomed to be performed by the faithful for other faithful departed, be done piously and devoutly according to the institutes of the church, and that those things which are owed for them from the foundations of testators or by any other reason be performed not perfunctorily but diligently and accurately by priests and the ministers of the church and by others who are bound to perform this.","Apud rudem vero plebem difficiliores ac subtiliores quaestiones, quaeque ad aedificationem non faciunt et ex quibus plerumque nulla fit pietatis accessio, a popularibus concionibus secludantur. Incerta item vel quae specie falsi laborant, evulgari ac tractari non permittantur. Ea vero quae ad curiositatem quandam aut superstitionem spectant vel turpe lucrum sapiunt, tamquam scandala et fidelium offendicula, prohibeant."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p509","Session 25 — 3-4 December 1563","The holy synod commands all bishops and others sustaining the office and care of teaching that, according to the use of the catholic and apostolic church received from the primitive times of the Christian religion, and according to the consent of the holy fathers and the decrees of the sacred councils, they above all diligently instruct the faithful concerning the intercession and invocation of saints, the honor of relics, and the legitimate use of images, teaching them that the saints, reigning together with Christ, offer their prayers to God for men; that it is good and useful to invoke them suppliantly, and to flee to their prayers, help, and assistance for the sake of obtaining benefits from God through His Son Jesus Christ our Lord, who alone is our Redeemer and Savior; that those who deny that the saints, enjoying eternal happiness in heaven, are to be invoked, or who assert either that they do not pray for men, or that their invocation for us to pray even for each individually is idolatry, or that it is contrary to the word of God and opposes the honor of the one mediator of God and men, Jesus Christ, or that it is foolish to supplicate with voice or mind those who reign in heaven: think impiously.","Curent autem episcopi ut fidelium vivorum suffragia, missarum scilicet sacrificia, orationes, eleemosynae, alia que pietatis opera quae a fidelibus pro aliis fidelibus defunctis fieri consueverunt, secundum ecclesiae instituta pie et devote fiant, et quae pro illis ex testatorum fundationibus vel alia ratione debentur, non perfunctorie, sed a sacerdotibus et ecclesiae ministris et aliis qui hoc praestare tenentur, diligenter et accurate persolvantur."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p510","Decree Concerning the Invocation, Veneration, and Relics of Saints, and Concerni","That the holy bodies of holy martyrs and of others living with Christ, which were the living members of Christ and the temple of the Holy Spirit, are to be venerated by the faithful, through which many benefits are bestowed by God on men, so that those who affirm that veneration and honor are not owed to the relics of the saints, or that they are uselessly honored by the faithful, and that the memorials of the saints are vainly frequented for the sake of obtaining their help: are altogether to be condemned, just as the church has long since condemned them and now also condemns them.","Mandat sancta synodus omnibus episcopis et ceteris docendi munus curam que sustinentibus, ut iuxta catholicae et apostolicae ecclesiae usum a primaevis christianae religionis temporibus receptum, sanctorum que patrum consensionem et sacrorum conciliorum decreta, in primis de sanctorum intercessione, invocatione, reliquiarum honore et legitimo imaginum usu fideles diligenter instruant, docentes eos sanctos, una cum christo regnantes, orationes suas pro hominibus deo offerre; bonum atque utile esse suppliciter eos invocare et ob beneficia impetranda a deo per filium eius Iesum christum dominum nostrum, qui solus noster redemptor et salvator est, ad eorum orationes, opem, auxilium que confugere; illos vero qui negant sanctos aeterna felicitate in coelo fruentes invocandos esse, aut qui asserunt vel illos pro hominibus non orare, vel eorum ut pro nobis etiam singulis orent invocationem esse idolatriam, vel pugnare cum verbo dei adversari que honori unius mediatoris dei et hominum Iesu christi, vel stultum esse in coelo regnantibus voce vel mente supplicare: impie sentire."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p511","Decree Concerning the Invocation, Veneration, and Relics of Saints, and Concerni","Moreover, that the images of Christ, of the Virgin Mother of God, and of the other saints are to be had and retained especially in temples, and that due honor and veneration is to be imparted to them, not that any divinity or virtue is believed to be in them, on account of which they are to be worshipped, or that something is to be asked from them, or that trust is to be placed in images, as was once done by the gentiles who placed their hope in idols; but because the honor which is shown to them is referred to the prototypes which they represent, so that through the images, which we kiss and before which we uncover our heads and prostrate ourselves, we adore Christ and venerate the saints whose likenesses they bear. That which was established by the decrees of councils, especially of the second Nicene synod, against the opponents of images.","Sanctorum quoque martyrum et aliorum cum christo viventium sancta corpora, quae viva membra fuerunt christi et templum spiritus sancti, ab ipso ad aeternam vitam suscitanda et glorificanda, a fidelibus veneranda esse, per quae multa beneficia a deo hominibus praestantur, ita ut affirmantes sanctorum reliquiis venerationem atque honorem non deberi, vel eas alia que sacra monumenta a fidelibus inutiliter honorari atque eorum opis impetrandae causa sanctorum memorias frustra frequentari: omnino damnandos esse, prout iampridem eos damnavit et nunc etiam damnat ecclesia."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p512","Decree Concerning the Invocation, Veneration, and Relics of Saints, and Concerni","Moreover, let the bishops diligently teach that by the histories of the mysteries of our redemption, expressed in paintings or other likenesses, the people are instructed and confirmed in remembering and assiduously recollecting the articles of faith; then, that great fruit is perceived from all sacred images, not only because the people are reminded of the benefits and gifts which have been bestowed upon them by Christ, but also because the miracles of God and the salutary examples of the saints are set before the eyes of the faithful, so that for them they may give thanks to God, may compose their own life and manners to the imitation of the saints, and may be excited to adore and love God and to cultivate piety.","Imagines porro christi, deiparae virginis et aliorum sanctorum in templis praesertim habendas et retinendas, eis que debitum honorem et venerationem impertiendam, non quod credatur inesse aliqua in iis divinitas vel virtus propter quam sint colendae, vel quod ab eis sit aliquid petendum, vel quod fiducia in imaginibus sit figenda, veluti olim fiebat a gentibus quae in idolis spem suam collocabant; sed quoniam honos qui eis exhibetur refertur ad prototypa quae illae repraesentant, ita ut per imagines, quas osculamur et coram quibus caput aperimus et procumbimus, christum adoremus et sanctos, quorum illae similitudinem gerunt, veneremur. Id quod conciliorum, praesertim vero secundae Nicaenae synodi, decretis contra imaginum oppugnatores est sancitum."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p513","Decree Concerning the Invocation, Veneration, and Relics of Saints, and Concerni","If anyone, however, shall have taught or thought contrary to these decrees: let him be anathema.","Illud vero diligenter doceant episcopi per historias mysteriorum nostrae redemptionis, picturis vel aliis similitudinibus expressas, erudiri et confirmari populum in articulis fidei commemorandis et assidue recolendis; tum vero ex omnibus sacris imaginibus magnum fructum percipi non solum quia admonetur populus beneficiorum et munerum quae a christo sibi collata sunt, sed etiam quia dei per sanctos miracula et salutaria exempla oculis fidelium subiiciuntur, ut pro iis deo gratias agant, ad sanctorum que imitationem vitam mores que suos componant, excitentur que ad adorandum ac diligendum deum et ad pietatem colendam."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p514","Decree Concerning the Invocation, Veneration, and Relics of Saints, and Concerni","But if any abuses have crept into these holy and salutary observances, the holy synod earnestly desires that they be altogether abolished, so that no images of false dogma and giving occasion of dangerous error to the rude be set up.","Si quis autem his decretis contraria docuerit aut senserit: anathema sit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p515","Decree Concerning the Invocation, Veneration, and Relics of Saints, and Concerni","But if sometimes it happens that histories and narratives of sacred scripture, when it will be expedient for the unlearned people, are expressed and figured, let the people be taught that for this reason the divinity is not figured as if it can be seen with bodily eyes or expressed by colors or figures.","In has autem sanctas et salutares observationes si qui abusus irrepserint, eos prorsus aboleri sancta synodus vehementer cupit, ita ut nullae falsi dogmatis imagines et rudibus periculosi erroris occasionem praebentes statuantur."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p516","Decree Concerning the Invocation, Veneration, and Relics of Saints, and Concerni","Moreover, let all superstition be removed in the invocation of saints, the veneration of relics, and the sacred use of images; let all filthy gain be eliminated; finally, let all lasciviousness be avoided, so that images be not painted nor adorned with provocative beauty; and that men not abuse the celebration of saints and the visitation of relics to feasts and drunkenness, as if feast days in honor of the saints are to be spent in luxury and lasciviousness.","Quodsi aliquando historias et narrationes sacrae scripturae, cum id indoctae plebi expediet, exprimi et figurari contigerit, doceatur populus non propterea divinitatem figurari quasi corporeis oculis conspici vel coloribus aut figuris exprimi possit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p517","Decree Concerning the Invocation, Veneration, and Relics of Saints, and Concerni","Finally, so great diligence and care is to be applied by bishops concerning these matters, that nothing disordered, or preposterously and tumultuously accommodated, nothing profane, nothing dishonest appear, since holiness befits the house of God.","Omnis porro superstitio in sanctorum invocatione, reliquiarum veneratione et imaginum sacro usu tollatur; omnis turpis quaestus eliminetur; omnis denique lascivia vitetur, ita ut procaci venustate imagines non pingantur nec ornentur; et sanctorum celebratione ac reliquiarum visitatione homines ad commessationes atque ebrietates non abutantur, quasi festi dies in honorem sanctorum per luxum ac lasciviam agantur."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p518","Decree Concerning the Invocation, Veneration, and Relics of Saints, and Concerni","So that these things may be more faithfully observed, the holy synod establishes that it is lawful for no one in any place or church, however exempt, to place or cause to be placed any unusual image, unless it has been approved by the bishop. Moreover, that no new miracles be admitted, nor new relics received, unless the same bishop recognizing and approving. Who as soon as he has discovered anything concerning these, having taken into counsel theologians and other pious men, let him do those things which he shall have judged to be consonant with truth and piety.","Postremo tanta circa haec diligentia et cura ab episcopis adhibeatur, ut nihil inordinatum aut praepostere et tumultuarie accommodatum, nihil profanum, nihil inhonestum appareat, cum domum dei deceat sanctitudo."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p519","Decree Concerning the Invocation, Veneration, and Relics of Saints, and Concerni","But if any doubtful or difficult abuse is to be extirpated, or absolutely any more grave question concerning these matters shall arise, let the bishop, before he resolves the controversy, await the opinion of the metropolitan and of the suffragan bishops in the provincial council, yet in such a way that nothing new or hitherto unusual in the church be decreed without the most holy Roman pontiff having been consulted.","Haec ut fidelius observentur, statuit sancta synodus nemini licere ullo in loco vel ecclesia, etiam quomodolibet exempta, ullam insolitam ponere vel ponendam curare imaginem, nisi ab episcopo approbata fuerit. Nulla etiam admittenda esse nova miracula, nec novas reliquias recipiendas nisi eodem recognoscente et approbante episcopo. Qui simulatque de his aliquid compertum habuerit, adhibitis in consilium theologis et aliis piis viris, ea faciat quae veritati et pietati consentanea iudicaverit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p520","Decree Concerning the Invocation, Veneration, and Relics of Saints, and Concerni","The same sacred synod, pursuing the matter of reformation, has judged that the following are to be established in the present session.","Quodsi aliquis dubius aut difficilis abusus sit exstirpandus, vel omnino aliqua de his rebus gravior quaestio incidat, episcopus antequam controversiam dirimat, metropolitani et comprovincialium episcoporum in concilio provinciali sententiam exspectet; ita tamen ut nihil inconsulto sanctissimo romano pontifice novum aut in ecclesia hactenus inusitatum decernatur."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p521","Decree on the Reformation","Since the suspicion of all avarice ought to be absent from the ecclesiastical order, let bishops and other ordainers, or their ministers, receive nothing for the conferral of any orders whatsoever, even of clerical tonsure, nor for dimissory or testimonial letters, nor for seal, nor for any other cause, even voluntarily offered, under any pretext.","Eadem sacrosancta synodus reformationem prosequens, ea quae sequuntur statuenda esse censuit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p522","Canon I","Notaries, however, in those places only in which there does not prevail the laudable custom of receiving nothing, may receive for each dimissory or testimonial letter only a tenth part of a gold coin, provided that no salary be established for them for exercising their office, nor can any emolument from the profits of the notaries for the same conferrals of orders come directly or indirectly to the bishop.","Quoniam ab ecclesiastico ordine omnis avaritiae suspicio abesse debet, nihil pro collatione quorumcumque ordinum, etiam clericalis tonsurae, nec pro litteris dimissoriis aut testimonialibus, nec pro sigillo, nec alia quacumque de causa, etiam sponte oblatum, episcopi et alii ordinum collatores aut eorum ministri quovis praetextu accipiant."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p523","Canon I","For then it decrees that they are altogether bound to give their work freely, altogether nullifying and interdicting contrary taxes, statutes, and customs even immemorial of any places, which can rather be called abuses and favorers of simoniacal depravity. And those who shall have done otherwise, both givers and receivers, beyond divine vengeance, shall incur by the very fact the penalties inflicted by law.","Notarii vero in his tantum locis in quibus non viget laudabilis consuetudo nihil accipiendi, pro singulis litteris dimissoriis aut testimonialibus decimam tantum unius aurei partem accipere possint, dummodo eis nullum salarium sit constitutum pro officio exercendo, nec episcopo ex notarii commodis aliquod emolumentum ex eisdem ordinum collationibus directe vel indirecte provenire possit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p524","Canon I","Since it is not fitting that those who are enrolled in divine ministry should beg with disgrace to their order or practice any sordid gain, and it is known that in many places very many are admitted to sacred orders with almost no discrimination, who by various arts and deceits feign that they obtain an ecclesiastical benefice or also suitable resources, the holy synod establishes that henceforth no secular cleric, however otherwise suitable in morals, knowledge, and age, be promoted to sacred orders unless it be first legitimately established that he peacefully possesses an ecclesiastical benefice which may suffice for his honest livelihood.","Tunc enim gratis operam suam eos praestare omnino teneri decernit, contrarias taxas, ac statuta et consuetudines, etiam immemorabiles, quorumcumque locorum, quae potius abusus et corruptelae simoniacae pravitati faventes nuncupari possunt, penitus cassando et interdicendo. Et qui secus fecerint, tam dantes quam accipientes, ultra divinam ultionem, poenas a iure inflictas ipso facto incurrant."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p525","Canon II","But he cannot resign that benefice unless mention be made that he was promoted to the title of that benefice; nor let that resignation be admitted unless it be established that he can otherwise live conveniently, and a resignation made otherwise shall be void.","Cum non deceat eos qui divino ministerio adscripti sunt cum ordinis dedecore mendicare aut sordidum aliquem quaestum exercere, compertum que sit quamplures plerisque in locis ad sacros ordines nullo fere delectu admitti, qui variis artibus ac fallaciis confingunt se beneficium ecclesiasticum aut etiam idoneas facultates obtinere, statuit sancta synodus ne quis deinceps clericus saecularis, quamvis alias sit idoneus moribus, scientia et aetate, ad sacros ordines promoveatur, nisi prius legitime constet eum beneficium ecclesiasticum, quod sibi ad victum honeste sufficiat, pacifice possidere."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p526","Canon II","Those obtaining patrimony or a pension, however, cannot henceforth be ordained unless those whom the bishop shall have judged to be assumed for the necessity or convenience of his churches, it having also first been considered that that patrimony or pension is truly obtained by them and is such that it suffices for sustaining them in life. And let those things be by no means alienated, extinguished, or remitted without the license of the bishop until they have obtained a sufficient ecclesiastical benefice or have elsewhere the means of living, the penalties of the ancient canons being renewed in these matters.","Id vero beneficium resignare non possit, nisi facta mentione quod ad illius beneficii titulum sit promotus; neque ea resignatio admittatur, nisi constiterit quod aliunde vivere commode possit, et aliter facta resignatio nulla sit."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p527","Canon II","Since benefices have been established for divine worship and for performing ecclesiastical duties, that divine worship be in no part diminished, but due service be given to it in all things, the holy synod establishes that in churches both cathedral and collegiate in which there are no daily distributions, or such small ones that they are verisimilarly neglected, a third part of the fruits and of whatsoever proceeds and obventions, both of dignities and of canonries, personates, portions, and offices, is to be separated and converted into daily distributions, which shall be divided proportionally among the dignities obtaining them and the others attending divine services, according to the division to be made by the bishop, also as a delegate of the Apostolic See, in the very first deduction of fruits, the customs, however, of those churches being preserved in which non-residents or non-serving persons receive nothing or less than a third part, any exemptions and other customs even immemorial, and appeals whatsoever, notwithstanding.","Patrimonium vero vel pensionem obtinentes ordinari posthac non possint, nisi illi quos episcopus iudicaverit assumendos pro necessitate vel commoditate ecclesiarum suarum, eo quoque prius perspecto patrimonium illud vel pensionem vere ab eis obtineri talia que esse quae eis ad vitam sustentandam satis sint. Atque illa deinceps sine licentia episcopi alienari aut exstingui vel remitti nullatenus possint, donec beneficium ecclesiasticum sufficiens sint adepti vel aliunde habeant unde vivere possint, antiquorum canonum poenas super his innovando."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p528","Canon III","Contumacy of those not serving increasing, let it be lawful to proceed against them according to the disposition of the law and of the sacred canons.","Cum beneficia ad divinum cultum atque ecclesiastica munia obeunda sint constituta, ne qua in parte minuatur divinus cultus, sed ei debitum omnibus in rebus obsequium praestetur, statuit sancta synodus in ecclesiis tam cathedralibus quam collegiatis in quibus nullae sunt distributiones quotidianae vel ita tenues ut verisimiliter negligantur, tertiam partem fructuum et quorumcumque proventuum et obventionum, tam dignitatum quam canonicatuum, personatuum, portionum et officiorum, separari debere et in distributiones quotidianas converti, quae inter dignitates obtinentes et ceteros divinis interessentes proportionabiliter, iuxta divisionem ab episcopo, etiam tamquam apostolicae sedis delegato, in ipsa prima fructuum deductione faciendam, dividantur, salvis tamen consuetudinibus earum ecclesiarum in quibus non residentes seu non servientes nihil vel minus tertia parte percipiunt. Non obstantibus exemptionibus et aliis consuetudinibus, etiam immemorabilibus, et appellationibus quibuscumque."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p529","Canon III","Bishops, also as delegates of the Apostolic See, in all parish or baptismal churches in which the people are so numerous that one rector cannot suffice for administering the ecclesiastical sacraments and performing divine worship, shall compel the rectors or others to whom it pertains to join to themselves as many priests for this duty as may suffice for exhibiting the sacraments and celebrating divine worship.","Crescente que non servientium contumacia, liceat contra eos procedere iuxta iuris ac sacrorum canonum dispositionem."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p530","Canon IV","In those, however, in which on account of the distance or difficulty of places the parishioners cannot without great inconvenience approach to receive the sacraments and hear divine offices, they may establish new parishes, even against the will of the pastors, according to the form of the constitution of Alexander III which begins Ad audientiam.","Episcopi etiam tamquam apostolicae sedis delegati in omnibus ecclesiis parochialibus vel baptismalibus in quibus populus ita numerosus sit ut unus rector non possit sufficere ecclesiasticis sacramentis ministrandis et cultui divino peragendo, cogant rectores vel alios ad quos pertinet sibi tot sacerdotes ad hoc munus adiungere quot sufficiant ad sacramenta exhibenda et cultum divinum celebrandum."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p531","Canon IV","Moreover, a competent portion shall be assigned to those priests who are to be placed over churches newly erected, at the discretion of the bishop, from the fruits belonging in any way to the mother church; and if it be necessary, he can compel the people to supply those things which suffice for sustaining the life of the said priests, any general or special reservation or affection concerning the said churches notwithstanding.","In iis vero in quibus ob locorum distantiam sive difficultatem parochiani sine magno incommodo ad percipienda sacramenta et divina officia audienda accedere non possunt, novas parochias, etiam invitis pastoribus, iuxta formam constitutionis Alexandri III quae incipit \"Ad audientiam,\" constituere possint."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p532","Canon IV","Nor can ordinances and erections of this kind be taken away nor impeded by any provisions whatsoever, even by virtue of resignation or by any derogations or suspensions.","Illis autem sacerdotibus qui de novo erunt ecclesiis noviter rectis praeficiendi, competens assignetur portio arbitrio episcopi ex fructibus ad ecclesiam matricem quomodocumque pertinentibus; et si necesse fuerit, compellere possit populum ea subministrare quae sufficiant ad vitam dictorum sacerdotum sustentandam, quacumque reservatione generali vel speciali vel affectione super dictis ecclesiis non obstantibus."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p533","Canon IV","That also the state of churches where sacred offices are ministered to God may be preserved in dignity, bishops, also as delegates of the Apostolic See, according to the form of law, without prejudice however to those obtaining them, may make perpetual unions of any parish and baptismal churches and of other benefices, whether with cure or without cure, with those having cure, on account of their poverty and in other cases permitted by law, even if the said churches or benefices were generally or specially reserved or in any way affected. Which unions also cannot be revoked nor in any way infringed by virtue of any provision, even on account of resignation or by any derogation or suspension.","Neque huiusmodi ordinationes et erectiones possint tolli nec impediri ex quibuscumque provisionibus, etiam vigore resignationis aut quibusvis aliis derogationibus vel suspensionibus."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p534","Canon V","Because illiterate and unskillful rectors of parish churches are less suitable for sacred offices, and others on account of the baseness of their life rather destroy than build up, bishops, also as delegates of the Apostolic See, to the same illiterate and unskillful, if otherwise they be of honest life, may depute coadjutors or vicars for a time, and assign to them a portion of the fruits for their sufficient support, or otherwise provide, any appeal and exemption being removed.","Ut etiam ecclesiarum status ubi sacra deo officia ministrantur ex dignitate conservetur, possint episcopi etiam tamquam apostolicae sedis delegati, iuxta formam iuris, sine tamen praeiudicio obtinentium, facere uniones perpetuas quarumcumque ecclesiarum parochialium et baptismalium et aliorum beneficiorum curatorum vel non curatorum cum curatis, propter earum paupertatem et in ceteris casibus a iure permissis, etiam si dictae ecclesiae vel beneficia essent generaliter vel specialiter reservata aut qualitercumque affecta. Quae uniones etiam non possint revocari nec quoquo modo infringi vigore cuiuscumque provisionis, etiam ex causa resignationis aut derogationis aut suspensionis."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p535","Canon VI","But those who live basely and scandalously, after they have been warned, let them coerce and chastise, and if still incorrigible they persevere in their wickedness, let them have the faculty of depriving them of their benefices according to the institutions of the sacred canons, any exemption and appeal being removed.","Quia illitterati et imperiti parochialium ecclesiarum rectores sacris minus apti sunt officiis, et alii propter eorum vitae turpitudinem potius destruunt quam aedificant, episcopi etiam tamquam apostolicae sedis delegati eisdem illitteratis et imperitis, si alias honestae vitae sint, coadiutores aut vicarios pro tempore deputare, partem que fructuum eisdem pro sufficienti victu assignare, vel aliter providere possint, quacumque appellatione et exemptione remota."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p536","Canon VI","Since that also is greatly to be taken care of, that those things which are dedicated to sacred ministries do not grow old by the injury of time and fall out of the memory of men, bishops, also as delegates of the Apostolic See, may transfer simple benefices, even of the right of patronage, from churches which have collapsed through age or otherwise and cannot be repaired on account of their poverty, the parties concerned having been summoned, to mother or other churches of the same places or of the nearer places, at their own discretion, and in the same churches let them erect altars or chapels under the same invocations, or transfer them to altars or chapels already erected, with all the emoluments and burdens imposed on the prior churches.","Eos vero qui turpiter et scandalose vivunt, postquam praemoniti fuerint, coerceant ac castigent, et si adhuc incorrigibiles in sua nequitia perseverent, eos beneficiis iuxta sacrorum canonum institutiones, exemptione et appellatione quacumque remota, privandi facultatem habeant."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p537","Canon VII","Parish churches, however, even if they be of the right of patronage, so collapsed they shall take care to be repaired and restored from the fruits and proceeds belonging in any way to the same churches; which if they be not sufficient, let them compel all patrons and others who receive any fruits coming from the said churches, or in default of them the parishioners, by all opportune remedies, to the aforesaid, any appeal, exemption, and contradiction being removed.","Cum illud quoque valde curandum sit ne ea quae sacris ministeriis dicata sunt temporum iniuria obsolescant et ex hominum memoria excidant, episcopi etiam tamquam apostolicae sedis delegati transferre possint beneficia simplicia, etiam iuris patronatus, ex ecclesiis quae vetustate vel alias collapsae sint et ob eorum inopiam nequeant instaurari, vocatis iis quorum interest, in matrices aut alias ecclesias locorum eorundem seu viciniorum, arbitrio suo, atque in eisdem ecclesiis erigant altaria vel capellas sub eisdem invocationibus, vel in iam erecta altaria vel capellas transferant, cum omnibus emolumentis et oneribus prioribus ecclesiis impositis."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p538","Canon VII","But if all suffer from excessive poverty, let them be transferred to mother or nearer churches, with the faculty of converting the said parish and other ruined churches to profane but not sordid uses, a cross having been erected there.","Parochiales vero ecclesias, etiam si iuris patronatus sint, ita collapsas refici et instaurari procurent ex fructibus et proventibus quibuscumque ad easdem ecclesias quomodocumque pertinentibus; qui si non fuerint sufficientes, omnes patronos et alios qui fructus aliquos ex dictis ecclesiis provenientes percipiunt, aut in illorum defectum parochianos, omnibus remediis opportunis ad praedicta cogant, quacumque appellatione, exemptione et contradictione remota."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p539","Canon VII","Most illustrious lords, most reverend fathers, does it please you that for the praise of almighty God an end be put to this sacred oecumenical synod, and that confirmation of all and each of the things which have been decreed and defined in it, both under Paul III and Julius III of happy memory and under our most holy lord Pius IV, Roman pontiffs, be sought in the name of this holy synod from the most blessed Roman pontiff through the legates of the Apostolic See and presidents?","Quodsi nimia egestate omnes laborent, ad matrices seu viciniores ecclesias transferantur, cum facultate tam dictas parochiales quam alias ecclesias dirutas in profanos usus non sordidos, erecta tamen ibi cruce, convertendi."],["vol2/trent/part-3/index.html","p540","Decree on the Closing of the Council and on Petitioning for Confirmation","","Illustrissimi domini, reverendissimi que patres, placet ne vobis ut ad laudem dei omnipotentis huic sacrae oecumenicae synodo finis imponatur, et omnium et singulorum quae tam sub felicis recordationis Paulo III et Iulio III quam sub sanctissimo domino nostro Pio IV, Romanis pontificibus, in ea decreta et definita sunt confirmatio nomine sanctae huius synodi per apostolicae sedis legatos et praesidentes a beatissimo Romano pontifice petatur?"]];